  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Professional-Research-Assistant/75058": {
      "title": "Professional Research Assistant",
      "description": "Professional Research Assistant Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Professional Research Assistant Requisition Number: 75058 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Part-Time Posting Close Date: 10-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 24-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Psychology Department at CU Boulder seeks a part-time Professional Research Assistant to manage a vole colony and dynamic animal husbandry duties within our social behavioral neuroscience laboratory. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The Department of Psychology and Neuroscience is composed of Faculty, Staff, Research Assistants, Graduate Students, and Undergraduate Students. Through teaching, learning, research, and community outreach, we seek to promote scholarship at the intersection of psychology and neuroscience. Driving this mission is a deep, shared commitment to improving the human condition for all people. The Department of Psychology and Neuroscience is responsible for over 3,700 students in two undergraduate majors and five PhD programs. Our 52 faculty teach and conduct research on topics that range from the molecular to the community level. Our work is spread across multiple campus locations (MUEN, IBG, WILD, & CINC), with an annual budget of $20 million, which includes approximately $12 million in grant-funded research and $600,000 in gift funds. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Colony management and animal husbandry Social behavioral analysis Record keeping Genotyping Tissue handling Histology Additional opportunities to contribute to research projects and develop skills in contemporary neuroscience techniques (stereotaxic surgeries, molecular biological assays, machine-learning behavioral analysis) will be available. What You Should Know This is a part-time position. This position requires flexibility to work outside of traditional business hours as needed (evenings, weekends, and holidays depending on animal work needed). What We Can Offer The annual salary range for this part-time position is $20,000-$25,000. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be inspired. Be Boulder. What We Require BA/BS in neuroscience or related field, research experience working directly with animal models. What You Will Need Experience maintaining detailed protocols and databases. Ability to troubleshoot independently. Reliability and strong communication skills to work within a team. What We Would Like You to Have Experience in animal behavioral analysis and statistical analysis software is favorable. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume/CV. Cover Letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. Transcripts/Proof of Degree. One Reference Letter (Non-Confidential) Please apply by October 10th, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/CIRES-NOAA-Global-Systems-Laboratory-Satellite-Data-Assimilation-Scientist/75030": {
      "title": "CIRES/NOAA Global Systems Laboratory, Satellite Data Assimilation Scientist",
      "description": "CIRES/NOAA Global Systems Laboratory, Satellite Data Assimilation Scientist Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail CIRES/NOAA Global Systems Laboratory, Satellite Data Assimilation Scientist Requisition Number: 75030 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 08-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 24-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Cooperative Institute for Research in Environmental Sciences (CIRES), a partnership of the University of Colorado Boulder and the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, has a job opening for a Satellite Data Assimilation Scientist at the NOAA Global Systems Laboratory (GSL) located in Boulder Colorado. The Data Assimilation Branch (DAB) within GSL’s Assimilation and Verification Innovation Division (AVID) develops and advances data assimilation capabilities for operational numerical weather prediction systems supporting a wide range of applications, including aviation, severe weather, renewable energy, and general weather forecasting. The Rapid Refresh Forecast System (RRFS) is NOAA’s convection-allowing data assimilation and forecast system designed to provide rapidly updated, high-resolution weather forecasts over North America. In collaboration with other partners, GSL is developing and improving RRFS using the Model for Prediction Across Scales (MPAS) and the Joint Effort for Data Assimilation Integration (JEDI) framework. To support NOAA's Geostationary Extended Observations (GeoXO) program, NOAA/GSL has been evaluating the data impact of hyperspectral infrared radiance data from polar-orbiting satellites and is also expanding to evaluate the data impact from geostationary hyperspectral infrared sounder data (i.e., the recently available Meteosat Third Generation Infrared Sounder (MTG-IRS) data). CIRES/NOAA GSL seeks a Satellite Data Assimilation Scientist to join the Data Assimilation Branch and contribute to the continued development and improvement of RRFS. The scientist will develop, evaluate, and test modifications in data assimilation procedures for satellite and other observations in RRFS, with a particular focus on satellite radiance data (e.g., MTG-IRS data) and satellite retrieval products. The candidate will work collaboratively with scientists at GSL, external partners, and the broader RRFS and UFS modeling communities. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are CIRES is an internationally recognized leader in innovative environmental science and research and is located at the University of Colorado Boulder. At CIRES, more than 900 environmental science professionals work to understand the dynamic Earth system, including people’s relationship with the planet. CIRES has partnered with NOAA since 1967, and our areas of expertise include weather and climate, changes at Earth’s poles, air quality and atmospheric chemistry, water resources, solid Earth sciences, and more. Our vision is to be instrumental in ensuring a sustainable future environment by advancing scientific and societal understanding of the Earth system. The Global Systems Laboratory (GSL) is one of ten NOAA Research laboratories and is located in Boulder, Colorado. GSL researchers develop the-state-of-the-art weather and air quality models, state-of-the-science decision support tools and visualization systems and use high-performance computing technology to support a Weather-Ready Nation. GSL strives to advance its workforce scientifically, technically, and professionally. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Development and testing of assimilation of hyperspectral sounding radiance from Meteosat Third Generation Infrared Sounder (MTG-IRS). (45%) Development and testing of assimilation of satellite retrieval products from Meteosat Third Generation Infrared Sounder (MTG-IRS) and other sounding retrieval data. (45%) Tune and optimize satellite data assimilation within the MPAS-JEDI based RRFS. (10%) A scientist assigned at the Research Scientist II level will take the following additional responsibilities: demonstration of independency and leadership. Contribute to the retrospective and real-time RRFS experiments to evaluate novel weather observations and data assimilation innovations. A scientist assigned at the Research Scientist III level will further take more responsibilities as below: May be asked to supervise employees, lead projects. Substantial and unique contributions to the atmospheric data assimilation, especially satellite data assimilation. What You Should Know This position will be rostered in CIRES at the University of Colorado Boulder but will be physically situated in the David Skaggs Research Center, 325 Broadway, Boulder, CO 80305, on a federal research campus. Selected finalists will be required to pass a federal background check for site access. Foreign nationals’ access to the federal facility will be limited to weekdays, and with the presence of a federal escort. Due to project deliverables, the targeted start date for this position is by March 1, 2027. What We Can Offer CIRES offers a generous compensation package. The annual hiring salary range for this position is $72,000- $114,000. Salary is commensurate with education and experience and determined based on our CIRES internal career track classification. The preferred modality for this position is either in-person or hybrid, but we may accommodate a fully remote work modality at the supervisor’s discretion, Some relocation funds are available to help offset expenses, following CIRES and the University of Colorado’s relocation processes and procedures. CIRES and the University of Colorado Boulder offer a robust training curriculum, opportunities for professional development, and a Mentorship Program. Boulder is a vibrant community with access to mountain parks, dog parks, miles of trails, rivers, lakes, cafes, restaurants, boutiques, theaters, museums, and sports venues. As an employee at CU Boulder, you will have free access to the regional public transit system, an outstanding network of buses, and light rail systems that service Boulder and connect to Denver, the Denver airport, and surrounding communities. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be enlightened. Be unique. Be Boulder. What We Require Ph.D. in meteorology, atmospheric science, or relevant field of study. Minimum of 1 year of experience working on atmospheric data assimilation. What You Will Need Strong programming skills in at least one modern advanced programming language (e.g. Fortran or C++). Demonstrated ability to use a version control system (e.g. Git/GitHub). Demonstrated ability to work in high-performance computing environments. Demonstrated ability to work independently and as part of a team. What We Would Like You to Have Please note that while the position details both required and preferred skills and experience, we invite applicants to apply even if they do not have the preferred skills outlined in this section. If you meet the requirements and have passion for the work, you are encouraged to apply. We encourage on the job training for any additional skills or knowledge that become relevant to the position. Experience with meteorological data assimilation (JEDI, GSI, or DART, etc.). Experience with analyzing satellite observations and their impact in data assimilation systems. Experience with satellite radiance data including data assimilation work on hyperspectral radiance data (e.g., AIRS/CrIS/IASI/MTG-IRS). Expertise with radiative transfer models (e.g., CRTM/RTTOV) used with satellite radiance data. Knowledge of satellite observations, satellite retrievals and the associated error characteristics and averaging kernels. Experience with one or more of the following programming languages: Fortran, C++, Python, Linux Shell scripting languages. Experience with scientific data formats (grib2, NetCDF, BUFR). Excellent written and oral presentation skills. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV. Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. Contact information (name, title, affiliation, email) for 3 references familiar with your professional qualifications for the position. You don’t need to include letters of recommendation with your initial application. If you are identified as a finalist for this role, the search committee will request letters of recommendation at a later time. If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources department using an approved online vendor. If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. Please apply by October 8, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Guoqing Ge Posting Contact Email: guoqing.ge@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Design-and-Construction-Project-Manager/75099": {
      "title": "Design and Construction Project Manager",
      "description": "Design and Construction Project Manager Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Design and Construction Project Manager Requisition Number: 75099 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 11-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary The Project Manager serves as a key representative of the University in the planning and execution of construction and renovation projects across campus. In this role, you will define project scope, handle budgets and schedules, and coordinate consultants, contractors, and internal partners to ensure timely and cost-effective project delivery. The Project Manager ensures compliance with university policies, state regulations, and industry standards while supporting the maintenance, improvement, and functionality of campus facilities. The position leads multiple small to large projects from project development though closeout, all together equaling several million dollars. For larger capital projects, the position operates under the guidance of senior leadership to ensure strategic alignment and oversight, while maintaining autonomy over day-to‑day project delivery. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are The PD&C department consists of three teams (Planning, Engineering, and Design & Construction) responsible for the planning, design, and construction of buildings on campus, in support of CU Boulder's academic mission. The Design & Construction team consists of approximately 40 high performing project delivery professionals leading over 400 projects across campus annually with a combined budget of over $500 million. The Design & Construction team strives to guide campus development in a way that gives physical form to the university's mission, vision, and programs through the effective use of human, environmental, and financial resources. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Project Development Meet with campus clients to review proposed improvements and define project scope. Communicate regularly with clients to ensure satisfaction and alignment with expectations. Coordinate with Facilities Management, building occupants, and auxiliary services (EH&S, OIT, Parking). Consult the department's Project Delivery Guide to ensure compliance with policies and regulations. Collaborate with Team Leads to review scopes of work and update monthly reports. Contractual Agreements Solicit proposals or qualifications and negotiate contract terms. Prepare and amend consultant and contractor contracts as needed. Ensure compliance with State rules and regulations in consultant selection. Monitor contract performance and ensure adherence to terms. Complete contract close-out procedures. Design Administration Lead design meetings with clients and consultants to finalize project documents. Review and circulate design documents for campus feedback. Track design progress and identify variances from project objectives. Implement corrective actions and ensure quality assurance. Communicate project updates to partners. Schedule and Budget Management Develop and maintain master project schedules using data from multiple sources. Monitor progress and adjust schedules to ensure timely project delivery. Prepare budget estimates and lead fiscal aspects of the project. Negotiate costs with consultants, contractors, and suppliers. Maintain and update budget tracking tools, including the Unifier Project Management module. Engage with Team Leads and D&C Leadership to review and report on budgets and schedules. Bidding and Construction Administration Oversee preparation of bidding documents and manage the construction phase. Advertise bids, conduct pre-bid meetings and site visits, and chair bid openings. Evaluate bids and award contracts in compliance with university procedures. Coordinate construction activities with contractors and university team members. Lead weekly construction meetings to review progress and resolve issues. Ensure quality control and communicate disruptions to the university community. Coordinate space activation activities with internal project partners, external project vendors and consultants, and client representatives to ensure a smooth launch transition. Dispute Resolution Mediate disputes and evaluate complex issues from multiple perspectives. Coordinate with supervisors on issues requiring higher-level approval. Review and negotiate change requests from consultants, clients, and contractors. Seek consensus among partners while ensuring compliance with codes and policies. What You Should Know What You Should Know This position is eligible for a hybrid work modality (at least 3 days a week are required to be in office), and has an anticipated work schedule of Monday - Friday during regular business hours. We may make multiple hires from this recruitment. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The salary range for this position is $82,000 - $87,500 annually. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be positive. Be willing. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Bachelor’s degree in one of the following fields: Construction Management, Architecture, Architectural Engineering, Construction Engineering, Civil Engineering, Mechanical Engineering Electrical Engineering, or a closely related field. Relevant experience may substitute for the required degree on a year-for-year basis. 3 years of construction project management experience involving the alteration, modification, or remodeling of institutional or commercial buildings, ranging from small ($2K) to large ($1M) renovation projects. This experience must include at least 1 year of experience in planning and cost estimating. What You Will Need What You Will Need Client‑focused relationship building; ability to serve as a trusted advisor to campus partners. Inclusive, collaborative leadership across cross‑functional teams of consultants, contractors, and internal partners. Clear, concise communication tailored to diverse audiences; strong meeting facilitation skills. Analytical problem‑solving, cost and schedule control, and rigorous attention to quality and safety. Negotiation, consensus building, and proactive risk management. Adaptability and continuous improvement approach. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Proven experience with a variety of project delivery methods, including: Design-Bid-Build, Design-Build, Construction Manager/General Contractor (CM/GC) Proven experience managing building construction and renovation projects, particularly within institutional or higher education environments. Experience delivering projects designed and built to sustainability standards (LEED, energy‑efficient design). Proficiency in Microsoft 365, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams and Project. Working experience with project management information systems (e.g., Unifier, e-Builder, Kahua, Procore, or similar) Familiarity with, or the ability to quickly learn, the State of Colorado’s Office of the Architect and State Buildings Program contractual agreements, or comparable frameworks. Strong oral and written communication skills, with a focus on customer service and team member engagement. Active participation in professional construction or project management organizations (e.g., Lean Construction Institute, Construction Owners Association of America, CMAA), and demonstrating commitment to industry standards and continuous professional development is a plus. Active professional industry certification(s) such as PMP (Project Management Professional), CCM (Certified Construction Manager), DBIA (Design‑Build Institute of America), or Lean Construction credentials (LCI), thereby demonstrating advanced expertise and commitment to industry standards is a plus. Work experience with, or a credential in, a High Performance Certification Program (like LEED, Green Building Initiative, or Green Globes) is a plus. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that highlights how your unique background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position, and tells us in your own words why this opportunity interests you. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 11, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Design-and-Construction-Estimator/75097": {
      "title": "Design and Construction Estimator",
      "description": "Design and Construction Estimator Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Design and Construction Estimator Requisition Number: 75097 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 12-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary This position serves as the department's internal subject matter expert in construction cost estimating, equipping Planners, Engineers, and Project Managers with the tools, knowledge, and confidence to develop well-informed project budgets from the earliest stages of project development. Rather than functioning as a gatekeeper in the project delivery process, the Estimator is a coach and enabler in building institutional cost knowledge, delivering targeted training, and stepping in as a direct contributor on large capital projects where deeper expertise is needed. This position represents a meaningful investment in PD&C's ability to deliver data-backed, reliable cost estimates that support sound planning and project decision-making across our department teams. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are The PD&C department consists of three teams (Planning, Engineering, and Design & Construction) responsible for the planning, design, and construction of buildings on campus, in support of CU Boulder's academic mission. The Design & Construction team consists of approximately 40 high performing project delivery professionals leading over 400 projects across campus annually with a combined budget of over $500 million. The Design & Construction team strives to guide campus development in a way that gives physical form to the university's mission, vision, and programs through the effective use of human, environmental, and financial resources. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Project Cost Database and Estimating Cost Model Develop, implement, and continuously refine a data-backed estimating cost model that empowers Planners and Project Managers to independently prepare well-informed initial project budgets, reducing reliance on the Estimator for routine cost development. Build and maintain a comprehensive project cost database by incorporating historical project cost data, current market pricing, and contractor-submitted cost information, ensuring the model reflects accurate and current conditions. Design the estimating cost model in a format that is accessible and practical for everyday use by Planners and Project Managers, with clear documentation that supports independent application across project types and sizes. Collect and integrate cost data from completed projects and contractor engagements on an ongoing basis, continuously improving the accuracy and relevance of the model over time. Develop detailed calculations from completed capital projects in collaboration with the Campus Controller's Office to assist with Campus capitalization reports. Training Lead department-wide training on estimating fundamentals and cost evaluation to build the team's skills and capacity in developing and reviewing project budgets without requiring the Estimator's direct involvement on every project. Develop and deliver training curriculum for Planners and Project Managers on how to use the department's estimating cost model to prepare initial project budgets, with an emphasis on independent application. Facilitate training sessions for Project Managers on how to evaluate contractor-submitted pricing documents, including Schedules of Values (SOVs), Guaranteed Maximum Price documentation (GMPs), Design-Bid-Build bids, and change orders. Provide targeted coaching to Project Managers on reviewing Contractor's Proposals (COPs) and other pricing deliverables, strengthening the department's first line of review at the project level. Conceptual Estimating Provide expert-level Class 5 and Class 4 estimates to support Planning, Design & Construction in developing reliable cost projections for capital, large deferred maintenance, and controlled maintenance projects prior to contractor or third-party estimator engagement. Prepare Class 5 and Class 4 conceptual estimates for capital projects in support of the Planning team, delivering data-backed and time-based cost projections that allow projects to advance with confidence through early development phases. Develop conceptual estimates for deferred maintenance and controlled maintenance projects in support of the Engineering team, informing prioritization and scoping decisions based on realistic cost expectations. Prepare Life Cycle Cost Analyses (LCCAs) on projects as requested, providing long-range cost perspective to inform decisions on materials, systems, and project scope. Market Trends Build and maintain active relationships with the regional construction market to gather current cost data, assess emerging trends, and keep PD&C informed of conditions that affect project budgets and planning decisions. Cultivate and sustain working relationships with general contractors and subcontractors in the Denver and Boulder metro area, establishing a reliable network for gathering current labor, material, and equipment cost data. Monitor and analyze local and regional construction market trends, incorporating findings into regular updates to PD&C's estimating cost model to keep it current and reflective of actual market conditions. Deliver periodic market cost trend reports to PD&C leadership and project teams, translating market conditions into actionable awareness that informs project planning and budget development. Procurement and Bid Support Support lead Project Managers on capital projects by providing experienced review of contractor-submitted pricing and contributing to award recommendations across multiple project delivery methods. Review contractor pricing submissions for completeness and accuracy on capital projects across CMGC, Design-Bid-Build, and Design-Build delivery methods, identifying gaps, inconsistencies, or risk areas in the pricing. Evaluate contractor bids and proposals relative to project scope, design documents, and current market conditions, providing an independent cost perspective that strengthens the award process. Prepare written documentation supporting award recommendations, offering clear and objective cost analysis that assists lead Project Managers and department leadership in making well-informed decisions. What You Should Know What You Should Know This position is eligible for a hybrid work modality (at least 3 days a week are required to be in office), and has an anticipated work schedule of Monday - Friday during regular business hours. This role is not expected to work University recognized holidays, weekends, or evenings, barring extenuating circumstances. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The salary range for this position is $114,000 - $129,000 annually. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be effective. Be successful. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Bachelor’s degree from an accredited, non-correspondence college or university in one of the following fields: Construction Management, Architecture, Architectural Engineering, Construction Engineering, Civil Engineering, Mechanical Engineering Electrical Engineering, Cost Estimating, or a closely related field. Relevant work experience may be substituted for the required degree on a year-for-year basis. 6 years of experience in construction cost estimating on capital construction projects of $10 million or greater in project budget, including a variety of building types, and at the program, conceptual, schematic, design development and construction document stages of design in elemental and CSI format for all architectural, structural, and civil components of new construction and renovation projects. What You Will Need What You Will Need Client‑focused relationship building; ability to serve as a trusted advisor to campus partners. Inclusive, collaborative leadership across cross‑functional teams of consultants, contractors, and internal partners. Clear, concise communication tailored to diverse audiences; strong meeting facilitation skills. Analytical problem‑solving, cost and schedule control, and attention to quality and safety. Negotiation, consensus building, and proactive risk management. Adaptability and continuous improvement approach. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Experience with Project Estimating Cost Models, solid understanding of AACE classifications, and excellent analytical skills. MEP estimating experience for new construction and renovation projects. AACE International and/or Certified Cost Professional certification. Proficiency with estimating software (Procore, Autodesk, Sage, etc.). Ability to read project construction drawings and specifications. Experience in training colleagues in construction estimating practices and approaches. Experience with a variety of project delivery methods, including: Design-Bid-Build, Design-Build, Construction Manager/General Contractor (CM/GC). Experience estimating building construction and renovation projects, particularly within institutional or higher education environments. Experience estimating and tracking costs of projects designed and built to sustainability standards (LEED, energy‑efficient design). Proficiency in Microsoft 365, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams and Project. Working experience with project management information systems (e.g., Unifier, e-Builder, Kahua, Procore, or similar). Familiarity with, or the ability to quickly learn, the State of Colorado's Office of the Architect and State Buildings Program contractual agreements, or comparable frameworks. Strong oral and written communication skills, with a focus on customer service and team member engagement. Active participation in professional organizations (e.g., AACE International, Lean Construction Institute, Construction Owners Association of America, CMAA) and demonstrating commitment to industry standards and continuous professional development is a plus. Active professional industry certification(s) such as PMP (Project Management Professional), CCM (Certified Construction Manager), DBIA (Design‑Build Institute of America), or Lean Construction credentials (LCI), thereby demonstrating advanced expertise and commitment to industry standards is a plus. Work experience with, or a credential in, a High Performance Certification Program (like LEED, Green Building Initiative, or Green Globes) is a plus. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that highlights how your unique background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position, and tells us in your own words why this opportunity interests you. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 12, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Campus-Bus-Driver-CDL/75096": {
      "title": "Campus Bus Driver-CDL",
      "description": "Campus Bus Driver-CDL Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Campus Bus Driver-CDL Requisition Number: 75096 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 07-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary CU Boulder encourages applications for multiple Bus Driver-CDL positions! These positions fulfill transit services for campus by completing routine routes or contracts for special transportation needs. Once certified to operate specific vehicles, these roles ensure timely transit for campus affiliates and guests in consideration of their needs, vehicle operational capacity and road conditions. This role is eligible for the following benefits: EcoPass; up to 22 vacation days; access to medical, dental, vision, and life insurance, tuition reimbursement; a 10% employer contribution retirement plan, opportunities for growth, and more! Per Federal Motor Carrier Safety Regulations , all drivers must be able to read and speak the English language sufficiently to converse with the general public, to understand highway traffic signs and signals, to respond to official inquiries, and to make entries on reports and records. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are Transportation Services provides comprehensive transit, fleet management, and maintenance to serve the faculty, staff, students, and visitors at the University of Colorado Boulder. The University of Colorado vehicle fleet numbers over 600; a robust transit system moving 1.4 million riders per year at the University of Colorado-Boulder campus; and guidance for driver operations for campuses located in Aurora, Boulder, and Denver. As such, we have the unique opportunity to influence the impressions of every member of our university community and our visitors. We are team-oriented professionals, dedicated to meeting the needs of our community and providing the highest possible customer service. Experience dynamic growth, self-directed efforts, in a rewarding industry. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Vehicle Operations Safely transports people using university vehicles, the incumbent meets certification to operate. The incumbent shall navigate a variety of routine and contracted routes on a time schedule with passenger safety, road and traffic concerns, as well as vehicle operational capacity under consideration. Reports Completes pre- and post-trip inspections results on the appropriate forms, ensuring all forms are filled out properly for CDL paperwork. This includes, but is not limited to, recording passenger count, mileage, fuel, and other fluid usages and completion of a statistical summary by route. Vehicle Inspections Conducts pre- and post-trip safety inspections to confirm vehicles suitability for route completion. Incumbent may add fluids and perform other maintenance tasks if needed. However, should a vehicle fail during a pre-trip inspection or during a route, the driver will evaluate or operate a suitable alternative as well as notify dispatch and appropriate management. Other Duties Unspecified duties directed by supervisors to maintain the operational readiness of the department. Complete continuing education to establish or maintain proficiency in vehicle operation capacities. Notify supervisor of safety incidents, near misses and all potential hazards. Maintains a safe work practices, participates in department safety meetings and completes all assigned trainings. What You Should Know What You Should Know The Buff Bus service runs every day of the week for up to 22 hours a day. This position may be required to work alternative shifts to accommodate our commitment to serving campus. Hours are guaranteed year-round with no split shifts. This position is eligible for our Facilities Management Employee Referral Program . What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The pay rate for this role is $25.00/hour, and there is shift differential pay for evening and weekend shifts. One-time $2,000 sign-on bonus paid out after 6 months of employment. We offer full benefits—check out our total compensation calculator to forecast your amount (this is a university staff position). Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be empowered. Be successful. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Must posses a valid CDL Class A or B license without air brake restriction. CDL with passenger endorsement and the ability to pass DOT drug and alcohol tests. Two years of experience operating equipment appropriate to the work assignment, reduced to 6-months of experience operating a transit bus such as HOP, RTD (including its fixed route contractors), or a school bus. Must satisfactorily complete a pre-placement physical and a medical evaluation for the operation of a CDL. What You Will Need What You Will Need Strong oral and written communication skills. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Prior experience operating transit buses. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. Optional : A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 7, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Essential Services Essential Services This position is designated “essential services.” The incumbent is required to respond to requests for work during campus emergencies (snow, wind, rain, flood and any other natural or man-made event) and report to work for his / her regularly assigned shift during emergency campus closures. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Equipment-Operator-II/75087": {
      "title": "Equipment Operator II",
      "description": "Equipment Operator II Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Equipment Operator II Requisition Number: 75087 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 30-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary The Campus Dining & Hospitality team at the University of Colorado, Boulder is seeking applications for an Equipment Operator! This position provides support to campus dining operations by insuring proper delivery of food, beverage, furniture, equipment and fixtures. This position acts as support to Food Store operations. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are The Division of Student Life consists of 36 departments that provide student support and services dedicated to helping students become successful, curious, competent individuals during their time at CU Boulder. At CU Boulder, dining is more than just a meal—it’s an experience. Our award-winning dining venues serve fresh, sustainable, and innovative cuisine crafted by a passionate team dedicated to your well-being. Every year, we proudly serve over 3 million meals, fueling healthy bodies and minds with options that are as delicious as they are nutritious. From community-building events to hands-on food education, we’re here to help Buffs build lifelong habits for a healthier future. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Loading, Unloading and Warehouse Review unit or department orders for accuracy and scheduling of deliveries. Load goods, materials, and equipment onto trucks and vehicles, secure items in vehicles and follow all safe driving rules and standards. Deliver goods or materials to both on and off campus event spaces and units as scheduled and dependent on business need. Unload truck or vehicles at event spaces and units and assure that authorized personnel receive and sign for the delivery. Ensure that all delivered goods, materials, or equipment are handled safely and properly to ensure quality and safety protocols. Ensure items are taken to designated storage or event areas. Maintain a daily travel log summary to be provided to supervisor. Deliver special function goods such as equipment, tents, cabinets, grills, water stations and/or other items as needed. Clean and maintain storage and warehouse areas. Equipment Care and Maintenance Maintain proper fluid levels, tire pressure in the truck. Monitor fleet vehicles, lift gates, dock equipment, pallet jacks, refrigerated storage and other equipment as needed daily for safe operation. Report to supervisor if repairs are needed. Ensure all fleet vehicles are clean and orderly. Keep all loading dock and parking lot clean and orderly. Other Duties as Assigned Assist other department operations as necessary and based upon business need. What You Should Know What You Should Know This position operates University vehicles and requires an active driver's license in good standing. Must be able to obtain and maintain commercial motor vehicle (CMV) license and successful completion of University of Colorado's Safety Training Program within 90 days of deployment to operate vehicles essential to the position. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The annual salary for this position is $46,128. Our outstanding benefits include: Paid days off: 22 vacation days, 15 sick leave days and 11 holidays per year. Access to medical, vision, dental and life insurance. Tuition reimbursement. A 10% employer contribution retirement plan. EcoPass for free RTD rides. Opportunity for career growth within the division of student life and the CU Boulder campus! Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be active. Be resourceful. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Two years of experience which includes warehousing, handling, shipping, receiving, storing, inventory, and/or distribution of goods. Equivalent combination of education and experience may substitute on a year for-year basis. Must obtain food safety certification appropriate for the position level within 6 months of hire and maintain certification for the duration of employment with CDH. What You Will Need What You Will Need Ability to work in fast paced environment. Excellent customer service skills. Strong attention to detail and safety. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Food Safety Certification. A thorough understanding of the flow of food from purchasing to ready-to-prepare state. Knows and can recite proper receiving temperatures as well as appropriate storage areas for goods and products. Can prioritize delivery activities to ensure that proper product temperatures are maintained. Previous Equipment Operator 1 experience preferred. Experience with C-Bord/FSS doing Inventory, Transfer Requisition and helping Dining and Retail units with out of stocks through Sysco. CMV certified. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by September 30, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Essential Services Essential Services This position is designated “essential services.” The incumbent is required to respond to requests for work during campus emergencies (snow, wind, rain, flood and any other natural or man-made event) and report to work for his / her regularly assigned shift during emergency campus closures. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Temporary-Medical-Assistant/75085": {
      "title": "Temporary Medical Assistant",
      "description": "Temporary Medical Assistant Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Temporary Medical Assistant Requisition Number: 75085 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Temporary Staff Schedule: On-Call Posting Close Date: 04-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Medical Services at the University of Colorado, Boulder are seeking applications for a Temporary Medical Assistant! This position provides comprehensive health care, education and customer service to include primary care, gynecological and transgender services primarily to the student population. This may also include care of CU Boulder faculty, staff and their dependents and on urgent occasions, treatment to injured or ill University visitors. Position provides team-based support to providers, improves efficiency and productivity to Medical Services. This position assists the provider(Physician/APN) to aid in clinic flow, productivity and continuity of care to patients and CU Boulder community. This is a temporary position that is anticipated to last through May 1, 2027. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The Division of Student Life consists of 36 departments that provide student support and services dedicated to helping students become successful, curious, competent individuals during their time at CU Boulder. Medical Services staff understand and are committed to the unique health concerns of our student population, making us an unparalleled source of advocacy for student wellness! Medical Services provides a wide range of health care services for students for primary care, physicals, sexually transmitted infection testing and treatment, allergy shots, vaccinations, travel health care, and concussion care. Our skilled team of providers collaborate to support the overall health of our students with our team of physicians/nurses, physical therapists, registered dietitians, chiropractors, acupuncturists, and massage therapists. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Provider Support/Patient Care/Customer Service Interview patients to identify primary complaints, collect appropriate vital signs, and record relevant medical history in the Electronic Medical Record (EMR) during the patient intake process. This includes gathering historical data on previous allergies, updating the patient's medication list, documenting significant medical history, family medical history, and identifying learning barriers. Responsible for charging and charting all procedures, treatments, and medications administered, implementing the provider's treatment plan for patients of all ages (primarily late adolescents and early adulthood population). Assist in direct patient care procedures, administer prescribed medications and immunizations, and support nurses and providers during procedures based on competencies in Medical Services. Familiar with the location of the crash cart and is available to assist RN and Provider staff in a code situation as delegated by an RN or provider. Responsible for daily and as-needed stocking of exam rooms, including infection prevention and cleaning between patients. Point of Care The MA performs Point of Care (POC) tests on patients regularly, ensuring compliance with CLIA regulations. Competency tests are conducted annually to maintain compliance. All POC tests are documented in the EMR for accurate charting and charging. EMR Documentation The MA is responsible for documenting and charging in the EMR for all procedures, POC tests, treatments, and medications administered. Charting and charging are completed at the time of care. To meet AAAHC standards, Medical Assistant I conducts random audits of Medical Assistant charts twice a year. What You Should Know This is a temporary, 1% position that is anticipated to work 24 to 40 hours per week. This position is anticipated to last through May 1, 2027. What We Can Offer The pay range for this position is $20.00 - $25.75 per hour. Benefits Temporary positions at the University of Colorado are not benefits-eligible, however, all positions are eligible for paid sick leave . Be Statements Be adaptable. Be proactive. Be Boulder. What We Require Completion of a Medical Assistant Program from an accredited institution with certification. Current Basic Life Support (BLS) certification required. Active Medical Assistant registration or certification such as: Registered Medical Assistant (RMA) and proof of current membership from American Medical Technologists (AMT), Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) and proof of current membership from American Association of Medical Assistants (AAMA) Certified Clinical Medical Assistant (CCMA) and proof of current membership from National Health career Association (NHA) National Certified Medical Assistant (NCMA) and proof of current membership from National Certified Competency Testing (NCCT) What You Will Need The position requires basic functional computer skills (experience with Windows based systems). What We Would Like You to Have One (1) year of Medical Assisting experience preferred. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 4, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/HR-Service-Center-Coordinator-Staff-Approval/75083": {
      "title": "HR Service Center Coordinator",
      "description": "HR Service Center Coordinator - Staff Approval Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail HR Service Center Coordinator - Staff Approval Requisition Number: 75083 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 04-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Human Resources Department at CU Boulder invites applications for an HR Service Center Coordinator on the Staff Approvals team! This position plays a critical role in maintaining the integrity of personnel, position management, payroll, and HCM security processes while ensuring compliance, data accuracy, and operational efficiency across the Boulder campus. The Service Center HR Coordinator serves as a significant resource and advisor to assigned departments on university and staff related personnel and payroll processes. The Service Center HR Coordinator recommends and advises on necessary methods to efficiently expedite all personnel and payroll related issues. This position serves as a significant resource for interpretation and implementation of school, department and university policies and procedures regarding university and classified staff. Additionally, the HR Coordinator will collaborate with the centers of excellence to provide a wide ranging repertoire of HR services to campus constituents. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Human Resources (HR) contributes leadership, guidance, tools and services to support employees throughout their careers at CU Boulder and to cultivate a culture which upholds the campus’ strongly held values. HR partners with campus in the achievement of the campus vision and strategic imperatives. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be HCM Personnel Entry, Position Processing, HCM Access: The HRSC Staff Coordinator supports Boulder campus departments by managing Staff personnel transactions, position processing, payroll-related actions, and HCM security access processes within the Human Capital Management (HCM) system. This position ensures the accurate and timely processing of personnel actions while providing guidance on HR policies, procedures, and payroll matters in compliance with university policies, Colorado State Personnel Rules, and federal regulations. Key Responsibilities: Process, review, and approve staff personnel transactions in HCM, including hires, transfers, promotions, career track changes, compensation changes, and additional pay. Manage position-related actions within HCM, ensuring positions are created, updated, and maintained accurately. Provide guidance to departments on staff personnel actions, position management, and payroll-related processes. Maintain employment records and supporting documentation to maintain compliance with audit and recordkeeping requirements. Conduct Length of Service (LOS) and Retention Rights reviews for Classified Staff employees in accordance with Colorado State Personnel Rules. Serve as the Boulder Campus HCM Access Coordinator. Assist requesters in identifying appropriate HCM security roles and required training based on job responsibilities and business needs. Review and evaluate HCM access requests to ensure requested roles align with security standards, business requirements, and compliance expectations. Partner with departments, campus partners, and system users to resolve personnel, payroll, and access-related issues while delivering excellent customer service. Campus Resource for HR Initiatives, Changes, and System Enhancements: The HRSC Staff Coordinator serves as a campus resource for Human Resources initiatives, process improvements, policy changes, and HCM system enhancements. This position partners with departmental liaisons and leadership to resolve complex issues, improve operational efficiency, and support the effective use of Human Capital Management (HCM) systems and processes. Key Responsibilities: Serve as a main resource for campus departments regarding HR initiatives, process changes, system upgrades, and HCM-related inquiries. Partner with department liaisons and leadership to resolve complex personnel and transactional issues. Find opportunities to improve efficiency and streamline HR processes by evaluating alternative solutions and recommending best practices. Provide guidance and training on HR procedures, personnel transactions, and effective use of HCM and related systems. Investigate and resolve complex transactional issues using knowledge of personnel administration, recruitment, compensation, benefits, employee relations, and HR information systems. Communicate and support the implementation of new HR initiatives, business processes, and system enhancements. Promote consistent application of University policies, Colorado State Personnel Rules, and established HR business practices. Serve as a campus subject matter resource for HCM functionality, troubleshooting, and process navigation. HRSC Outreach and Customer Service: The HRSC Staff Coordinator establishes and maintains strong working relationships with assigned departments by providing responsive, high-quality customer service and serving as a trusted HR resource. This position partners with campus partners to understand departmental needs, promote effective HR practices, and support the delivery of consistent and efficient HR services. Key Responsibilities: Build and maintain constructive, collaborative relationships with assigned departments and campus partners. Serve as a main point of contact for HR-related questions, providing timely guidance, support, and problem resolution. Assess customer needs and deliver services that meet quality standards and support departmental goals. Monitor and evaluate customer satisfaction, finding opportunities to improve service delivery and customer experience. Partner with departments to address concerns, provide recommendations, and support effective HR operations. Represent the Human Resources Service Center as a member of the broader HR team. What You Should Know This is a hybrid position that works primarily in a remote environment. What We Can Offer The annual starting salary for this full-time position is $48,000. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be adaptable. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require Bachelor’s Degree. HR or administrative experience may be substituted for the degree on a year for year basis. What You Will Need Attention to detail and work with accuracy. Ability to multitask and work under pressure while remaining calm, friendly and professional. Strong oral and writing skills. Ability to meet all deadline. Ability to resolved and handle particular challenging or complex situations. What We Would Like You to Have University HR experience. Knowledge of HCM Peoplesoft system. Knowledge of regent policy and classified state system. One year of related experience. Experience working within a state system. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 4th, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Associate-Director-of-Staff-Experience/75082": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Staff Experience",
      "description": "Associate Director of Staff Experience Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Associate Director of Staff Experience Requisition Number: 75082 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 11-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Residence Life encourages applications for an Associate Director of Staff Experience ! This role is a full-time, twelve-month, live-out, exempt professional position and a senior leader within Residence Life. This position provides strategic leadership for the department's staff experience, ensuring that every stage of the employee lifecycle, from recruitment through offboarding, is intentional, engaging, and aligned with the department's mission and values. The Associate Director leads the department's staff experience strategy, including professional and student staff recruitment and selection, onboarding, training, professional development, recognition, and offboarding. This role is responsible for continually improving these programs to create efficient, welcoming, and high-impact experiences that attract, develop, and retain exceptional staff. The position supervises an Assistant Director and Coordinator who support this work. Success in this role requires exceptional collaboration. The Associate Director partners closely with colleagues across Residence Life, University Housing, Student Life, Human Resources, and Finance to coordinate complex processes, implement new initiatives, and ensure compliance with university policies and practices. The position also provides leadership to multiple departmental committees and cross-functional workgroups responsible for advancing strategic priorities related to staff development and organizational effectiveness. The successful candidate will bring a strong sense of accountability and follow-through, including the ability to be present and engaged during critical periods for the department. They will be a strategic and collaborative leader who can establish vision and direction while remaining connected to the day-to-day work of the department. They will be comfortable moving between long-term planning and hands-on execution. As a member of the Residence Life Leadership Team, the Associate Director helps guide the daily operations and long-term direction of a comprehensive residential program serving more than 10,000 residents across 24 residence halls and apartment communities. The department includes more than 47 professional staff and over 500 student employees working within Residence Life's areas of engagement, support, and operations. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The Division of Student Life consists of 36 departments that provide student support and services dedicated to helping students become successful, curious, competent individuals during their time at CU Boulder. Residence Life Residence Life provides residential communities that support student success and enrich the learning experience outside the classroom. By supporting students, engaging them in the community, and effectively managing the residential environment, we create spaces where students connect with others, explore new perspectives, and grow throughout their time at CU. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Organizational Effectiveness Serve as a member of the Residence Life Leadership Team, contributing to departmental vision, strategic planning, organizational priorities, and operational decision-making. Provide leadership for departmental committees, task forces, and cross-functional workgroups to advance critical initiatives and organizational effectiveness. Facilitate leadership meetings, retreats, and department-wide meetings that promote communication, collaboration, and shared accountability. Lead norming conversations that promote consistency in supervisory practices, staff expectations, and decision-making across Residence Life. Guide the development and continuous improvement of departmental systems, structures, and standard operating procedures that support organizational effectiveness. Foster a culture of continuous improvement, collaboration, innovation, and employee engagement throughout the department. Recruitment and Onboarding Administration Provide strategic leadership for all professional and student staff recruitment, selection, onboarding, transition, and offboarding efforts within Residence Life. Design and continuously improve recruitment and selection processes that attract exceptional candidates while ensuring equitable, timely, and welcoming candidate experiences. Lead the development of recruitment strategies that strengthen applicant pools and advance the department's dedication to inclusive excellence. Oversee comprehensive onboarding experiences that prepare new employees for success while fostering early connection, belonging, and engagement. Develop systems that effectively support both annual and off-cycle onboarding of professional and student employees. Coordinate Residence Life’s intern recruitment, selection, and development processes. Ensure compliance with all federal, state, university, and departmental employment policies and hiring procedures. Serve as the primary departmental liaison to Human Resources on staffing initiatives, policy implementation, and employment practices. Assess staffing models, recruitment outcomes, and onboarding practices to find opportunities for continuous improvement. Staff Learning & Professional Development Provide strategic leadership for the department's professional staff learning and development philosophy. Design and implement comprehensive learning opportunities that support employees throughout their career lifecycle, from onboarding through advanced leadership development. Develop intentional multi-year professional development pathways that prepare staff for increased responsibility and career advancement. Expand the use of engaging online and blended learning experiences that increase accessibility, consistency, and learning effectiveness. Oversee required compliance and institutional trainings while ensuring meaningful integration into broader professional development efforts. Coordinate departmental participation in conferences and other professional development opportunities. Evaluate the effectiveness of training initiatives and implement improvements based on assessment data and participant feedback. Develop annual budget recommendations that support departmental training, professional development, conferences, and staff development initiatives. Allocate and manage resources to improve the impact of professional development investments. Personnel Administration & Compliance Supervise, coach, develop, and evaluate an Assistant Director and Coordinator while fostering a culture of accountability, professional growth, and continuous learning. Provide oversight for employment-related administrative processes, including hiring and appointment actions, position management, onboarding documentation, and employee transitions, ensuring processes are completed accurately and in a timely manner. Ensure compliance with university policies, employment regulations, and departmental standards. Oversee departmental recognition programs that celebrate employee contributions and reinforce organizational values. Monitor employment trends, staffing metrics, and workforce needs to inform planning and decision-making. Develop and maintain documentation, procedures, and resources that support consistent personnel practices across the department. Department Leadership & Crisis Response Serve as a senior departmental leader supporting major operational initiatives including opening, closing, occupancy transitions, and other departmental priorities. Participate in the departmental leadership on-call rotation, providing leadership during emergencies and complex situations. Support the implementation of departmental emergency response, business continuity, and crisis management protocols. Serve as acting departmental leader in the Director's absence, as appropriate. Promote a culture of care, safety, accountability, and student-centered decision-making throughout the department. Perform other duties as assigned to support the effective operation of Residence Life. Serve as the department's strategic lead for the Residence Life staff experience by using assessment, employee feedback, and organizational data to continuously improve recruitment, onboarding, learning, recognition, engagement, and retention initiatives. Partnerships & University Collaboration Build and maintain collaborative relationships with Human Resources, University Housing, Finance, Student Life Employee Experience, Career Services, and other campus partners. Represent Residence Life on university committees, workgroups, and collaborative initiatives related to employee experience and organizational development. Partner with campus collaborators to align staffing practices, professional development, and employee support initiatives. Develop and sustain partnerships that enhance recruitment, retention, and employee engagement across Residence Life. Collaborate with campus partners to implement new initiatives, policies, and organizational priorities. What You Should Know This position has the ability to work in a hybrid modality, with at least four days per week in the office. The anticipated work schedule is Monday through Friday during regular business hours, with occasional evening and weekend hours. The position is also expected to participate in on-call coverage and respond as needed, including on days when the employee is scheduled to work remotely. What We Can Offer The annual salary for this position is $88,000. Onboarding assistance may be available per Student Life guidelines. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be adaptable. Be game-changing. Be Boulder. What We Require Master’s degree in Student Affairs and Higher Education, Human Resources, Organizational Development or related field. Equivalent combination of education and experience may substitute on a year-for-year basis. 3+ years of experience working in Residence Life or Housing. 5+ years of experience that demonstrates progressively responsible organizational authority in higher education. 2+ years of experience supervising full-time professionals. Experience leading employee recruitment, onboarding, training, and professional development initiatives. What You Will Need Demonstrated dedication to Residence Life's values of Safety, Resident- focused care, Responsibility, Inclusive Communities, and Development. Proven ability to build collaborative partnerships across complex organizations. Demonstrated dedication to inclusive hiring, equitable employee development, and belonging. Excellent project management and organizational leadership skills. Strong facilitation and presentation skills. Ability to analyze data and use assessment to improve programs and processes. Ability to balance strategic planning with operational execution. What We Would Like You to Have Experience working with other student affairs departments or committees; including developing partnerships for joint initiatives. Proven ability to lead organizational change, facilitate collaboration, resolve conflict, and build high-performing teams. Experience with strategic planning and program development. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 11, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Essential Services This position is designated “essential services.” The incumbent is required to respond to requests for work during campus emergencies (snow, wind, rain, flood and any other natural or man-made event) and report to work for his / her regularly assigned shift during emergency campus closures. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/UMC-Director/75077": {
      "title": "UMC Director",
      "description": "UMC Director Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail UMC Director Requisition Number: 75077 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 06-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The University Memorial Center (UMC) at the University of Colorado, Boulder is seeking applications for a UMC Director! This position is in charge of the administration and operation of the UMC and its associated programs. This individual oversees and leads the planning, development, implementation, and assessment of facilities, programs and services that support the educational, cultural, social, and recreational needs of students and the broader CU Boulder community. The Director is responsible for setting a high standard in the areas of policy implementation; delivery of quality services and revenue generation; advocacy for diversity, sustainability, and assessment; facility maintenance and renovation; and student development through student employment and advising the UMC Board, a joint board of CU Student Government (CUSG). The Director works collaboratively with other Student Life offices and reports administratively to the Division of Student Life. The UMC Director supervises the following areas of the student union: administration; building management, operations, reception desk, and security; building and campus non-academic scheduling and related sales (CU Events Planning & Catering); event audio visual (AV) and set-up; student safe ride program (CU NightRide); games area (The Connection); assessment; student art program; contract negotiations for campus and private leases and Memoranda of Understanding (MOUs) in the union; Veterans Memorial Lounge; critical incident planning; and Public Information Officer (PIO). In addition, the Director supervises the functional areas of Campus Alcohol Management and the central campus Event Management System (EMS). The position also supervises the provision of budget and financial support services for the UMC. The individual leads the union’s short- and long-term facility and strategic foresight by working collaboratively with UMC staff, the UMC Board, and campus administration and partners. The UMC Director is seen as a leader in the Division of Student Life and on campus given the role and visibility of the student union for the campus and larger community. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The Division of Student Life consists of 36 departments that provide student support and services dedicated to helping students become successful, curious, competent individuals during their time at CU Boulder. With more than 1.6 million visitors each year, the University Memorial Center (UMC) is a dynamic hub where community and engagement come to life. Known as the “heart of campus,” the UMC buzzes with activity, welcoming thousands daily to dine, study, shop, bowl or play billiards at The Connection, or immerse in gaming at the Alienware Buffalo Esports and Gaming Lounge. We host numerous live events, meetings, and conferences for the CU Boulder campus and wider community in our various spaces, including the Glenn Miller Ballroom. As a workplace, the UMC offers a fast-paced, people-centered environment where every interaction contributes to campus life. Home to a wide range of student services and an active CU student government, we foster leadership, collaboration, and innovation among our staff and student employees. We celebrate diversity through food, art, music, and open exchange of ideas, cultivating belonging while supporting the campus spaces where expression and activism thrive. At the UMC, we are committed to student success and will work together to build human capacity focused on a culture of care for all. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Leadership Performs administrative and supervisory functions related to the coordination of all UMC facilities, programs, and services which constitute an approximate $10 million operation. These responsibilities include hiring, training, and terminating professional UMC employees (state classified and university exempt and non-exempt) and the general supervision of approximately 55 full-time staff and 125 student employees. Additionally, the position is well-versed in student and leadership development; games area operations; revenue generation; facility operations, maintenance, repair and replacement (R&R), and scheduling; budgeting, information technology (IT); capital construction; strategic foresight; and assessment. Facility and Operations Develops plan for facility and operational improvements, including major repairs and construction. Leads work with campus entities for the future renovation and expansion of the UMC. Ensures staff serve as proactive liaisons to Housing Facility Services, Campus Dining and Hospitality, and the CU Book Store, ensuring clear communication and strong collaboration in support of the UMC’s facility services, dining, and bookstore operations. Campus Collaboration Works with various University departments including Finance, Procurement Services, Staff Council, Human Resources, Facilities Management, Risk Management, Strategic Relations, Police Department, Vice Chancellor's and Chancellor's offices, and Student Life departments. Serves as a colleague to other directors in the Division of Student Life. Works with and may chair committees on Division and University initiatives, policies, and student engagement and retention. Supports the Division's and University's strategic plan and goals. Student Engagement Works with various student-run boards impacting the success of the UMC. Attends regularly scheduled meetings of the UMC Board, CUSG Finance Board, and CUSG Legislative Council; various student organization functions; and other meetings that pertain to the operation of the UMC and Division of Student Life. Engages in dialogue with students and evaluates decisions through a student development lens. Positively promotes the image of the UMC and university. This position will serve on the Student Engagement Response Team (SERT) committee in response to student activism and civic engagement. Fiduciary Prepares and presents the annual operating budget and authorizes expenditures of UMC funds and other funds under their supervision. These funds include student fees and self-generated revenues. Maintains proper internal fiscal controls. Additional Responsibilities Works with various individuals in the effort to explain University and UMC policy. Serves as a liaison to the USS Colorado Alumni Association and maintains the UMC as the state's veterans memorial. Continues to develop the UMC as the \"heart of campus\" and a key campus leader for event planning. Maintains active assessment and marketing efforts by working with Division leadership in these areas. Co-leads staff to support free expression for students (SERT). Engages in critical incident planning and drills with staff. Other assigned duties which, by nature of this position, can be varied. What You Should Know Monday-Friday 8:00am-5:00pm. Hybrid days and time to be decided once hired. What We Can Offer The annual salary range for this position is $129,825 - $144,250. Onboarding assistance may be available per Student Life guidelines. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be challenged. Be dynamic. Be Boulder. What We Require Master's degree in higher education, student affairs, business management, or a related field. Relevant experience may substitute for education on a year for year basis. 5 years of experience managing a complex organization and with increasing responsibility for supervision, business operations, facility operations, and auxiliary services. 3 years of experience overseeing building maintenance, facilities operations, construction, or renovation projects in amounts of $500K+ What You Will Need Organizational Leadership - The ability to develop and communicate a vision so that others commit and act to fulfill the mission and goals of the organization. Financial Acumen - Maintains and applies a broad understanding of financial management principles to ensure decisions are fiscally sound and responsible. Critical and Analytical Thinking - Ability to objectively assess situations and make impartial, well-reasoned decisions. Written and Oral Communication - Ability to communicate in a clear, concise, organized, and convincing manner in both written and oral formats. Critical Thinking - Formulates objectives and priorities, and implements plans consistent with the long-term interest of the organization, capitalizes on opportunities and manages risks. Team Building - Inspires and fosters team commitment, spirit, pride, and trust. Facilitates cooperation and motivates team members to accomplish group goals. Partnering - Develops networks and builds alliances; collaborates across boundaries to build strategic relationships and achieve common goals. Dedication to social justice, diversity, inclusivity and equity in the student union and through its mission to serve the CU Boulder community. Knowledge of student activities and programming in a higher education setting and that intentionally supports student development, success, and retention. Knowledge of principles and practices of contract negotiations and business management. Knowledge of physical plant systems and building maintenance programs. What We Would Like You to Have Demonstrated knowledge and experience to help support student development in a higher education setting. Demonstrated experience supervising large capital projects in amounts of $500k, renovations, and general construction for a facility that remains open to business operations and the public. Experience supervising an event and scheduling department. Experience in a leadership role for a facility with an alcohol license. Demonstrated ability to work with a complex student government system in an efficient and effective manner. Demonstrated ability to collaborate and work with student organizations, a student governing board, and use and implement student development theories and practices. Demonstrated ability to select and supervise professional staff. Demonstrated ability to perform a variety of tasks in a dynamic environment, including creating and implementing a strategic plan that optimizes the facility's use for students and the CU Boulder community. Demonstrated ability to stay abreast of the changing role of the student union and student life and development in higher education. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain working relationships with a diverse student body, staff, faculty, and general public. Demonstrated ability to work in a highly visible position, respond to media inquiries, and navigate university and community requests. Experience in designing, implementing, and evaluating student. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: 1. A current resume. 2. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 6, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Marketing-and-Communications-Specialist/75076": {
      "title": "Marketing and Communications Specialist",
      "description": "Marketing and Communications Specialist Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Marketing and Communications Specialist Requisition Number: 75076 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 04-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The University of Colorado Law School welcomes applications for a Marketing and Communications Specialist! This role is responsible for supporting internal and external communications of the Law School to sustain and enhance the school’s reputation for world-class education, scholarship and service. This position assists in the execution of a comprehensive marketing communications strategy that regionally and nationally promotes Colorado Law's faculty, staff, students, centers, and programs. This position ensures all marketing and communications activities of the law school are high-quality, consistent, and aligned with central messaging priorities and branding standards. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are At the Colorado Law School , we are an engaged, diverse, and welcoming community of students, staff, faculty, and alumni who help one another succeed. Our selective admissions process keeps our student body small, enabling our faculty, staff, alumni, and community to invest deeply in each student’s success. Because we take our responsibility to educate and train future lawyers very seriously, our curriculum, research centers, and experiential learning opportunities are crafted to prepare students for success in today’s changing legal environment. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Content Creation: Write, edit, distribute and track content for various audiences, including news stories, feature articles, profiles, website content, emails, e-newsletters (such as the monthly alumni newsletter Law Points), annual reports, talking points and class notes. Assist with writing, editing, planning and logistics for the annual Amicus alumni magazine. Update the Colorado Law website, including the creation of new webpages and editing existing content. Provide communications support for major events such as lectures, conferences, alumni programs, commencement, orientation and signature law school events. Assist Director of Marketing and Communications in the development and coordination of print communications projects including brochures, event flyers, the admissions viewbook, admitted students' collateral, event postcards and programs, and thought-leader collateral. Find opportunities to tell compelling stories in creative ways across channels and audiences that exemplify Colorado Law’s mission and strategic direction. Ensure all content created conforms to university brand guidelines and publishing policies, editorial style, organization, and appropriateness. Coordinate with Marketing and Communications Director and Strategic Relations and Communications to submit news items to appropriate outlets and pitch story ideas for submission to news outlets. Design, Photography, and Digital Communications: Create graphics for social media posts, event flyers, email newsletters, and web content, ensuring digital content is consistent and aligned with university brand guidelines. Manage the design and deployment of digital posters on law school display systems. Assist with managing the law school’s online calendar. Photograph events in the law school community and coordinate with freelance photographers for coverage of higher-profile events. Identify subjects and locations, coordinate shot lists and organize photo assets. Connect with Strategic Relations and Communications and campus communicators as necessary, attending communications meetings and trainings. Assist the director on additional projects as needed. Supervise and guide a student employee. Social Media: Monitor social media strategies to create engaging content for all social media platforms. Develop and implement a content calendar to schedule and track posts. Be the primary manager of social media channels to provide accurate and timely outreach and information to prospective students, alumni, donors, and other friends and external audiences. Track metrics to increase social media reach and engagement. Assist with video ideation, storyboarding, and interviewing, as needed. What You Should Know This role has an anticipated work schedule of Monday – Friday during regular business hours. Some evening and weekend hours may be required. This position has the ability to work in a hybrid work modality, with 3 days a week required to be in person during the academic year, and 2 days per week required to be in person during the summer. What We Can Offer The salary range for this position is $58,200 – $67,500 annually, depending on qualifications. Relocation assistance is available within department guidelines. Access our Total Compensation Calculator to see what your total rewards could be for this University Staff position. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be dynamic. Be supportive. Be Boulder. What We Require Bachelor’s degree in Communications, Marketing, Public Relations, English, Business or similar field. Related professional work experience may substitute on a year-for-year basis for the degree. One year of related marketing and communications experience. What You Will Need Sound project management skills and ability to plan and organize multiple projects with established budgets, deliverables, and timelines. Knowledge of a wide range of communications practices and tactics including research, writing, editing, integrated digital communications, social media strategy, and public relations. Exceptional oral communication skills. Highly collaborative teammate and fast learner. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite. Ability to dive into projects (even if they require acquisition of new skills). Adapt readily to changing priorities and propose/implement solutions when problems arise. Ability to exercise discretion in handling sensitive information and maintaining confidentiality. Ability to communicate professionally and cordially with students, donors, alumni, employers, faculty, staff, and other members of the community. Ability to promote a welcoming environment which reflects the broad diversity and backgrounds represented by our faculty, students, staff, and community members. What We Would Like You to Have 3-4 years of relevant communications and marketing experience. Experience using graphic design programs, including the Adobe Creative Suite and/or Canva. Experience with website editing platforms, such as WebExpress or Drupal. Experience using targeted email marketing programs such as Pardot. Experience with CRM software, including Salesforce. Experience capturing photography and video for social media and websites. Marketing and communication experience in education/university setting. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 4, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Open-Rank-Tenure-Track-Professor-in-the-Institute-for-Behavioral-Genetics/75033": {
      "title": "Open Rank, Tenure-Track Professor in the Institute for Behavioral Genetics",
      "description": "Open Rank, Tenure-Track Professor in the Institute for Behavioral Genetics Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Open Rank, Tenure-Track Professor in the Institute for Behavioral Genetics Requisition Number: 75033 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Institute for Behavioral Genetics (IBG), University of Colorado Boulder, invites applications for a tenured faculty position at the Associate Professor or Professor level. Successful candidates will hold academic appointments in affiliated departments or another appropriate academic unit aligned with research expertise and interests. Appointments are anticipated to begin in August 2027. The Institute seeks to build on its historic strength in behavioral genetics. We are particularly interested in individuals with an internationally recognized externally funded program of research using statistical genetics and/or twin and family approaches to address scientific questions aligned with current Institute strengths. Competitive candidates may conduct cutting-edge human and/or computational research using whole-genome analysis and methods development, statistical and quantitative genetics, genetic epidemiology, population genetics, computational biology, bioinformatics, machine learning, artificial intelligence, genomic structural equation modeling, Mendelian randomization, and related methodologies. Substantive research areas include but are not limited to lifespan development and aging, neurodegeneration, cognition, mental health, psychiatric disorders, substance use, learning disabilities, sleep and circadian processes, and related areas of behavioral and health research. The Institute seeks to build on its historic strength in behavioral genetics. We are particularly interested in individuals using statistical genetics and/or twin and family approaches to address scientific questions aligned with current Institute strengths. The University of Colorado Boulder is committed to building a culturally diverse community of faculty, staff, and students dedicated to contributing to an inclusive campus environment. We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including veterans and individuals with disabilities. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Founded in 1967, the Institute for Behavioral Genetics (IBG) is one of the world's leading research institutes for genetic research on behavior. Its mission is to conduct and facilitate research which examines the genetic bases of individual differences in behavior and to conduct research training in this interdisciplinary area. There are currently 9 tenured or tenure-track faculty rostered in the Institute and based at IBG. In total there are 28 faculty fellows, most of whom hold joint appointments in academic units on the Boulder and Denver campuses. In addition to our research mission, faculty on the Boulder campus teach both undergraduate and graduate students. IBG administers about 45-50 grants (including Center components and subcontracts), with external support of ~$10 million per fiscal year currently and expenditures ranging from $6-14 million annually over the past decade. We direct a NIMH pre- and postdoctoral training grants supporting 4 graduate students and 1 postdoctoral fellow, and we provide NIMH-supported methodology workshops for about 110 scientists annually. Appointees will have the opportunity to recruit postdoctoral fellows and graduate students using funds from these training grants. More detailed information can be found at: https://www.colorado.edu/ibg/ What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Appointees will participate in the research and teaching missions of both the Institute for Behavioral Genetics and their home department and contribute service to the Institute, department, university, and profession. Classroom teaching assignments will be determined in accordance with departmental policy and in consultation with the Department Chair and Institute Director. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an internationally recognized, externally funded research program and provide intellectual and scientific leadership that contributes to the strategic growth of the Institute. Appointees will foster interdisciplinary collaborations within IBG and across the broader university community, mentor graduate students, postdoctoral fellows, and junior faculty colleagues, and contribute to the training of the next generation of scientists. The appointed individual will also be expected to contribute to undergraduate and graduate education while advancing the Institute's mission through research excellence, collaboration, mentorship, and service. What You Should Know The University of Colorado Boulder has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their current and previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination, before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer $110,000-$145,000 (9-month salary), with an expected range of $110,000-$125,000 at the Associate level and $125,000-$145,000 at the Full Level. Candidates may expect starting salaries within the posted range depending upon accomplishments–including publication record and external funding, years of experience in comparable faculty positions, and discipline. There is a negotiable start-up package, and relocation assistance is available depending on department guidelines. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be authentic. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must hold a doctoral degree (PhD, MD/PhD, DPhil, or equivalent) in genetics, psychology, neuroscience, epidemiology, biostatistics, computational biology, physiology, or a related discipline. A minimum of 7 years of experience in a professor or equivalent position following postdoctoral training is expected. What We Would Like You to Have Applicants must have a record of achievement appropriate for appointment with tenure at the Associate Professor or Professor level at the University of Colorado Boulder. Candidates should demonstrate a distinguished record of scholarly accomplishment, sustained success in obtaining external funding, evidence of scientific leadership, and a strong commitment to mentoring and training. Demonstrated excellence in teaching and contributions to service are expected. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual files: Resume/CV Cover letter Statement of research interests (4 pages) Statement of teaching philosophy (2-3 pages) 3-5 research papers (there will be 5 file uploads listed below to upload each research paper individually) During the application process you will need to enter contact information for three references, from whom we will request letters of recommendation and additional materials, if needed, as the search progresses. Review of applications will be begin on October 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Assistant-Professor-Historian-of-Education-Tenure-Track/75032": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Professor – Historian of Education (Tenure Track) Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Assistant Professor – Historian of Education (Tenure Track) Requisition Number: 75032 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The School of Education invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in the History of Education . The successful candidate will help students critically examine the political, social, economic, and organizational forces that have shaped education policy and practice. This position will enhance the School’s interdisciplinary strengths by bringing historical perspectives to contemporary issues in education, including in areas such as educational policy, higher education, and education law. We particularly encourage scholars to apply whose work focuses on educational equity and/or intersectional struggles for justice. The faculty member will play an important role in the School’s Education Foundations, Policy, and Practice (EFPP) graduate program area. This program area brings together foundational disciplines—including history, philosophy, sociology, anthropology and law—as well as critical perspectives from curriculum theory and gender studies, to explore the social, political and economic forces that structure educational aims, institutions and practices. The faculty member will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the history of education and related areas of education policy and practice. They will strengthen doctoral training, support students undertaking historical research, and expand course offerings in the history of education. Through research, teaching, mentoring, and service, this position will advance scholarship that draws on historical understandings of education to inform educational policies and practices that are just, inclusive and effective. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder’s School of Education is a collaborative, research-driven community dedicated to transforming education through scholarship, innovation, and partnership. We prepare educators, researchers, and leaders to address complex challenges in learning and educational systems through rigorous research, engaged teaching, and meaningful collaboration with schools, communities, and organizations. Our faculty generate impactful scholarship that informs practice and policy, and our work is grounded in a deep commitment to equity, inclusive excellence, and expanding opportunity for learners across diverse contexts. We are driven by our shared commitments to diversity, democracy, equity, and justice and evidence-based policy and practice. For more information about the School of Education, please visit: https://www.colorado.edu/education/. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Maintaining an active research agenda and corresponding publication record that demonstrates a coherent progression of scholarship in the history of education. Conducting historical research that contributes to contemporary understandings of education policy and practice. Teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in the history of education, educational foundations, and related areas of education policy and practice. Contributing to the development of the School’s undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral programs. Teaching, advising and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students undertaking historical research and supporting their development as scholars. Contributing historical perspectives to interdisciplinary research, teaching, and program development across the School and university. Drawing on historical scholarship to inform curricula, policies, and practices that promote more equitable, just, and democratic educational outcomes. Providing intellectual leadership and outreach to campus partners, schools, communities, professional organizations, and the broader field. Contributing service to the program, School, university, profession, and broader community. What You Should Know This role is located in Boulder, CO. The workload for this role is 40% research/scholarly work, 40% teaching, advising & mentoring, and 20% service. Tenure track faculty are expected to teach four courses per academic year, and all courses are typically taught in person on campus. What We Can Offer The salary range for this nine-month faculty role is $87,500 - $97,500, plus an $8,000 relocation allowance as applicable. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be inspired. Be Boulder. What We Require Doctorate (Ph.D. or Ed.D.) in the history of education, educational foundations, education, history, education policy, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. A record of scholarly productivity—or demonstrated promise of such—in the history of education. Expertise in the history of education in the United States, including one or more of the following areas: education policy; higher education; education law; education and social movements; the influence of social, political, and cultural contexts on education. Training and experience using historical research methods. Evidence of effective teaching at the university level, at either the undergraduate or graduate level. A record of working successfully with people from a wide range of backgrounds and fostering a strong sense of community and belonging. What We Would Like You to Have A research agenda that examines connections between historical and contemporary issues of educational equity and justice. Expertise in historical questions connected to intersectional movements for justice, with an interest in applying this expertise to the history of Colorado and the surrounding region. Experience teaching courses in the history of education, educational foundations, education policy, or related interdisciplinary areas. Experience teaching or mentoring at the doctoral level. Experience advising or supporting graduate students conducting historical research. Demonstrated expertise in historical and archival research methods, as well as other innovative methods for producing and interpreting historical evidence. Potential for interdisciplinary collaboration within the School of Education and across the university. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A 3-page cover letter addressing how you meet the required and preferred qualifications with an emphasis on your scholarly/research agenda Curriculum Vitae 1-2 page Teaching Statement Contact information for three references Additional materials may be requested of candidates who advance to the second phase of the search (sample syllabi and letters of reference). Review of applications will begin November 5, 2026 (preference will be given to applicants who submit by this deadline) and will continue until the position is filled. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Assistant-Professor-s-Tenure-Track-Institute-for-Behavioral-Genetics/75031": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor(s) Tenure Track Institute for Behavioral Genetics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor(s) Tenure Track Institute for Behavioral Genetics Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Assistant Professor(s) Tenure Track Institute for Behavioral Genetics Requisition Number: 75031 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Institute for Behavioral Genetics (IBG), University of Colorado Boulder, invites applications for up to three tenure-track Assistant Professor positions. Successful candidates will hold academic appointments in affiliated departments or another appropriate academic unit aligned with research expertise and interests. Appointments are anticipated to begin in August 2027. The Institute seeks to build on its historic strength in behavioral genetics. We are particularly interested in individuals using statistical genetics and/or twin and family approaches to address scientific questions aligned with current Institute strengths. Substantive research areas include but are not limited to lifespan development and aging, neurodegeneration, cognition, mental health, psychiatric disorders, substance use, learning disabilities, sleep and circadian processes, and related areas of behavioral and health research. Competitive candidates may conduct cutting-edge human and/or computational research using whole-genome analysis and methods development, statistical and quantitative genetics, genetic epidemiology, population genetics, computational biology, bioinformatics, machine learning, artificial intelligence, genomic structural equation modeling, Mendelian randomization, and related methodologies. This search prioritizes computational, quantitative, theoretical, and data-driven research programs, or otherwise not dependent on dedicated wet-laboratory or animal research facilities. The University of Colorado Boulder is committed to building a culturally diverse community of faculty, staff, and students dedicated to contributing to an inclusive campus environment. We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including veterans and individuals with disabilities. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Founded in 1967, the Institute for Behavioral Genetics (IBG) is one of the world's leading research institutes for genetic research on behavior. Its mission is to conduct and facilitate research which examines the genetic bases of individual differences in behavior and to conduct research training in this interdisciplinary area. There are currently 9 tenured or tenure-track faculty rostered in the Institute and based at IBG. In total there are 28 faculty fellows, most of whom hold joint appointments in academic units on the Boulder and Denver campuses. In addition to our research mission, faculty on the Boulder campus teach both undergraduate and graduate students. IBG administers about 45-50 grants (including Center components and subcontracts), with external support of ~$10 million per fiscal year currently and expenditures ranging from $6-14 million annually over the past decade. We direct a NIMH pre- and postdoctoral training grants supporting 4 graduate students and 1 postdoctoral fellow, and we provide NIMH-supported methodology workshops for about 110 scientists annually. Appointees will have the opportunity to recruit postdoctoral fellows and graduate students using funds from these training grants. More detailed information can be found at: https://www.colorado.edu/ibg/ What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Appointees will participate in the research and teaching missions of both IBG and their home department and contribute service to IBG, the Department, and profession. Classroom teaching assignments will be in accordance with Department policy. They will typically include two courses per academic year, one undergraduate and one graduate course each year as long as the appointees remain research active. The courses and course load will be determined in consultation with the Department Chair and Institute Director. To earn tenure, the appointed individuals are expected to establish an independent, externally funded research program and contribute to departmental teaching and mentoring of the next generation of scientists. What We Can Offer This position offers a 9-month salary range of $95,000-$110,000. Candidates may expect starting salaries within the posted range depending upon accomplishments–including publication record and external funding, years of experience in comparable faculty positions, and discipline. There is a negotiable start-up package, and relocation assistance is available depending on department guidelines. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be authentic. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must hold a doctoral degree (PhD, MD/PhD, or DPhil) in a life science discipline that includes, but is not limited to, neuroscience, biology, genetics, psychology, or physiology. Completion of a postdoctoral training program or equivalent is expected. What We Would Like You to Have Accomplishments during graduate and postgraduate training should have stamped the candidate as a creative and promising investigator. If the individual does not have formal teaching experience, they should have demonstrated a commitment to develop the skills for high-quality collegiate teaching. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual files: Resume/CV Cover letter Statement of research interests (4 pages) Statement of teaching philosophy (2-3 pages) 3-5 research papers (there will be 5 file uploads listed below to upload each research paper individually) During the application process you will need to enter contact information for three references, from whom we will request letters of recommendation and additional materials, if needed, as the search progresses. Review of applications will be begin on October 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-the-ATLAS-Institute-with-tenure-home-in-Mechanical-Engineering/74980": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in the ATLAS Institute",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty in the ATLAS Institute - with tenure home in Mechanical Engineering Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenure-Track Faculty in the ATLAS Institute - with tenure home in Mechanical Engineering Requisition Number: 74980 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The ATLAS Institute at the University of Colorado Boulder invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in creative technology and design. We seek an imaginative, collaborative, and boundary-pushing scholar whose work transcends traditional disciplinary boundaries and engages deeply with technology, design, and human experience. The successful candidate will lead a forward-looking and externally funded research agenda, teach across ATLAS’s undergraduate and graduate programs, and contribute meaningfully to the institute’s interdisciplinary community. The position will have a tenure home in the Mechanical Engineering department. We welcome candidates whose work connects with existing ATLAS strengths while also introducing new directions. Example areas include, but are not limited to: Bio-inspired & miniature robotics, compliant actuation, soft robotics, and muscle-tendon locomotion for dynamic systems. Wearable robotics, exoskeletons, prosthetics, flexible/printed electronics, electronic skin, and adaptive human-assistive platforms. Medical & surgical manipulators, physical human–machine interaction, sensorimotor control, bio-sensing, and safe soft-tissue intervention. Smart textiles, soft/animate materials, and functional bio-wearables bridging digital fabrication, molecular/tattoo interfaces, and interactive crafts. Dynamic shape-changing interfaces, programmable materials, spatial/environmental sensing, and tangible/AR-VR physical computing. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs, earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally importantly, we are committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college has three core foci: (i) Research & Innovation, (ii) Education, and (iii) Inclusion. We believe that our college’s success requires faculty who actively work to create an inclusive culture within the college and are committed to the campus plan of creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science, please visit the college website . One of 12 institutes at CU Boulder, ATLAS is home to researchers who transcend traditional disciplinary structures of engineering, design, science, and art to inspire new realms of invention. We make tangible and digital tools and methods that shape how people interact with the world for a more inclusive, inspired, and sustainable society. ATLAS is a home for radical creativity and invention—where faculty and students from engineering, design, science, and the arts converge to prototype new tools, methods, and experiences that shape a more inclusive, inspired, and sustainable world. Our labs span areas such as e‑textiles, human‑computer interaction, biomaterials, nanotechnology, data visualization, fabrication, music, performance, linguistics, and brain–music interfaces. Additionally, CU Boulder is internationally recognized as a research center in the areas of physics, geography, spatial modeling, and remote sensing, earth and environmental sciences, and sustainability. To learn more, please visit the ATLAS website . More than 1,700 students and over 80 faculty and staff in the Paul M. Rady Department of Mechanical Engineering form an innovative and supportive community in the foothills of the Rocky Mountains. Students work together to learn the fundamentals of the field and engage in rigorous, team-based experiences addressing real-world problems. The department’s award-winning faculty tackle societal challenges through technological innovation impacting the environment, security and human health. As a department, we are committed to high-impact research addressing real-world problems, active learning and experiential engineering education, and an inclusive community that brings together students, faculty, staff and alumni. For more information about the Paul M. Rady Department of Mechanical Engineering, please visit the department website . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Successful candidates will lead an innovative, externally funded research group or creative lab, teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate offerings, and provide service to the institute, campus, and professional community while advancing inclusive excellence. The standard workload distribution for this role is 40% scholarly and creative work (research), 40% teaching, and 20% leadership and service, with an emphasis on interdisciplinary collaboration across engineering, design, environmental science, and the humanities. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2027, but a later start date may be negotiated. Appointments will be rostered in the ATLAS Institute with a tenure home in the Department of Mechanical Engineering , College of Engineering and Applied Sciences, at the University of Colorado. The successful candidate should be prepared to obtain external sponsorship to support their research group and teach and work with students at all levels from a variety of cultural and intellectual backgrounds. As part of the University of Colorado Boulder’s deep commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion, the campus has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination – before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer The anticipated academic-year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $118,000-$130,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to some of these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate in engineering, physical sciences, or a closely related field, or have their degree conferred before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials* as individual PDF files: Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals. Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society. (2-3 pages) A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement in or with engineering, or serving on advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision . (1-2 pages). During the application process, you will be asked to provide the name and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, however for full consideration please apply by November 1, 2026. Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/CIRES-CSL-Temporary-Researcher-Lidar-Scientist/74972": {
      "title": "CIRES/ CSL Temporary Researcher, Lidar Scientist",
      "description": "CIRES/ CSL Temporary Researcher, Lidar Scientist Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail CIRES/ CSL Temporary Researcher, Lidar Scientist Requisition Number: 74972 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Part-Time Posting Close Date: 25-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Cooperative Institute for Research in Environmental Sciences (CIRES) at the University of Colorado Boulder encourages applications for Temporary Researcher within NOAA’s Chemical Sciences Laboratory (CSL). The successful candidate will work within the Atmospheric Remote Sensing programs alongside a team of federal and university employees engaged in research investigating tropospheric ozone, stratospheric intrusions, and their impacts on regional air quality. A primary responsibility of this role will be leading the synthesis of complex field campaign datasets and authoring high-impact, peer-reviewed scientific publications. Our group consists of scientists and researchers at all stages of their careers working together to analyze remote sensing data, evaluate atmospheric transport models, and interpret the photochemistry driving ozone production. We are looking for an expert with an extensive, proven track record of writing and publishing peer-reviewed research papers on atmospheric composition, particularly involving ozone lidar networks or aircraft-based observations. A Ph.D. in Atmospheric Science, Chemistry, or a closely related field is highly preferred. CSL has a highly collaborative work culture, and we are looking for a candidate who actively subscribes to this philosophy. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are At CIRES , the Cooperative Institute for Research In Environmental Sciences, more than 900 people work to understand the dynamic Earth system, including people’s relationship with the planet. An institute at the University of Colorado Boulder, we have partnered with the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) since 1967. Our areas of expertise include weather and climate, wildfire and water, changes at Earth’s poles, air quality and atmospheric chemistry, water resources, solid Earth sciences, and more. Our researchers work in 10 CU Boulder departments and eight NOAA laboratories and centers. NOAA's Chemical Sciences Laboratory (CSL) is a world leader in atmospheric chemistry and composition, developing and deploying advanced technologies to ensure NOAA remains at the forefront in protecting lives, property, and livelihoods of the American people. https://csl.noaa.gov/ What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Analyze and interpret lidar measurements and use data for various boundary layer research studies including air quality, wildfires, and climate, as well as measurement studies designed to inform numerical model improvement efforts. Participate in the planning of group short- and long-term research strategies. Present results at scientific meetings or publish them in peer-reviewed literature. What You Should Know This appointment is not eligible for vacation leave, holidays, annual merit pay increases, nor employee health, retirement, or related benefits, except those required by law. This appointment will accrue 0.034 hours of sick leave for every hour worked. The hours for this position may vary and are not guaranteed. This temporary position cannot extend past a maximum of 12 months. The targeted start date for the appointment is October 1, 2026. This position will be rostered in CIRES at the University of Colorado Boulder but will be physically situated in the David Skaggs Research Center, 325 Broadway, Boulder, CO 80305, a federal campus. If you are the selected finalist you will be required to pass a federal laboratory background clearance for site access. Non-US citizens without green cards may have limited building access, and may require an escort in the building. Contact Michael Zucker (michael.zucker@colorado.edu) with any questions This position can accommodate a hybrid work modality. What We Can Offer The hourly rate for this position ranges from $48.56 - $64.42 per hour. Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Benefits Temporary positions at the University of Colorado are not benefits-eligible, however, all positions are eligible for paid sick leave . Be Statements Be driven. Be successful. Be Boulder. What We Require A Ph.D. in physics, chemistry, meteorology, atmospheric science, environmental engineering, or a closely related field. Experience in experimental physical/atmospheric sciences. Experience in data analysis and programming (such as MATLAB, IDL, or other image visualization software). What You Will Need Demonstrated ability to perform boundary layer research, publish in peer-reviewed journals, and present at national/international conferences. The ability to work in a team and participate in the development of original research projects. Ability to work in a collaborative environment with a diverse group of scientists. Ability to be self-driven and meet deadlines. What We Would Like You to Have Experience with ozone and/or Doppler lidars. Experience working with lidar data. A record demonstrating the ability to communicate science through publication and conference presentations. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume/CV. If you are identified as a finalist, the search committee will request one letter recommendation at a later time. If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources department using an approved online vendor. If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. Please apply by September 25, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Michael Zucker Posting Contact Email: michael.zucker@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/CIRES-CSL-Stratospheric-Modeling-and-Analysis-SMA-Program-Temporary-Researcher/74971": {
      "title": "CIRES/ CSL Stratospheric Modeling and Analysis (SMA) Program, Temporary Researcher",
      "description": "CIRES/ CSL Stratospheric Modeling and Analysis (SMA) Program, Temporary Researcher Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail CIRES/ CSL Stratospheric Modeling and Analysis (SMA) Program, Temporary Researcher Requisition Number: 74971 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Part-Time Posting Close Date: 25-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Cooperative Institute for Research in Environmental Sciences (CIRES) at the University of Colorado welcomes applications for a Temporary Researcher to join the Stratospheric Modeling and Analysis (SMA) Program of the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's Chemical Sciences Laboratory (CSL) in Boulder, Colorado. Our group uses a variety of models and observations to study the complex relationships between stratospheric composition, chemistry, dynamics, and radiation, and how these processes affect weather and air quality. If hired, you will work closely with a team of federal and CIRES scientists to investigate how aerosols from rocket emissions and satellite re-entry affect our atmosphere. This project will involve work with large satellite and model data sets, chemistry-climate modeling in a supercomputing environment, aerosol and debris tracking, simulation of rocket emissions and satellite reentry events, and assessment of the impact of space activity on the upper atmosphere and stratosphere. We seek a person with extensive experience and interest in global-scale modeling and data analysis. A PhD degree is required, preferably in atmospheric science. The ideal candidate would have experience with satellite reentry simulations, aerosol physics, statistics, atmospheric chemistry/radiation/dynamics, numerical modeling, and computer programming. While few candidates will possess every desirable skill, we prioritize applicants who have specialized expertise in the area of space activity impacts on the atmosphere, an eagerness to advance scientific understanding using simulations and observations, and a willingness to serve as an early-career mentor and scientific advisor to the team. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are At CIRES , the Cooperative Institute for Research In Environmental Sciences, more than 900 people work to understand the dynamic Earth system, including people’s relationship with the planet. An institute at the University of Colorado Boulder, we have partnered with the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) since 1967. Our areas of expertise include weather and climate, wildfire and water, changes at Earth’s poles, air quality and atmospheric chemistry, water resources, solid Earth sciences, and more. Our researchers work in 10 CU Boulder departments and eight NOAA laboratories and centers. NOAA's Chemical Sciences Laboratory (CSL) is a world leader in atmospheric chemistry and composition, developing and deploying advanced technologies to ensure NOAA remains at the forefront in protecting lives, property, and livelihoods of the American people. https://csl.noaa.gov/ . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Investigate Space Activity Impacts and determine how aerosols from rocket emissions and satellite re-entry events affect the upper atmosphere and stratosphere. 20% Analyze and interpret large-scale satellite and model datasets to track atmospheric aerosols and space debris. 20% Design simulations specifically focused on rocket emissions and satellite re-entry events using applied aerosol physics and numerical modeling. 10% Act as a subject matter expert and scientific advisor to the broader SMA team, specifically regarding space activity impacts on the atmosphere. 25% Serve as a dedicated mentor to guide and develop early-career researchers on the team. 20% Performs other duties as necessary. 5% What You Should Know This appointment is not eligible for vacation leave, holidays, annual merit pay increases, nor employee health, retirement, or related benefits, except those required by law. This appointment will accrue 0.034 hours of sick leave for every hour worked. The hours for this position may vary and are not guaranteed. This temporary position cannot extend past a maximum of 12 months. What We Can Offer The hourly rate for this position ranges from $48.56 - $64.42/hour. Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Benefits Temporary positions at the University of Colorado are not benefits-eligible, however, all positions are eligible for paid sick leave . Be Statements Be engaged. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require PhD degree in atmospheric, climate and global change research. Minimum of 20 years of experience in related fields. What You Will Need Strong knowledge of the impact of space activity (such as rocket emissions and satellite re-entry) on the atmosphere. Ability to serve as a scientific advisor and dedicate time to mentoring early-career scientists on the team. Strong leadership and connections to the broader international science community in related fields. What We Would Like You to Have A strong background in aerosol physics, atmospheric chemistry, stratospheric dynamics, and radiation. Experience conducting simulations focused on satellite re-entry and aerosol/debris tracking. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume/CV. If you are identified as a finalist, the search committee will request one letter recommendation at a later time. If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources department using an approved online vendor. If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. Please apply by September 25, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Professional-Research-Assistant/74949": {
      "title": "Professional Research Assistant",
      "description": "Professional Research Assistant Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Professional Research Assistant Requisition Number: 74949 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 07-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The proposed project will contribute complementary advancement of measurement science supporting AM-specific performance metrics connected to challenges in surface quality measurement science. Better understanding of measurement and characterization of surface notches in AM components will further NIST’s mission to advance measurement science and increase industrial competitiveness through better processes for making fatigue life predictions from non-destructive surface texture measurements. This position is part of the National Institute of Standards and Technology's (NIST) Professional Research Experience Program (PREP). NIST recognizes that its research staff may wish to collaborate with researchers at academic institutions on specific projects of mutual interest, thus requires that such institutions must be the recipient of a PREP award. The PREP program requires staff from a wide range of backgrounds to work on scientific research in many areas. Employees in this position will perform technical work that underpins the scientific research of the collaboration. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are PREP is a special partnership between the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) and the University of Colorado Boulder. PREP provides research opportunities to CU undergraduate and graduate students, as well as researchers with a Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD in NIST labs to gain research experience alongside NIST scientists. The particular group that needs a researcher is the Fatigue and Fracture Group at NIST. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Surface roughness measurement and analysis for additively manufactured metals. Fatigue testing and fractography for additively manufactured metals. Fatigue modeling for additively manufactured metals. Publishing results in peer-reviewed journals. Presenting results at internal meetings, and occasional meetings with external partners. Ensuring that results, protocols, software, and documentation have been archived or otherwise transmitted to the larger organization. What You Should Know This position requires the successful completion of an NAIS agreement and federal security clearance as mandated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST). Employment is contingent upon approval of these requirements as determined by NIST. Anticipated start date is December 28, 2026. Anticipated end date is one year, but no later than March 31, 2028. Privacy Act Statement Authority: 15 U.S.C. § 278g-1(e)(1) and (e)(3) and 15 U.S.C. § 272(b) and (c) Purpose: The National Institute for Standards and Technology (NIST) hosts the Professional Research Experience Program (PREP) which is designed to provide valuable laboratory experience and financial assistance to undergraduates, post-bachelor’s degree holders, graduate students, master’s degree holders, postdocs, and faculty. PREP is a 5-year cooperative agreement between NIST laboratories and participating PREP Universities to establish a collaborative research relationship between NIST and U.S. institutions of higher education in the following fields including (but may not be limited to) biochemistry, biological sciences, chemistry, computer science, engineering, electronics, materials science, mathematics, nanoscale science, neutron science, physical science, physics, and statistics. This collection of information is needed to facilitate administrative functions of the PREP Program. Routine Uses: NIST will use the information collected to perform the requisite reviews of the applications to determine eligibility, and to meet programmatic requirements. Disclosure of this information is also subject to all the published routine uses as identified in the Privacy Act System of Records Notices: NIST-1: NIST Associates. Disclosure: Furnishing this information is voluntary. When you submit the form, you are indicating your voluntary consent for NIST to use of the information you submit for the purpose stated. What We Can Offer The salary range for this full-time position is $54,000 - $78,000 annually. Onboarding/relocation assistance may be available, however, if within a 12-month period you exercise your right to willfully terminate your employment with the University of Colorado, you hereby agree to reimburse the department for your relocation expense. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be fun. Be talented. Be Boulder. What We Require A Bachelor’s degree in Computer Science, Engineering, Manufacturing, or a related field. What We Would Like You to Have Familiarity with the following techniques: X-ray micro computed tomography, laser confocal scanning microscopy, focus variation microscopy, fatigue testing, fractography, fatigue modeling. Strong oral and written communication skills. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV. Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor. If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process, you will need to enter contact information for a reference. We will request the letter of recommendation immediately following your application. Please apply by October 7, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Lisa Valencia Posting Contact Email: prepscientist@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Assistant-Professor-in-Mathematics-Education-Tenure-Track/74919": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Mathematics Education (Tenure Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Mathematics Education (Tenure Track) Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Assistant Professor in Mathematics Education (Tenure Track) Requisition Number: 74919 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The School of Education invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Mathematics Education. We seek a scholar with a strong or promising research trajectory in mathematics education and experience teaching in elementary classrooms who will strengthen our undergraduate, teacher licensure, and graduate programs by contributing expertise in mathematics education, expanding course offerings in elementary mathematics methods, and fostering meaningful partnerships with local school districts. The expectations for this position are to maintain an active research program; teach elementary education courses and other courses in our undergraduate and graduate programs; contribute to elementary licensure preparation; and support equitable evidence-based mathematics instruction for learners who have been historically underserved. We particularly welcome applicants whose research addresses K-8 mathematics education, especially K-5 teaching, learning and teacher preparation. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder’s School of Education is a collaborative, research-driven community dedicated to transforming education through scholarship, innovation, and partnership. We prepare educators, researchers, and leaders to address complex challenges in learning and educational systems through rigorous research, engaged teaching, and meaningful collaboration with schools, communities, and organizations. Our faculty engage in scholarship that informs practice and policy. We are driven by our shared commitments to diversity, democracy, equity and justice, and evidence-based policy and practice. For more information about the School of Education, please visit: https://www.colorado.edu/education/ What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Maintaining an active, thoughtful and compelling research agenda and corresponding publication record that demonstrates a coherent progression of scholarship in K-8 mathematics education. Seeking grant funding to support a research agenda and graduate students. Contributing to the School’s undergraduate teacher education, licensure, master’s, and doctoral programs. Advising undergraduate students; advising and mentoring graduate students. Providing outreach and leadership to schools, districts, campus partners, professional organizations, and broader communities. Teaching undergraduate elementary mathematics education courses, as well as undergraduate and graduate courses in mathematics education, courses in teacher learning and practice, and student assessment. Supporting the development of equitable, inclusive, culturally responsive, and evidence-based mathematics education curricula, with particular attention to preparing educators to support historically marginalized, multilingual, and emergent bilingual learners. Building and sustaining meaningful partnerships with local schools, districts, educators, and communities to strengthen elementary mathematics teaching and teacher preparation. Contributing service to the program, School, university, profession, and broader community. What You Should Know This role is located in Boulder, CO. The workload for this role is 40% research, scholarly, and creative work, 40% teaching, advising & mentoring, and 20% service. Tenure-track faculty are expected to teach four courses per academic year, and all courses are typically taught in person on campus. What We Can Offer The salary range for this nine-month faculty role is $87,500 - $97,500, plus an $8,000 relocation allowance as applicable. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be creative. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require Doctorate in mathematics education, curriculum and instruction, teacher education, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. A record of scholarly productivity—or demonstrated promise of such—in mathematics education. Expertise in one or more of the following areas: early elementary mathematics education; mathematics teaching and learning; equitable and inclusive mathematics instruction; mathematics teacher learning and development; or social and cultural contexts of mathematics education. PK–12 teaching experience in U.S. schools, preferably at the elementary level. Evidence of effective teaching at the undergraduate level and demonstrated capacity to prepare elementary teachers to teach mathematics. A record of working successfully with people from a wide range of backgrounds and fostering a strong sense of community and belonging. What We Would Like You to Have Three or more years of elementary teaching experience in U.S. schools. Expertise in elementary mathematics education, particularly early elementary mathematics teaching and learning. A research agenda focused on educational equity, justice, and access to high-quality mathematics learning opportunities. Expertise in supporting educators working with culturally and linguistically diverse students, including emergent bilingual learners, in mathematics education settings. Knowledgeable about the affordances and constraints of contemporary digital technologies (particularly GenAI) to support teaching practice and student learning. Experience supporting teacher licensure programs. Experience developing partnerships with schools, districts, educators, or community-based organizations. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A 3-page cover letter addressing how you meet the required and preferred qualifications with an emphasis on your scholarly/research agenda. Curriculum Vitae. 1-2 page Teaching Statement. During the application process you will need to enter contact information for three references who will be contacted to provide letters of recommendation and additional materials, if needed, as the search progresses. Review of applications will begin October 31, 2026 (preference will be given to applicants who submit by this deadline) and will continue until the position is filled. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: David Webb Posting Contact Email: dcwebb@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Senior-Executive-Digital-Strategist/74880": {
      "title": "Senior Executive Digital Strategist",
      "description": "Senior Executive Digital Strategist Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Senior Executive Digital Strategist Requisition Number: 74880 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 29-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary The Office of Strategic Relations and Communications at CU Boulder is seeking a senior executive digital strategist to support the Chancellor’s social media presence and strategy! The Senior Executive Digital Strategist leads the strategic development, creation, management, and execution of social media content for the Chancellor and Chancellor’s spouse. The position is responsible for developing and delivering a coordinated digital strategy that raises the Chancellor’s visibility and voice. It promotes thought leadership and builds connections to important groups. The strategy also supports CU Boulder’s mission, priorities, and reputation. The strategist will develop compelling, timely and authentic content tailored to each platform and audience; find opportunities to amplify the Chancellor’s leadership and the university’s impact; and ensure a consistent, distinctive digital presence across channels. The role requires strong editorial judgment, exceptional storytelling and writing skills, an understanding of emerging digital trends and the ability to move quickly and strategically in a dynamic public-facing environment. Reporting to the senior director of chancellor communications, this role will work closely with the Chancellor, his executive team, staff and spouse, and with social media and content creators from around campus including those from strategic communications, athletics and student life. The ideal candidate will be a highly creative content driver with a demonstrated knowledge of Instagram, LinkedIn, TikTok, X, Threads, YouTube and Facebook, while monitoring new and emerging channels. The role will work irregular hours, including nights and weekends, on and beyond campus in order to generate, capture and distribute content. The position may occasionally encounter sensitive information–a dedication to confidentiality is vital. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are Strategy + Promise + Experience As CU Boulder’s communications leaders, we advance the university’s mission through an integrated marketing and communications approach. Partnering with communication colleagues campus-wide, our team synthesizes expertise in strategic communications, marketing, constituent experience and technology to support CU Boulder’s brand promise, mission, vision and strategic priorities. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Chancellor social media and content generation Develop and implement an editorial calendar and social media strategy for the Chancellor. Capture, post and monitor various platforms where the Chancellor or the Chancellor’s spouse has a presence. Attend campus and external events to generate content, with some events occurring on nights and weekends. Develop metric tracking and reporting to measure social media outcomes and refine the strategy on an ongoing basis. Build a comprehensive strategy specific to each social media platform, with clear goals, audiences, content approaches, voice, cadence and measures of success. Ensure the Chancellor’s social presence is cohesive across platforms while taking advantage of the distinct audiences, formats, and strengths of each channel. Continue to build partnerships and collaboration efforts between social media teams and personnel from around campus to aid in strategy, cross-sharing, content ideas and generation. Develop executive-level strategies that align with CU Boulder priorities and goals. Other duties as assigned Participate in news and media efforts, support marketing campaigns, strategic events, crisis communications and other goals and efforts for Strategic Relations and Communications or the Chancellor’s office. What You Should Know What You Should Know This position will work normal business hours in addition to irregular hours, including nights and weekends, to generate and distribute social media content for senior university leaders that aligns with the university’s strategic priorities. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The annual salary range for this position is $85,000-$91,000. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be ambitious. Be collaborative. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require A bachelor’s degree in marketing, advertising, public relations, communications, journalism, or a related field. 5+ years of professional job-related experience. Photography, videography, and editing experience, particularly for the social media space. Demonstrated understanding of how to measure, analyze, and report on social media performance using platform- specific analytics, web analytics, social listening and monitoring tools, and other measurement technologies. What You Will Need What You Will Need Ability to develop and implement strategic digital communications that advance organizational priorities and strengthen engagement with key audiences. Exceptional storytelling, editorial judgment, and communications skills, with the ability to craft authentic and compelling content for executive leaders. Ability to build effective partnerships and influence collaborators across a complex organization to achieve shared communication goals. Strong critical thinking and analytical skills, including the ability to translate data, audience insights, and emerging trends into actionable recommendations. Ability to thrive in a dynamic, high-profile environment by exercising discretion, adapting to changing priorities, and managing multiple deadlines simultaneously. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Experience in managing social media accounts for senior executives, elected officials, organizational leaders, university presidents/chancellors, or other high-profile figures. 7+ years of professional job-related experience. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current Resume. A Cover Letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. A PDF sample of work that includes examples of professional content created for the social media space. Optional: Portfolio link We may request references at a later time. Please apply by September 29, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Project-Coordinator-and-REACH-Lab-Manager/74854": {
      "title": "Project Coordinator and REACH Lab Manager",
      "description": "Project Coordinator and REACH Lab Manager Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Project Coordinator and REACH Lab Manager Requisition Number: 74854 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 15-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Project Coordinator and REACH Lab Manager will oversee the day-to-day coordination of a NIH-funded mixed-methods study examining how neighborhood environments shape cognitive health and dementia risk across adulthood, while also supporting the broader research activities of the REACH Lab (Research on Environments, Aging, Communities, and Health). Responsibilities will include coordinating research activities across multiple qualitative field sites and national longitudinal cohort studies; organizing project meetings, timelines, and study documentation; supporting participant recruitment and community partnerships; coordinating training and supervision of graduate and undergraduate research assistants to ensure standardized protocols and secure data management; managing interview transcription, qualitative data organization, and multi-site data workflows; and facilitating coordination with collaborators conducting geospatial and quantitative analyses. The position will also support general REACH Lab operations, including onboarding and coordinating research team members, maintaining shared protocols and project resources, assisting with lab meetings and research dissemination, and supporting communication across multiple ongoing projects. Additional responsibilities include tracking project spending and milestones, assisting with NIH progress reports, IRB submissions and renewals, manuscripts, presentations, and other research products. The ideal candidate will be highly organized, collaborative, detail-oriented, and comfortable coordinating interdisciplinary research involving multiple investigators, trainees, institutions, and methodological approaches. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The Institute of Behavior Science (IBS) is a leading interdisciplinary research institute dedicated to addressing problems of societal importance through collaborative social and behavioral science research. The institute includes approximately 400 members, including fellows, research and administrative staff, postdoctoral scholars, and graduate and undergraduate student researchers, and manages a typical annual grant portfolio of approximately $40 million. By engaging faculty from the social and behavioral sciences at the University of Colorado Boulder, IBS encourages work that transcends disciplinary boundaries, that illuminates the complexity of social behavior and social life, and that has important implications for social policy. The institute is connected to a wide range of academic departments, including Anthropology, History, Economics, Environmental Studies, Geography, Psychology, Political Science, Sociology, Women and Gender Studies, and others. The institute is home to 13 programs and centers, including the Prevention Science Program, International Development Program, Center for the Study and Prevention of Violence, Natural Hazards Center, and CU Population Center. While IBS has historical strengths in research related to population, the environment, violence prevention, health, and international issues, it also embraces scholarship on well-being and resilience, archaeology, and crime and justice, among myriad other topics. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Life Course Cognability Project Coordination Coordinate day-to-day activities for the NIH-funded Life Course Cognability study across multiple qualitative field sites and national longitudinal cohort studies. Support launch and implementation of qualitative fieldwork, including participant recruitment, community partnerships, interviewer scheduling, and multi-site coordination. Coordinate training of graduate and undergraduate research assistants to ensure standardized research protocols. Manage transcription workflows, organization of qualitative data, secure data storage, quality-control procedures, and documentation across field sites. Facilitate coordination with investigators, collaborators, and national cohort studies conducting geospatial and quantitative analyses. Support workflows for custom neighborhood exposure measures, data-use procedures, documentation, data transfers, and integrated analyses across cohort studies. Maintain project timelines, organize team and investigator meetings, track milestones and deliverables, coordinate communication across collaborating institutions, and maintain study documentation and shared project resources. Research Dissemination and Project Communications Support preparation of manuscripts, conference presentations, reports, figures, community-facing materials, and other research products. Assist with communication of study activities and findings to collaborators, community partners, participants, and broader audiences. Research Team Training, Supervision, and REACH Lab Management Support onboarding, training, coordination, and day-to-day supervision of graduate and undergraduate research assistants. Help organize REACH Lab meetings, maintain lab protocols and shared resources, coordinate research team activities, and facilitate communication across multiple ongoing projects. Support a collaborative, organized, and professional research environment. Research Administration, Compliance, and Financial Tracking Track project spending, personnel effort, milestones, and other administrative requirements. Assist with NIH annual progress reports, IRB submissions and renewals, data-use agreements, study documentation, and other institutional and sponsor reporting requirements What You Should Know The start date is flexible. Ideally late fall 2026, with January 2027 as the preferred latest start date. The position may be fully in person at the Institute of Behavioral Science (IBS) at CU Boulder or hybrid. Residence in the Denver–Boulder metropolitan area is strongly preferred. What We Can Offer $72,000 annual salary plus benefits. Dedicated workspace at CU Boulder’s Institute of Behavioral Science, with in-person or hybrid flexibility. Hands-on experience managing a large NIH-funded, multi-site research project. Close mentorship and collaboration with an interdisciplinary national research team. Opportunities to contribute to manuscripts, presentations, and other scholarly products. Professional development in project management, grant administration, research coordination, and team supervision. Access to CU Boulder and IBS seminars, workshops, and research networks. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require Bachelor’s degree in public health, geography, sociology, psychology, gerontology, epidemiology, social sciences, or a related field. Relevant experience supporting research, project coordination, or program administration. Candidates should have demonstrated organizational and communication skills; experience coordinating multiple tasks, timelines, or collaborators; and the ability to work independently and collaboratively within an interdisciplinary research team. What We Would Like You to Have Master’s or PhD in public health, geography, sociology, psychology, gerontology, epidemiology, social sciences, or a related field; and/or prior work experience working with human-subjects research, research assistants, qualitative or mixed-methods projects, data management, or university research administration. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Cover letter. Resume/CV. List of at least three references. (Optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree: If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor. However, if your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit an English-translated version as an Optional document. Please apply by October 15, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Post-Doctoral-Associate/74423": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Associate",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Associate Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Post-Doctoral Associate Requisition Number: 74423 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 23-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 23-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary We are seeking a Postdoctoral Associate to investigate fundamental problems in nanoscience, with an emphasis on structural characterization using cryogenic electron microscopy (cryo-EM). The project focuses on semiconductor nanocrystal–enzyme complexes to uncover how nanoscale structure influences interfacial electron transfer and photocatalytic function. Research activities may include cryo-EM sample preparation and imaging, advanced single-particle analysis, and structure–property studies of nanocrystal–enzyme interfaces. This position offers the opportunity to work at the intersection of nanomaterials and biomolecular science while developing innovative methods for studying complex hybrid systems. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The Department of Chemistry at the University of Colorado Boulder is proud to be a vibrant, research-driven department dedicated to exploring the fundamental nature of matter and energy. Our faculty, students, and staff are engaged in groundbreaking work across a wide range of fields—from analytical, organic, and physical chemistry to materials and nanoscience. Nationally recognized and consistently ranked among the top programs in the country, our department is home to award-winning researchers, innovative educators, and a collaborative spirit that drives discovery. To learn more about our department and our team please visit our website: https://www.colorado.edu/chemistry/ What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Conduct research on semiconductor nanocrystal–enzyme complexes and nanoscale optoelectronic materials. Design and perform cryo-EM experiments, including sample preparation, imaging, and single-particle analysis. Investigate structure–property relationships related to electron transfer, catalysis, and photocatalytic function. Analyze and interpret experimental data to guide research direction and project goals. Prepare research reports, manuscripts, proposals, presentations, and related scientific communications. Collaborate with interdisciplinary research teams and mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers. Develop innovative methods for studying nanocrystal–enzyme systems. What We Can Offer Annual salary range: $58,500–$62,000 Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Ph.D. in biochemistry, structural biology, biophysics, molecular biology, chemistry, physics, materials science, computational biology, or related field What You Will Need Excellent written and oral communication skills Strong analytical and interpersonal problem-solving skills Excellent organizational skills What We Would Like You to Have This position is best suited for candidates with expertise in structural characterization of biomolecules by cryo-EM. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as PDF's. Resume or CV Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources department using an approved online vendor. If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process you will need to enter contact information for three references who may be contacted to provide a letter of recommendation and additional materials, if needed, as the search progresses. For consideration, please apply by October 23, 2026. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Assistant-Athletic-Trainer-Football/75041": {
      "title": "Assistant Athletic Trainer",
      "description": "Assistant Athletic Trainer - Football Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Assistant Athletic Trainer - Football Requisition Number: 75041 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 27-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 22-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The CU Boulder Athletics Department is seeking an Assistant Athletic Trainer for the football program. This role will provide for the care, prevention, treatment and rehabilitation of athletic injuries and illnesses. Work includes administering injury prevention measures for athletes; serving as first responder to injury situations; maintaining injury records; reporting on rehabilitation progress to coaches; and planning return-to-play for injured athletes in collaboration with team physicians and physical therapists. The role also supports the direction of the Sports Medicine Program by supervising the training room and serving on assigned task forces and programs. This position reports to the Head Football Athletic Trainer. This position is responsible for promoting the philosophy and objectives of the intercollegiate athletics program. This shall include adhering to the principles of the Athletic Department’s Mission Statement, Core Values, Policies and Procedures, as well as the rules and regulations of the University, the Big 12 Conference and the NCAA. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are As one of 34 U.S. public research institutions belonging to the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU)—and the only member in the Rocky Mountain region—our goal at CU Boulder is to directly affect Colorado communities through collaborative research, innovation and entrepreneurship. Our faculty, staff and students work with the broader community to establish outstanding connections that have lasting outcomes—both across Colorado and around the world. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Sport Coverage: Prevention and care of athletic related injuries for all sports programs. Has primary responsibility for Football including athlete injury prevention and care; athlete records, pre-participation physicals, event and practice coverage, team travel and sport camp coverage. Maintenance of accurate and up to date injury records and daily correspondence with the coaching staff. In conjunction with the rest of the athletic training staff, has responsibility for NCAA mandated coverage of all off-season conditioning sessions. Administration: Oversee and maintain the aquatic therapy area of the sports medicine department. Responsible for the Gatorade and Pepsi ordering for the department. Responsible for the yearly certification of therapeutic modalities. Other duties as assigned by the Head Football Athletic Trainer, and the Associate AD for Health and Performance. Student Intern Program: Assist in the coordination of the recruitment and hiring of sports medicine student interns. Additionally, help with the oversight of the budget for student interns, monitoring their payment for hours worked in Sports Medicine Department. Assist in the supervision of a sports medicine intern program Student education: Act as an instructor in the education and training program for intern athletic trainers. Participate in program planning, teaching, and evaluation of graduate athletic training students. Athletic Camps: Serve as one of the certified athletic trainers for camps per CU camp policy. Oversee student-trainers working summer camps. Department Responsibilities (Continual): Demonstrate a thorough knowledge of NCAA, Big 12 Conference, University and departmental rules and regulations. The violation of NCAA or Conference rules on the part of Athletic Department Personnel may result in disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment. Adhere to principles stated in the Athletic Department’s Mission Statement and promote loyalty to the mission by all staff and student-athletes under their supervision. What You Should Know Requires travel with the football team to all away games and bowl/playoffs, ranging from 5-8 trips a year.  Location of travel are in the US and are determined by the NCAA and the Big 12 Conference. What We Can Offer The starting salary range for this position is $65,000 - $72,000 annually. Relocation assistance is available within department guidelines. Additional Compensation Details: Athletic Event Tickets: Per Athletic Department Policy and NCAA regulations, Nike Elite Employee Benefit: Per Nike Elite Policy. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be active. Be game-changing. Be Boulder. What We Require Bachelor’s degree in health related field, undergraduate collegiate athletic training experience. One year post graduate experience. Equivalent combination of education and experience may substitute. Must be willing to work weekends, evenings and holidays. What You Will Need Knowledge of medical needs, expectations, and requirements of student-athletes to promote, maintain, and enhance their health and well-being. Knowledge of injury prevention, data collection, and reporting and injury prevention programs. Knowledge and support of NCAA rules and regulations. Strong interpersonal skills. Proven ability to effectively work in teams. Strong organization and problem-solving skills. Proven effectiveness in cross-disciplinary collaboration both inside (in other units and functions) and outside the organization. What We Would Like You to Have Master's Degree in Health or Sports Related Field. Two to three years of full-time athletic training experience at the collegiate or professional level. NATABOC Certification. Experience as a professional football Athletic Trainer at a Power 4 NCAA institution or in a NFL setting. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by September 27, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Essential Services This position is designated “essential services.” The incumbent is required to respond to requests for work during campus emergencies (snow, wind, rain, flood and any other natural or man-made event) and report to work for his / her regularly assigned shift during emergency campus closures. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Part-Time-Electrical-Engineer/74996": {
      "title": "Part-Time Electrical Engineer",
      "description": "Part-Time Electrical Engineer Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Part-Time Electrical Engineer Requisition Number: 74996 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 24-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 22-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary LASP's Electrical Engineering Department develops flight and ground electronics for scientific instruments and spacecraft across planetary, lunar, heliophysics, astrophysics, and Earth-science missions. Engineers work across mission classes and collaborate with systems, software, mechanical, thermal, science, quality, parts, radiation, manufacturing, and program-management teams. This is an hourly position where you will be used to augment LASP's full time professional staff's capacity by supporting a variety of our technology development programs including rocket missions, technology demonstrators, and small satellites. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are Founded a decade before NASA, the Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics (LASP) at the University of Colorado Boulder is revolutionizing human understanding of the cosmos. LASP is deeply committed to inspiring and educating the next generation of space explorers. From enabling the first exploratory rocket measurements of Earth’s upper atmosphere to trailblazing observations of every planet in the solar system, LASP is at the forefront of solar, planetary and space physics research, space-weather monitoring, and the search for evidence of habitable worlds. LASP advances scientific discovery and inspires the next generation through research, innovation, and education in space sciences. We employ over 700 scientists, engineers, support personnel, and students that work closely to cultivate creative ideas and explore novel solutions to make the impossible possible. LASP is dedicated to building and maintaining a unique synergism of expertise in space science, engineering, and spacecraft operations. Through research projects, LASP participates actively in the training of the future leaders of space research and helps the University of Colorado to educate students with valued technical and scientific skills. Development and use of innovative technologies and continuing participation in new research initiatives ensures a strong leadership role for LASP into the future. LASP is proud of our work environment that supports employee creativity and productivity and offers opportunities for a broad range of experiences. Further information about LASP can be found here: https://lasp.colorado.edu/home/. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Support scientists and full-time engineering team through the development of next generation space instrument electronics Consult with instrument scientists and engineers to convert measurement objectives into hardware design requirements Develop electronic subsystems for detector readout, power distribution, data processing, and instrument control Perform EEE part selection and derating to meet project environmental requirements Develop and build test GSE Design and develop documentation for the assembly and test of printed wire board assemblies (PWBA) Other duties as assigned. What You Should Know What You Should Know This position will regularly handle export controlled and other CUI level information. Due to the nature of spaceflight hardware development, presence on-site is regularly required during system test and debugging phases What We Can Offer What We Can Offer Hourly Rate: $70 - $91 Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Minimum 20 years experience in space flight and other high reliability electronics design Knowledge of LASP production system process and standards Experience designing electronics for space environments (radiation, thermal, vibration, etc) Expert knowledge of PxWB design techniques including materials, stack ups, and features such as impedance controlled traces and high bandwidth data bus design High proficiency using schematic capture tools like OrCAD or Altium Expert level understanding of electrical subsystem integration, command and control topologies, and space science instrument architectures Experience using typical electrical lab test equipment such as oscilloscopes, function generators, power supplies Due to requirements to access export-controlled data and information, only U.S. citizens, lawful permanent residents (green card holders), or other protected individuals (i.e., persons designated as an asylee, refugee, or temporary resident under amnesty provisions) may apply. What You Will Need What You Will Need Expert level proficiency in either digital or analog electronics design with working apprentice level knowledge of the other Experience designing with BGA and CCGA FPGAs, DDR3/4 memory, and NAND and MRAM storage devices Experience debugging high speed and bi-directional data busses Experience managing subcontractor efforts for PWB fabrication and electronics assembly Motivation to work collaboratively on one or more interdisciplinary teams Willingness to work in laboratory/ ESD controlled settings Experience in assembly and rework of PWBAs and ability to perform high-quality electrical rework independently. A self-starter and self-motivated attitude inspired by exploration and pursuit of new discovery. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by September 24, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Professional-Research-Assistant/74988": {
      "title": "Professional Research Assistant",
      "description": "Professional Research Assistant Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Professional Research Assistant Requisition Number: 74988 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 04-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 22-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Eidem Lab, within the Department of Civil, Environmental and Architectural Engineering, is seeking a Professional Research Assistant. This is a full-time (1.0 FTE), 12-month research faculty appointment, annually renewable, contingent on continued funding and performance. The Eidem Lab studies the respiratory and immune health of workers, focusing on the cannabis industry. Our NIH-funded program characterizes what workers breathe on the job, with a focus on bioaerosols, including airborne dusts, microorganisms, and allergens, and connects those exposures to biological responses in cell and animal models. Our research sits at the intersection of environmental engineering, aerosol science, microbiology, and respiratory immunology, in collaboration with investigators at CU Boulder, the University of Nebraska Medical Center, and CU Anschutz. We are hiring a research staff scientist to be a core member of this program from the ground up. In the first phase, you will work closely with the PI on field campaigns: setting up and running air sampling equipment inside cannabis facilities, collecting real-time indoor air quality data, and collecting airborne microbial and allergen samples, then processing and analyzing those samples in the lab. As the program grows, your role will expand into more molecular biology, cell culture, and animal work, so you will have room to deepen existing skills and build new ones over the life of the award. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The fields of civil, environmental and architectural engineering offer careers in planning, design, construction and management of the built environment as well as in the interaction between the built and natural environment. The Department of Civil, Environmental and Architectural Engineering's mission is the education of undergraduate and graduate students to become leaders in the professional practice of engineering, contributing to technological advances that benefit humankind while enhancing the earth’s physical and biological resources. The Department of Civil, Environmental and Architectural Engineering fosters an environment of inclusivity and celebrates the increasing diversity of our student body. We are committed to fostering inclusive excellence and have adopted priorities to guide our values and practices. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Field Sampling (Cannabis Facilities): Prepare, calibrate, deploy, and retrieve air sampling equipment, including button aerosol samplers with personal pumps, real-time indoor air quality monitors, and bio-aerosol samplers. Collect indoor air quality measurements, including microbial and allergen samples, during facility visits. Maintain indoor air quality datasets and sample repositories. Maintain field records, sample logs, and equipment in good working order, and troubleshoot equipment issues in the field. Coordinate visit logistics with facility contacts and the PI, and follow all site safety and PPE requirements, including respirator use as needed. Laboratory Analysis: Process environmental samples: allergen extraction, culturing and enumeration of environmental microorganisms, DNA extraction, qPCR, sequencing, and immunoassays. As the program develops, contribute to mammalian cell culture work, and following training and IACUC approval, to mouse exposure and airway studies. Prepare reagents and media, maintain aseptic technique, and operate and maintain instrumentation. Data and Lab Operations: Record data accurately and completely in lab notebooks (physical and electronic) and project databases. Maintain lab inventory and ordering, chemical and biological safety records, and equipment maintenance schedules. Support onboarding of graduate and undergraduate researchers as the group grows. Contribute to lab safety compliance, including biosafety (BSL-2), chemical hygiene, and IACUC requirements. Program Contributions: Participate in lab meetings, collaborator calls, and data discussions. Contribute to figures, methods sections, and protocols for manuscripts and reports. Present work at internal meetings and, where appropriate, at conferences. What You Should Know Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Relocation reimbursement is not available for this position. What We Can Offer The salary range for this position is $50,000 - $60,000 annually. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be resourceful. Be game-changing. Be Boulder. What We Require Bachelor's degree in molecular biology, microbiology, cell biology, biochemistry, environmental health, or a related field. 2+ years of hands-on laboratory experience in molecular biology, microbiology, or cell culture, in an academic, industry, or government setting. Research experience during a degree program counts. Demonstrated proficiency with standard molecular and microbiological methods, for example nucleic acid extraction, qPCR, aseptic technique, and culturing. Careful, organized record-keeping and comfort with electronic data tools such as spreadsheets, lab notebooks, and basic databases. Willingness and ability to travel to cannabis cultivation and processing facilities, work in environments with elevated organic dust and allergen levels, wear a respirator and other PPE as needed, and lift and carry field equipment up to approximately 40 pounds. Ability to obtain and maintain a Colorado Marijuana Enforcement Division (MED) occupational license, which requires passing a State of Colorado MED background check. This license is required for entry into and work within licensed cannabis facilities. A current license is not required to apply; the successful candidate will apply for one after hire, with support from the lab. This is expected to be completed within 10 weeks of the hire date. Valid driver's license and ability to drive to field sites. What You Will Need We are looking for someone with genuine curiosity about how things work, the grit to keep going when an experiment or a field day does not cooperate, and a drive to get it done. You take ownership of your work, ask good questions, and follow through. You are comfortable moving between a cannabis grow room and a tissue culture hood in the same week, and you are excited to build something new with an emerging research team. What We Would Like You to Have Experience with air sampling, aerosol science, or occupational or environmental exposure assessment. Experience with immunoassays, flow cytometry, or multiplex platforms such as Luminex. Experience with mammalian cell culture and, ideally, rodent handling or IACUC-regulated work. Familiarity with fungal or bacterial culturing and related molecular work, endotoxin or glucan assays, or allergen quantification. Basic data analysis and scripting skills (Excel, R, Python, or similar). Experience or interest in the cannabis industry, agricultural health, or worker health and safety. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume/CV Cover Letter (Optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree : If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor. However, if your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit an English-translated version as an Optional document. During the application process, you will be asked to provide the names and email addresses for three references who would be willing to provide letters of recommendation on your behalf. We may request these letters directly from your references later in the search process - you will not be asked to provide letters at the time of application. For consideration, please apply by October 4, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/CIRES-GSL-Temporary-Researcher-Deep-Cumulus-Parameterization-Developer/74968": {
      "title": "CIRES/ GSL Temporary Researcher, Deep Cumulus Parameterization Developer",
      "description": "CIRES/ GSL Temporary Researcher, Deep Cumulus Parameterization Developer Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail CIRES/ GSL Temporary Researcher, Deep Cumulus Parameterization Developer Requisition Number: 74968 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 24-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 22-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Cooperative Institute for Research in Environmental Sciences (CIRES) at the University of Colorado Boulder welcomes applications for a Temporary Researcher, Deep Cumulus Parameterization Developer. This role is needed to refine and translate developments from the Grell-Freitas-Li deep cumulus parameterization to the next-generation Community Convective Cloud (C3) parameterization to support the “Improving Numerical Weather Prediction through Model Physics Development” task within the 2026-27 CIESRDS-GSL Science Plan, specifically the objective to “Improve physics parameterizations and their interconnectivity for future NOAA operational modeling systems.” This appointment is for no more than 0.25 FTE (~10 hours per week) for 12 months. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are At CIRES , the Cooperative Institute for Research In Environmental Sciences, more than 900 people work to understand the dynamic Earth system, including people’s relationship with the planet. An institute at the University of Colorado Boulder, we have partnered with the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) since 1967. Our areas of expertise include weather and climate, wildfire and water, changes at Earth’s poles, air quality and atmospheric chemistry, water resources, solid Earth sciences, and more. Our researchers work in 10 CU Boulder departments and eight NOAA laboratories and centers. GSL is one of ten NOAA Research Laboratories and is located in Boulder, Colorado. Our research improves environmental prediction models, develops state-of-the-science decision support tools, visualization systems, and uses high-performance computing technology and artificial intelligence research to support a Weather-Ready, Fire-Ready Nation. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Further develop, test, and refine a novel physically-based scale-awareness representation for deep cumulus parameterizations and implement it within the community-governed Community Convective Cloud (C3) parameterization (55%). Further develop, test, and refine the representation of atmospheric aerosols for the C3 parameterization (15%). Prepare briefing materials and presentations for branch management, internal meetings, conferences, and workshops, and prepare and submit a scientific manuscript for eventual publication on the work completed (15%). Contributes code developments to the C3 parameterization Github repository, working with other C3 parameterization developers to ensure that all contributions are designed and implemented in a mutually agreeable fashion (10%). Other duties related to maintaining and debugging existing deep cumulus parameterization codes used in NOAA operational models as assigned (5%) What You Should Know This appointment is not eligible for vacation leave, holidays, annual merit pay increases, nor employee health, retirement, or related benefits, except those required by law. This appointment will accrue 0.034 hours of sick leave for every hour worked. We expect this position to be at approximately 10 hours per week. This is an hourly paid position with timesheets required. The hours for this position may vary and are not guaranteed. This temporary position cannot extend past a maximum of 12 months. What We Can Offer The hourly rate for this position ranges from $55.88 - $74.04 per hour. Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Benefits Temporary positions at the University of Colorado are not benefits-eligible, however, all positions are eligible for paid sick leave . Be Statements Be courageous. Be proactive. Be Boulder. What We Require A Ph.D. in Atmospheric Science or a related field plus at least twenty years of experience developing, testing, and evaluating deep cumulus parameterizations. What You Will Need Ability to effectively collaborate with other developers of the community-governed deep cumulus parameterization to ensure that innovations are effectively and efficiently integrated into the shared code base. Ability to work as part of a team of diverse scientists, researchers, developers, engineers, and staff in a research environment. What We Would Like You to Have Familiarity with Github, particularly including using it to conduct and manage code development within a shared code base. Experience with community modeling approaches for numerical weather prediction, including developing physics parameterizations in an interoperable way such that they can be used with multiple host models. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume/CV. If you are identified as a finalist, the search committee will request one letter of recommendation at a later time. If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources department using an approved online vendor. If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. Please apply by September 24, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Visiting-Researcher/72480": {
      "title": "Visiting Researcher",
      "description": "Visiting Researcher Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Visiting Researcher Requisition Number: 72480 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 31-May-2027 Date Posted: 22-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Bruce D. Benson Center for the Study of Western Civilization (the Benson Center) and the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Colorado Boulder invite applications for a Visiting Researcher (Sabbatical Fellow) for the 2026-27 and 2027–28 academic year. We seek scholars from across the humanities and social sciences whose work engages the ideas, institutions, and debates that have shaped Western civilization and the American founding. Scholars with established records of publication, as well as scholars who have recently received their doctorates, are invited to apply. The Visiting Researcher will have access to a private office in the Benson Center and is expected to be in residence at the University of Colorado Boulder to fulfill responsibilities relating to in-person participation. Applicants for spring 2028 and any portion of the academic year 2026-27, 2027–28 and/or 2028–29 will be considered. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The mission of the Benson Center is to foster scholarly excellence in the study of the Western tradition, its culture, languages, and ideas, ranging from antiquity to modernity. The Center is especially concerned with the values that emerge from that tradition and their ongoing role in American life. Integral to this mission is the Center's commitment to rigorous research, open debate, and sustained dialogue on enduring questions. The Center seeks to enrich the intellectual life of the University of Colorado Boulder by fostering viewpoint diversity, engaging the full range of political, economic, and philosophical perspectives, and cultivating the habits of mind essential for informed citizenship and civic leadership. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be The Visiting Researcher is expected to pursue original research and writing that engages with the Benson Center's mission to advance understanding of the ideas, institutions, and interactions that have shaped Western civilization. Specific duties include conducting original research and participating in the various activities of the Center. There may also be opportunities to teach undergraduate courses, depending on interest and availability. What You Should Know The successful candidate will receive an appointment, without tenure, in the College of Arts & Sciences The candidate is expected to be in residence at the University of Colorado Boulder for the length of the appointment to fulfill in-person responsibilities and duties What We Can Offer Applicants' home institutions are expected to provide at least half of their academic-year salary. Candidates with partial support may apply for matching funds from the Center. If no external funding is available, the Benson Center will provide a monthly salary of $6,000 - $10,000, depending on experience The compensation package is competitive, including relocation subsidy, dedicated budget, and administrative assistance Signing bonus and incentive pay may be available, depending on the candidate's experience and the Center's funding Benefits are available for 50% time or greater appointments, including medical, dental, and life insurance, as well as tax-deferred annuity and optional savings plans, e.g., 403(b), 457, 401(k). An Eco Pass (for the bus system) will also be provided Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be creative. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require Candidates are expected to have a Ph.D. at the time of appointment or a doctoral degree in progress with a dissertation defense date scheduled. It is expected that candidates are not employed by another institution during their time serving as a Visiting Researcher. Fellows are expected to be regularly present and in-person at the Benson Center to contribute to its intellectual life. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume/CV Cover Letter describing your research agenda and how your work engages the Benson Center's mission (up to three pages). Please indicate your preferred timeline, e.g., spring 2028, or any portion of AY 2027-28 or AY 2028-29, in your application materials Executive Summary of Proposed Project (up to 200 words) Statement of Financial Support indicating the level of financial support needed and any support available from the applicant's home institution During the application process, you will need to enter contact information for three references who will be asked to provide a letter of recommendation as the search progresses. Application review will begin immediately, position will remain open until May 31, 2027. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email.  For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Utility-Plant-Electrician/75025": {
      "title": "Utility Plant Electrician",
      "description": "Utility Plant Electrician Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Utility Plant Electrician Requisition Number: 75025 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 05-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 21-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary Utility and Energy Services at CU Boulder encourages applications for a Utility Plant Electrician! The primary focus is the installation, maintenance, inspection, troubleshooting, and repair of electrical equipment, controls, metering, and related systems. Work is performed with a strong emphasis on safety, code compliance, and minimizing downtime. The position also supports the campus wide utility metering system, I&C, and Medium Voltage tasks. Candidates should have general knowledge of power distribution equipment, motors, Variable Speed/Frequency Drives (VSD, VFD), electrical metering, totalizers, and pneumatic devices, and a working knowledge of electrical diagrams and circuits, and familiarity with NEC. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are Utility and Energy Services is responsible for the design, operation, maintenance, and repair of the campus’ energy generation and distribution infrastructure for steam (heating), chilled water (cooling), and electricity. We provide the campus community with reliable utility services in an efficient and environmentally responsible manner and offer measurement, line locating, performance engineering, regulatory, and data management services. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Troubleshoot, maintain, repair, and install electrical equipment and devices within district energy plants. This includes VFD’s, Motor starters, UPS systems, Electrical distribution systems and a variety of industrial control and metering devices. Complete small projects including the installation of RMC and EMT conduit within plants and campus buildings. Troubleshoot and diagnose issues specific to plant automation controls used in a utility plant environment. Perform regular maintenance and inspections to ensure optimal performance and minimize downtime. Inspect contractor installed equipment and ensure work follows national and local electrical codes, safety regulations, and company/campus standards. Work with other groups and departments on campus to generate LOTO’s and safety procedures, troubleshoot electrical metering, coordinate outages, respond to customer demands and supervise contractors. Collaborate with plant operations and maintenance teams to identify electrical needs and provide recommendations for improving plant performance and safety. Communicate with supervision and coworkers on system needs and assignments, keeping management informed. Maintain documentation of plant repairs and PM’s. Schedule and coordinate work tasks, ordering parts and material, analyzing trends and data, reviewing drawings and schematics. What You Should Know What You Should Know Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. This role is designated as essential services and must be on the after hour response list in order to respond to campus emergencies. This position is eligible for our Facilities Management Employee Referral Program . What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The salary for this role is $85,000 – $92,000 annually Relocation assistance is available per department guidelines. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be empowered. Be proactive. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require 2 years of experience of electrical maintenance in a plant/industrial environment. 5 years of general electrical experience or a combination of relevant education or relevant experience equal to 5 years. Ability to run RMC and EMT raceways with minimal supervision. Willingness to work on and around voltages ranging from 24V-15KV. Must be willing to respond to plant and campus emergencies/outages. Must have a valid driver’s license. What You Will Need What You Will Need Ability to work successfully independently and as part of a team. Ability to read and interpret complex electrical schematics and blueprints related to power distribution systems, plant control systems and supporting infrastructure. Strong knowledge of electrical theory. Proficient in using basic hand and power tools. Communicate effectively and build relationships. Proven problem-solving, critical thinking, and decision-making skills. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Valid Journeyman Electrical License (CO or reciprocal state). Understanding of process controls. Knowledge and experience in safe handling practices of flammable gases, liquids, and high-pressure systems. Knowledge and experience with high pressure gas and liquids, processing equipment, medium voltage equipment, pumps, gas turbine engines and compressors. Knowledge or prior experience with boilers, chillers and associated equipment. Experience with AC and/or DC controls. Experience with instrumentation devices. Experience with diagnostic electrical test equipment. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. Optional : A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 5, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Essential Services Essential Services This position is designated “essential services.” The incumbent is required to respond to requests for work during campus emergencies (snow, wind, rain, flood and any other natural or man-made event) and report to work for his / her regularly assigned shift during emergency campus closures. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Artist-in-Residence/75010": {
      "title": "Artist in Residence",
      "description": "Artist in Residence Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Artist in Residence Requisition Number: 75010 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 09-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 21-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary The Creative Writing Program in the Department of English at the University of Colorado Boulder invites applications for two non-renewable 3-year term visiting Assistant Professors, one for Fiction and one for Poetry. The anticipated start for these positions is Spring Semester 2027. The MFA program in Creative Writing at CU Boulder is a three-year degree program that emphasizes literary study and innovation. Our prolific faculty value interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary approaches to writing that expand the limits of traditional practice and conventional forms. The program also offers an undergraduate major track for English majors and a minor for non-majors. Creative Writing and English are in one of CU Boulder's most historic buildings, home to the Mary Rippon Theatre, the primary outdoor venue for the annual Shakespeare Festival. Faculty and students enjoy proximity to the Denver Metro area and a rich landscape of Front Range writers, artists, and adjacent literary communities. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are The College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Colorado Boulder is the intellectual core of the university, conducting research, scholarship, creative work, and education in more than 60 fields. Our research generates new knowledge, solving some of the world’s most critical problems. The college leads nationally in undergraduate and graduate education in the natural sciences, social sciences and arts and humanities, bringing world-class research and scholarship to our students, both in and out of the classroom. Among the most diverse and challenging programs in the country, the English Department at CU Boulder is an inclusive culture that welcomes all, and is committed to providing essential skills in language, writing, research, close reading, and critical thinking across multiple genres, applicable to a multitude of career fields, while allowing students to study what they love. Our priority is to cultivate empathetic and responsible engagement with texts, peers, and community on a variety of scales, from local to international in a changing world to solve longstanding and emerging problems. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be 2/2 teaching load (two courses per semester; two semesters per academic year) at both undergraduate and graduate level. Working with MFA students on thesis projects. Normal service obligations for creative writing faculty. Maintain a strong record of research and creative work. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The salary for this position is $100,000. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be creative. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require MFA and/or Ph.D., or equivalent terminal graduate degree, in-hand by start date of appointment. What You Will Need What You Will Need Successful candidates will have a strong record of publication (at least two books published and/or under contract in primary genre). Candidates should present a strong record of teaching. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Additional qualifications include secondary genre writing and publication in a second genre with preference given to those with experience and interest in small press publishing, conference organizing, creative leadership, and/or community building. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume/CV Cover Letter PDF Sample of Work; a writing sample no more than 20 pages in length During the application process you will need to enter contact information for three references who will be contacted to provide letters of recommendation immediately after you apply. For consideration, please apply by October 9, 2026. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Executive-Assistant-to-the-Institute-Director/75007": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to the Institute Director",
      "description": "Executive Assistant to the Institute Director Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Executive Assistant to the Institute Director Requisition Number: 75007 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 27-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 21-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary This position provides a unique opportunity to work closely with the Institute Director and Deputy Director and gain broad exposure to leadership, strategy, and organizational decision-making. The Executive Assistant serves as a trusted associate to the Institute Director, helping manage the flow of information, relationships, commitments, and priorities through excellent management of the Director’s calendar, email, travel, meetings, and follow-up. The Executive Assistant helps the Director focus attention where it matters most and ensures that important commitments move forward. Additionally, the Executive Assistant helps to manage special projects and initiatives. We are looking for someone exceptionally capable, organized and responsive who is also intellectually curious, entrepreneurial, highly motivated, ambitious, and eager to learn. The ideal candidate gets satisfaction both from making complex systems work exceptionally well and from tackling new problems with increasing independence. The position serves as a primary point of contact on behalf of Institute leadership, requiring excellent judgment, professionalism, discretion, and strong interpersonal skills in representing the Director and Deputy Director to internal and external partners. The position will sit within the Data and Systems team and will have management and supervisory responsibilities. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The Crown Institute’s research focuses on practices, programs, and policies that foster wellness among children and youth, families and caregivers, educators, and mental health and health providers. Scholars in multiple fields work together and in partnership with families, teachers, young people, and community members. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Administrative and Communication Assistance Managing and prioritizing information and task flow; taking minutes during meetings and sending follow-up as needed; handling Advisory Board and other confidential materials; working closely with other Crown Institute teams including advancement, communications, education, and finance; organizing and maintaining electronic and paper records to ensure information is updated and easily accessible; coordinating travel; processing travel reimbursements and Purchasing Card and Travel Card transactions; working with printers and designers for production and distribution of materials including PowerPoint presentations, printed and digital assets; providing any support for events that directly impacts the Institute Director. Serving as the main point of contact for the Institute Director, communicating in response to requests for scheduling and prioritizing and managing scheduling, managing correspondence with internal team members and external partners; representing the Director with team members and external partners; working directly with Crown Institute Advisory Board, Executive Committee, Faculty Fellows, and Advancement team to support the Institute Director and Deputy Director with planning, completing, and documenting critical decisions, activities, and relationships necessary for the success of the Crown Institute. Project Management of Special Projects and Strategic Initiatives Supporting a wide range of special projects and strategic initiatives as identified or initiated by the Institute Director or Deputy Director, ensuring clear and concise communications with internal team members and external partners, performing with excellent project management skills, establishing and monitoring Asana project management boards and task completion including maintaining a system to ensure that all key deliverables that impact the Director are on track; providing regular updates and reports to the Director as needed, and other general support duties. Supervision Supervising an administrative support position, including hiring, onboarding, training, assigning and reviewing work, conducting performance evaluations, and supporting employee development; plan, prioritize, and delegate administrative work to ensure efficient office operations and appropriate workload distribution; provide ongoing coaching, guidance, and performance feedback in accordance with university policies. The position may require national and international travel with the Director. What You Should Know This role will primarily be onsite, with flexibility to work remotely when there are no requirements to be on campus. What We Can Offer Starting salary range is $70,000-85,000. Relocation assistance is available within department guidelines. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require Bachelor's degree or equivalent combination of education or experience. Three+ years’ experience supporting executive, managerial, or operational function. Experience in Asana or other project management software. Equivalent combination of education and experience may substitute. What You Will Need Strong organizational and project coordination skills with high attention to detail. Ability to manage and maintain structured data systems (e.g., tracking databases, SharePoint, Excel). Experience with Microsoft Office Suite (Outlook, Word, Excel, PowerPoint). Strong written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently and manage multiple priorities. Demonstrated ability to handle confidential information with discretion. Customer service orientation and professional interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to learn new systems and too. What We Would Like You to Have 3+ years’ experience supporting executive-level leadership. Experience managing agreements, contracts, or partnership records. Complex organizations. Higher ed - nice to have, fast paced, high intensity organization. Experience working in higher education or a large, complex organization. Experience in Concur. Experience and knowledge of FRPA reporting system. Supervisory/managerial or demonstrated leadership experience. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by 9/27/26 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Postdoctoral-Associate/74990": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Postdoctoral Associate Requisition Number: 74990 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 20-Nov-2026 Date Posted: 21-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Department of Physics is seeking a Postdoctoral Associate to work in quantum condensed matter theory and many body quantum information, broadly defined. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are College of Arts and Sciences - The College is the intellectual core of the university, conducting research, scholarship, creative work and education in more than 60 fields. Our research generates new knowledge, addressed toward solving some of the world’s most critical problems. The college leads nationally in undergraduate and graduate education, bringing world-class research and scholarship to our students, both in and out of the classroom. The college is the academic home to all five of the university’s Nobel laureates. Additionally, it is home to the university’s four National Medal of Science winners, nine MacArthur \"Genius Award\" winners, 100 Fulbright Scholars, 17 Guggenheim Fellows, 14 Packard Fellows, 24 members of the National Academy of Sciences, and 25 members of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences. CU Boulder is the only university in the Rocky Mountain region in the American Association of Universities, a group of the nation’s leading research institutions. Department of Physics - The Department includes 54 tenured and tenure-track faculty performing cutting-edge research. Its graduate physics program draws ~1200 applications per year and is ranked by the National Research Council to be among the top 15 overall. The Department appears as high as 12th worldwide in international rankings of university Physics Departments. The department is strongly affiliated with centers and institutes such as JILA, a research institute administered jointly by NIST and the University of Colorado, and the Center for Theory of Quantum Matter, as well as with nearby National Laboratories (NIST and NREL), offering world-class facilities and broad opportunities for research collaboration. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be The candidate will carry out cutting edge research in condensed matter theory and many body quantum information theory, in collaboration with the Boulder many body quantum theory faculty. What You Should Know The position is expected to start in July 2027. What We Can Offer Annual salary range: $60,000 to $70,000 per year Moving reimbursement for up to $3,000 Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require PhD in physics expected by start date (subtopic: condensed matter theory, quantum information theory, statistical physics or similar). Strong research record and evidence of creativity and independence. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. Statement describing research related goals If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources department using an approved online vendor. If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process you will need to enter contact information for three references who will be contacted immediately at the time of application to provide letters of reference. For full consideration, please apply by November 15, 2026. The posting will remain open until November 20, 2026. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Innovations-in-Jewish-Life-Archivist/74952": {
      "title": "Innovations in Jewish Life Archivist",
      "description": "Innovations in Jewish Life Archivist Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Innovations in Jewish Life Archivist Requisition Number: 74952 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 11-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 21-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) Libraries is excited to invite applications from collaborative and creative candidates for the position of Innovations in Jewish Life Archivist! The Innovations in Jewish Life Archivist is a two year, limited-term teaching faculty position that will manage the University Libraries’ unique Innovations in Jewish Life (IJL) archival collections, which document the work of individuals and groups who transformed the American Jewish experience from post-World War II to the present. The IJL collections are co-stewarded by both the Libraries and CU’s Program in Jewish Studies (JWST).  The position’s primary responsibilities include supporting collection development of the IJL collections, including appraisal, acquisition, and accessioning; leading Libraries instruction sessions with these materials for classes across campus units; participating in related ongoing grant-funded activities co-led by the Libraries and JWST; supporting Libraries and JWST fellowship programs and other opportunities for outreach and engagement with the IJL collections; and delivering reference services and student support for these collections. In close collaboration with other archivists in the University Libraries, the Innovations in Jewish Life Archivist will also process, arrange, and describe IJL archival collections, supervise student employees, interns, apprentices, and/or volunteers to assist with this work, and participate in weekly service point shifts in the Reading Room in Norlin Library. This position is 100% librarianship; research and service duties are not required by this role. The Innovations in Jewish Life Archivist has the opportunity to work in a hybrid capacity and reports to the Head of the Distinctive Collections & Institutional Memory Unit. The Distinctive Collections & Institutional Memory Unit is part of the Collection and Archival Strategy Department which is part of the Research and Innovation Strategies Division. Please see the Libraries’ organizational chart. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are Mission We are a knowledge catalyst. We fuel inquiry, discovery, collaboration, creation, and the dissemination of knowledge. We welcome everyone, wherever they are on their educational journey. Vision An information-empowered world. Values Include • Empower • Connect • Learn • Inspire Our values define our interactions within the Libraries and radiate outwardly. This means that we develop a culture that compassionately and authentically embodies our values in all aspects of our work. The University Libraries encourage applications from people of all backgrounds. We are most interested in finding the best candidate for the job and that person may be one who comes from a less traditional background. As you review the qualifications, please think about how your qualifications are transferable if (at first) they do not seem directly related. Share how your skills translate in your cover letter. We encourage you to apply, even if you don’t meet every preferred qualification. About the University, Libraries and Archives, and Program in Jewish Studies: As the flagship University of a multi-campus system in the State of Colorado, CU Boulder is a dynamic community of scholars and learners situated on one of the most beautiful college campuses in the country. As one of 35 U.S. public institutions belonging to the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU) we have a proud tradition of academic excellence, with five Nobel laureates and more than 50 members of prestigious academic academies. CU Boulder is committed to Colorado communities through collaborative research, innovation and entrepreneurship. Our faculty, staff and students work with the broader community to establish unique connections that have lasting outcomes—both across Colorado and around the world. The CU Systemwide Lands Recognition Statement, arrived at after connecting with Native American groups on all CU campuses, as well as with campus chief diversity officers, chancellors and system leadership, is available at https://president.cu.edu/statements/cu-systemwide-lands-recognition-statement. The University Libraries faculty and staff are diverse, dedicated, and hardworking people who share this vision and mission. Our organization employs professionals of various academic tracks and ranks as well as university and classified staff, and all work is valued and appreciated regardless of position type. The Libraries are a member of the Association of Research Libraries and hold the largest collection in the Rocky Mountain region. The Libraries faculty and staff collaborate internally, with colleagues beyond the campus and with our clients to advance information literacy, intellectual freedom, lifelong learning, the disciplinary growth of information, and the amplification of knowledge. The University Libraries maintains a formal partnership with the CU’s Program in Jewish Studies to manage the Innovations in Jewish Life Collections. The Program in Jewish Studies explores Jewish culture, history, society and thought from a broad, interdisciplinary perspective. Program faculty include an Academic Director of the Innovation in Jewish Life Collections who is appointed by the Director of Jewish Studies and advises the Libraries on collection development and engagement with these archives. The University of Colorado Boulder Libraries’ Archives was founded in 1918 and holds more than 1,800 collections and 40,000 linear feet of materials. This includes manuscripts, correspondence, diaries, photographs, posters, sound recordings, and moving images. Current collecting foci includes the Atomic West, Innovations in Jewish Life, American popular music, experimental film and video, Rocky Mountain counterculture and social change, and the history and records of University of Colorado Boulder campus and its faculty and students. About Boulder, Colorado Boulder and the surrounding area are known as one of the best places to live because of the spectacular setting, acres of open space, and vibrant atmosphere. At 5,430 feet above sea level, acres of vast open space entwine into Boulder's cityscape nestled into the foothills of the Rocky Mountains. Home to approximately 90,000 residents, Boulder has a mild, dry climate with more than 300 days of sunshine per year. Boulder offers activities for every interest. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Collaborates with CU Boulder’s Program in Jewish Studies (JWST) and the Senior Collections Archivist to appraise, acquire, and accession new archival collections documenting Innovations in Jewish Life (JL), following the Libraries’ collection development policies; Offers primary source instruction in the Libraries for classes using IJL archival collections, in collaboration with other Libraries personnel engaged in instruction and with other faculty as appropriate; Participates in and supports ongoing grant-funded activities related to the IJL collections, in collaboration with the Senior Collections Archivist and JWST; Supports and delivers opportunities for student, faculty, staff, and community engagement with the IJL collections through outreach events, exhibits, fellowship programs, and/or curation of collection materials for digitization and inclusion in the CU Digital Library, in collaboration with JWST and/or other Libraries personnel; Provides reference and student support for the IJL collections by holding research consultations, supporting JWST and Libraries research fellows with research assistance, creating LibGuides, and assisting the Reference Archivist with other patron reference needs; and Researches, processes, re-houses, arranges, and describes new acquisitions and legacy archival materials in the Innovations in Jewish Life (IJL) collections, including creating finding aids in ArchivesSpace, in accordance with institutional policies and professional best practices. Participates in weekly service point shifts in the Reading Room and other Libraries’ service points as assigned. What You Should Know What You Should Know This position is offered in a hybrid capacity. There will be an information session on Monday, October 5, 2026 from 11:00-11:45 am MT. At this information session, the search committee will share information about the Innovations in Jewish Life Archivist job search, working at CU Boulder, and answer attendee questions. A webinar link will be provided to registrants via email. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The successful candidate will be appointed as a full-time (12-month) teaching professor-track faculty member at the Assistant Teaching Professor rank. The salary range for Assistant Teaching Professor is $75,000-$90,000. A candidate with seven or more years of professional experience may be eligible for the rank of Associate Teaching Professor with a starting salary range of $83,000-$98,000. Limited relocation assistance may be available within University Libraries guidelines. Faculty receive limited professional development funds annually. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be creative. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require ALA-accredited Master’s degree in Library and Information Science, Archival Studies, or Public History with a focus or concentration in archives management; Minimum of 1 year of experience working with archival collections; Demonstrated initiative and flexibility to work both independently and collaboratively; Demonstrated ability to develop partnerships and collaborate with and receive input from others while supporting a welcoming and inclusive environment; Experience applying professional best practices to the arrangement and description of archival collections, including using ArchivesSpace to create finding aids. What You Will Need What You Will Need Experience supporting or leading collection development activities for archival collections; Experience supporting or leading instruction, outreach, and/or engagement opportunities for archival collections; Experience delivering reference services for archival collections. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Experience conducting appraisal and accessioning of acquisitions of archival collections; Experience supporting relationships with donors of archival collections; Experience identifying and resolving preservation concerns for archival collections; Degree, coursework, or related professional experience in Religious Studies or Jewish Studies; Proficiency in Hebrew and/or Yiddish. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A cover letter of application specifically addressing qualifications for the position CV/Resume References will be requested at a later stage in the search process. Review of applications will begin immediately. Applications must be submitted by 11:59 pm on October 11, 2026, to receive full consideration. Questions may be directed to George Karpoff, Search Coordinator, at George.Karpoff@Colorado.edu. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: George Karpoff Posting Contact Email: george.karpoff@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Postdoctoral-Associate-or-Research-Associate/74948": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate Requisition Number: 74948 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 05-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 21-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary This work aims to develop advanced quantum communication protocols for optical quantum networks.  To this end, we are exploring single-rail quantum networks that can operate with a provable quantum advantage.  Careful modeling of network architectures is required, as well as developing tools for characterizing and benchmarking the performance of different quantum communications protocols that rely on entanglement.  The position requires working with both theory and experimental teams to develop quantum communication protocols that can be rapidly deployed and tested.  The project scope is limited to fundamental research, and results will be available in the public domain through journal articles and conference presentations. This position is part of the National Institute of Standards and Technology's (NIST) Professional Research Experience Program (PREP).  NIST recognizes that its research staff may wish to collaborate with researchers at academic institutions on specific projects of mutual interest, thus requires that such institutions must be the recipient of a PREP award.  The PREP program requires staff from a wide range of backgrounds to work on scientific research in many areas.  Employees in this position will perform technical work that underpins the scientific research of the collaboration. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are PREP is a special partnership between the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) and the University of Colorado Boulder.  PREP provides research opportunities to CU undergraduate and graduate students, as well as researchers with a Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD in NIST labs to gain research experience alongside NIST scientists. The particular group that needs a researcher is the Quantum Nanophotonics Group at NIST. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Develop and implement metrology for measuring and characterizing path-entanglement distributed over public fiber. Coordinate with entangled sources team to develop device-independent quantum communications protocols. Model multi-node quantum network layouts to develop optimal network topographies. Present results at internal meetings, and occasional meetings with external stakeholders. Ensuring that results, protocols, software, and documentation have been archived or otherwise transmitted to the larger organization. What You Should Know This position requires the successful completion of an NAIS agreement and federal security clearance as mandated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).  Employment is contingent upon approval of these requirements as determined by NIST. Anticipated start date is December 27, 2026.  Anticipated end date is March 30, 2028. Privacy Act Statement Authority: 15 U.S.C. § 278g-1(e)(1) and (e)(3) and 15 U.S.C. § 272(b) and (c) Purpose: The National Institute for Standards and Technology (NIST) hosts the Professional Research Experience Program (PREP) which is designed to provide valuable laboratory experience and financial assistance to undergraduates, post-bachelor’s degree holders, graduate students, master’s degree holders, postdocs, and faculty. PREP is a 5-year cooperative agreement between NIST laboratories and participating PREP Universities to establish a collaborative research relationship between NIST and U.S. institutions of higher education in the following disciplines including (but may not be limited to) biochemistry, biological sciences, chemistry, computer science, engineering, electronics, materials science, mathematics, nanoscale science, neutron science, physical science, physics, and statistics. This collection of information is needed to facilitate administrative functions of the PREP Program. Routine Uses: NIST will use the information collected to perform the requisite reviews of the applications to determine eligibility, and to meet programmatic requirements.  Disclosure of this information is also subject to all the published routine uses as identified in the Privacy Act System of Records Notices: NIST-1: NIST Associates. Disclosure: Furnishing this information is voluntary. When you submit the form, you are indicating your voluntary consent for NIST to use of the information you submit for the purpose stated. What We Can Offer The salary range for the full-time Postdoctoral Associate position is $74,000 - $108,000 annually. The salary range for the full-time Research Associate position is $78,000 - $135,000 annually. Onboarding/relocation assistance may be available, however If within a 12-month period, you exercise your right to willfully terminate your employment with the University of Colorado, you hereby agree to reimburse the department for your relocation expense. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Postdoctoral Associate : A PhD in Electrical Engineering, Physics, or a related field with 0 – 4 years of post-doctoral degree experience. Research Associate : A PhD in Electrical Engineering, Physics, or a related field with 5+ years of post-doctoral degree experience. What We Would Like You to Have Two or more years of relevant experience with device-independent quantum protocols. Experience with statistical methods for analyzing Bell tests. Knowledge of quantum optics theory. Familiarity with Python, Matlab, or similar programming language. Strong oral and written communication skills, including a strong record of peer-reviewed publications. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor.  If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process, you will need to enter contact information for a reference.  We will request the letter of recommendation immediately following your application. For full consideration, please apply by October 5, 2026. Note:  Application materials will not be accepted via email.  For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Lisa Valencia Posting Contact Email: recruitprep@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Assistant-Director-of-Advising-and-IUT/74925": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Advising and IUT",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Advising and IUT Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Assistant Director of Advising and IUT Requisition Number: 74925 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 05-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 21-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The College of Communication, Media, Design and Information (CMDI) at CU Boulder encourages applications for an Assistant Director of Advising and IUT! CMDI is at the forefront of innovation in communication, digital technology, media, and design. CMDI offers an interdisciplinary education grounded in creativity, collaboration, and emerging technologies. Across seven academic departments and an independent PhD program, students, staff, and faculty work across fields to address complex challenges and shape culture in meaningful ways. CMDI is committed to fostering a diverse, equitable, and inclusive community and seeks candidates who demonstrate a dedication to supporting historically underrepresented populations in higher education. The CMDI Student Success & Advising unit advances student learning, persistence, engagement, and graduation through well-rounded, developmental advising and student success initiatives, and career readiness programming. The Assistant Director of Advising and Intra-University Transfer (IUT) serves as a key member of the CMDI Advising Leadership Team and reports to the Director of Academic Advising and Student Success. This position provides leadership for the Intra-University Transfer program into CMDI and for CMDI’s academic minors. This role collaborates closely with academic departments, faculty, staff, and campus partners to support student success and strengthen the student experience from matriculation through graduation. This position directly supervises professional advising staff and is responsible for the strategic leadership, assessment, and continuous improvement of the college’s IUT and minor programs. The Assistant Director develops and implements initiatives that support enrollment management, student retention, academic success, and operational effectiveness across CMDI. This role also supports the operationalization of unit strategic goals and aligns advising initiatives with college and campus-wide priorities established by the CMDI Assistant Dean of Student Success. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The College of Communication, Media, Design and Information (CMDI) is at the forefront of the revolution in communication, digital technology, and design. CMDI prides itself on offering students an interdisciplinary education with a focus on innovation and creativity! Our students and faculty from seven departments think across boundaries, innovate around emerging problems and create culture that transcends convention. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Academic Operations and Program Management IUT Program Management Under the direction of the Director of Academic Advising and Student Success, provide strategic leadership and oversight for CMDI’s IUT program, including processing, policy implementation, enrollment planning, assessment, and operational management for the summer, fall and spring cycles to ensure accuracy, consistency, and compliance with university and college policies. Develop, evaluate, and recommend updates to CMDI IUT procedures, and admission criteria in partnership with college leadership to support enrollment goals and student success outcomes. Serve as the primary resource and subject-matter expert on CMDI IUT policies for campus partners, including the Office of the Registrar and the campus-wide student success community. Act as the lead advisor and key point of contact for prospective CMDI IUT students, offering expertise on IUT policies, procedures, and degree requirements, and initial degree planning. Provide training on CMDI degree programs and IUT policies and requirements to campus partners. Cultivate and sustain strong working relationships across the campus community to enhance collaboration, communication, and support for CMDI IUT students. Develop and manage outreach efforts for students identified as admitted, on-track, or denied providing proactive guidance on advising and registration topics. Design and implement advising resources for prospective IUT students and campus partners in advising, such as IUT curriculum guides. Contribute on the campus IUT committee along with the Director of Academic Advising and Student Success. Partner with the Director of Academic Advising and Student Success to maintain and update all IUT advising and student success webpages and Canvas modules. Provide leadership through the assessment and reporting of IUT enrollment data and trends. Share this information with advising and college leadership to support long-term planning, enrollment management, and student retention and success goals. Coordinate with Director of Academic Advising &Student Success on advisor assignments for incoming IUT students. Collaborate with department course schedulers, ensure courses identified as “On-Track” have the appropriate requisites prior to each registration period. Minor Management Act as Lead Advisor for non-CMDI students pursuing a CMDI minor. Provide strategic oversight and stewardship of CMDI’s academic minors. Monitor enrollment trends, student demand, and completion data for CMDI minors and provide recommendations regarding growth opportunities, resource needs, and program effectiveness. Manage the college’s declaration process for CMDI minors, including reviewing and updating student records. Support the launch of new CMDI minors including the promotion and development of advising and student resources, as well as the promotion of current minors to campus partners. Collaborate with colleges, schools, and programs to promote CMDI minors. Coordinate with departments to provide student data related to minors. Process Undergraduate Student Petitions Support the processing of college-wide student petitions including late drops, course substitutions, credit overload, and independent studies. Collaborate with the CMDI Advising Leadership Team on review and decision-making processes, department chairs, and the Registrar’s Office to facilitate petition review and implementation. Student Advising and Advisor Support CMDI Major Advising Maintain a limited advising caseload of 50-150 CMDI students and provide comprehensive, developmental advising to support student persistence, retention, and academic success. Conduct proactive outreach and engagement with students who may not actively seek advising support. Use advising protocols, student success technologies, and campus systems to provide effective advising services. Provide advising coverage and operational support for advising staff as needed. Supports New Student Orientation and other recruitment and retention activities as well as provide occasional evening/weekend hours to support strategic College-wide and campus programming as assigned. IUT Advising Help prospective IUT students assess their interests, values, personal and academic goals as they explore and learn about the different degree programs offered in CMDI. Maintain weekly afternoon drop-in hours for prospective IUT students and offer additional drop-in hours as needed. Maintain sufficient individual advising appointment availability (typically 8-10 appointments) for prospective IUT students each week, adjusting availability during peak advising periods. Offer group advising workshops for prospective or newly admitted IUT students. Minor Advising Maintain a caseload of non-CMDI students in all CMDI minors. Offer limited individual advising appointments for non-CMDI students in CMDI minors. Conduct proactive outreach to students in the minors as needed. Supervision and Staff Leadership Supervise, coach, and evaluate full-time professional staff members and student success personnel. Establish performance expectations, conduct mid-cycle coaching conversations, and support professional growth planning for supervised staff. Provide oversight for assigned programmatic areas including resource planning and allocation in collaboration with the Director of Academic Advising & Student Success and the Assistant Dean of Student Success. Consult with the Director of Academic Advising & Student Success and the Assistant Dean of Student Success regarding significant resource allocation adjustments throughout the planning cycle. Manage expenditures, Procurement Card transactions, reconciliations, and related fiscal responsibilities in accordance with university policies. Support strategy, planning, operational effectiveness, and continuous improvement with supervised functional areas. Assist with the review and processing of student petitions, including late drops, course substitutions, grade appeals, credit overload requests, independent studies, research assistantships, and incomplete agreements. Support the coordination and resolution of student and family concerns with professionalism, care, and sensitivity. Participate in the Advising Leadership Team to support strategy, planning, operational decision-making, cross-functional collaboration, and the advancement of student success initiatives across the unit. Professional Development and Duties Proactively develop and maintain currency of knowledge of standard methodologies in academic advising and student success and higher education administration through conferences, mentoring, workshops, professional associations, and research. Participate in unit and leadership meetings and contribute to the continuous improvement, development, and implementation of unit standard processes. Perform additional duties and special projects assigned to support the mission and goals of CMDI and the Student Success and Advising unit. What You Should Know Monday to Friday 8am to 5pm, with occasional evening and weekend hours; hybrid modality. What We Can Offer The salary range for this position is $79,000 - $84,000 annually. Relocation stipend may be offered based on department guidelines Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be collaborative. Be engaged. Be Boulder. What We Require Bachelor's degree and three years of professional academic advising, or related experience. Equivalent combination of education and experience my substitute. What You Will Need Demonstrated ability to supervise and lead a collaborative student success team. Dedication to providing an exceptional student experience through multidisciplinary and developmental advising practices. Ability to build partnerships and collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, and campus partners. Strong organizational, communication, and project management skills. Experience balancing operational leadership with student advocacy and support. Strong computer skills including proficiency with Microsoft 365 applications, SharePoint, Excel, Teams, and student success technologies. Knowledge of academic advising practices, student records systems, degree audits, and advising technologies at CU Boulder. Familiarity with CU Boulder advising systems and student success infrastructure. Demonstrated cultural competency and dedication to inclusive student support practices. What We Would Like You to Have Master’s degree and six years of related professional experience. Experience supervising professional staff or student employees. Experience leading student retention and early intervention initiatives. Knowledge of advising and degree audit technologies, including Transferology, CU-SIS, and Salesforce. Experience developing and implementing training or professional development programs. Experience managing multiple projects, initiatives, and operational processes simultaneously. Experience troubleshooting and maintaining degree audit systems. Experience with program management. Experience with data and assessment management. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 5, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Asset-Accounting-Manager/74950": {
      "title": "Asset Accounting Manager",
      "description": "Asset Accounting Manager Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Asset Accounting Manager Requisition Number: 74950 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 05-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 18-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary The Campus Controller's Office at CU Boulder welcomes applications for an Asset Accounting Manager! The Asset Management Unit exists to assure proper accounting for capital equipment and inventories for the University of Colorado at Boulder (UCB). This unit provides consulting, training, and mentoring regarding asset management to campus personnel on the use of the ﬁnance systems and the business processes associated therewith, including departmental roles and responsibilities. They assist campus partners in facilitating accounting management and control of ﬁscal operations and resources. This position exists to control new acquisition of capital equipment by campus departments by reviewing and verifying purchasing documents; contacting departments; correcting improper accounting codes; tracking/uploading ﬁles; reconciling transactions to department ﬁles and assigning tags and coordinating the tagging of new equipment. The lifecycle of campus capital equipment is ultimately managed by this position to ensure correct policies, procedures, accounting, and ﬁnancial reporting. This position also trains and manages the Property Accounting Specialist and students (when needed) in completing periodic capital equipment inventory tasks; including but not limited to: capital equipment tagging, capital equipment inventory cycles from start to ﬁnish, data input into the PSAM system and statistical sampling and testing of department completed capital equipment inventories. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are The Campus Controller's Office maintains and supports the university's fiscal responsibility through accurate financial accounting and reporting and in compliance with laws, regulations, policies/procedures and contract requirements for sponsored projects. We provide oversight, assistance and training in financial stewardship for the campus. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Execution and Management of Capital Asset Process Execute and manage the collection of capital equipment by reviewing purchase orders, journal entries and information from campus partners. Execute and manage the entry of a large volume of capital assets into PSAM each month. Manage the new and replacement of capital equipment tags for campus partners within the current and new process. Execute and manage the Gift-in-kind process. Manage the reconciliation of capital accounts. Review of non-capital expense accounts to ensure proper accounting assignment. Manage, Research and Attend to Campus Requests Review and approve Journal Entries for account code 810XXX on sponsored research speedtypes for allowability. Check to ensure Journal Entries are necessary and allowable under capital equipment guidelines and policies. Research Financial Services (RFS) and RFS Closeout route these JE’s journal entries to this position for approval within PeopleSoft. Monitor and address all capital equipment related emails in the property email inbox with a focus on training, collaboration, responsiveness, issue resolution and customer service to our campus partners. Capital Equipment Fabrication Tracking and Recording Work with departments to set up new capital equipment fabrications and make additions to fabrications. Create fabrication tag numbers and review and approve new fabrication speedtypes. Monitor expenses that are charged to the fabrication expense account code. When a fabrication is complete, work with the department to make sure the expenses are complete, accurate in the correct account code. Review, reconcile, and approve fabrication costs then enter the fabrication into PSAM and create the necessary Journal Entry. Work with RFS on any capital equipment issues or questions that they may have. Work with the RFS Closeout group in the Closeout process of awards to ensure that Fabrications are completed before the award is closed out and all capital related expenses are complete and accurate before the ﬁnal report is sent to the sponsor and the speedtypes are closed. Campus Training, Website Maintenance and Policy Updates Responsible for co-planning an annual capital equipment and fabrication training for departmental staff, one-on-one training as needed, attending the Office of Contracts and Grants (OCG) and Property Services Training as a resource and working with the CCO Trainers as needed. Works closely with the CCO Leadership to update equipment related policies and procedures, including the Capital Equipment Property Accounting Office (PAO) standard operations manual. There are several equipment related forms that require periodic maintenance, updating and approval. There is a dedicated PAO website that this role is the primary contact. Train, Supervise and Manage the Property Accounting Specialist and Students Trains and manages the Property Accounting Specialist and Students (when needed) in completing periodic equipment inventory tasks; including but not limited to: equipment tagging, equipment inventory cycles from start to finish, data input into the PSAM system and statistical sampling and testing of department completed equipment inventories Coordination with Property Services and Equipment Disposal Works closely with Property Services on the disposal of equipment. This includes accessing their system and reviewing (or monitoring) the reviewing of campus items being disposed through Property Services to ensure the completeness and accuracy of disposed equipment, that will then be disposed of in PSAM. Continue to coordinate with Property Services to find the most efficient way for campus partners to dispose and transfer their equipment. Special Projects This position will be required to assist with short-term and long-term special projects as they are identified and deemed necessary by CCO Leadership. The focus of these projects are to enhance and modernize the capital equipment systems, processes and policies of campus. What You Should Know What You Should Know Hybrid work modality offered. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The salary range for this position is $90,000 - $114,000 annually. Relocation may be offered within departmental guidelines. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be balanced. Be positive. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Bachelor's degree in Accounting, Business, Finance or asset related field. Five years of accounting experience, including asset management accounting or budgeting experience with strong Excel skills and previous supervisory experience. Equivalent combination of education and experience may substitute. What You Will Need What You Will Need Thorough knowledge of advanced accounting concepts, principles and practices, rules and regulations, GASB, GAAP, NACUBO accounting principles and guidelines. Ability to manage advanced cost accounting analyses and evaluations independently. Advanced skills in Microsoft Excel. This must include advanced spreadsheet/database skills, including use of data warehouse and manipulation of large data sets. This position must be able to establish rapport and become a credible source of information and guidance for campus partners. Proven ability to develop solutions to a variety of problems of moderate scope and complexity using existing policies and practices as a guide. Attention to detail and the ability to recognize unusual situations, question them and provide alternative solutions. Ability to prioritize and use sound judgment to meet deadlines and work independently. Analytical thinking and the ability to recognize and question unusual situations. Ability to learn and use new software systems. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to collaborate with varying levels of authority and cross-functional teams. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Bachelor's degree in Accounting. Three to five years capital asset management. Experience with Peoplesoft Asset Management system or related asset management software. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 5, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-Electrical-Computer-and-Energy-Engineering/74871": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Electrical, Computer and Energy Engineering",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Electrical, Computer and Energy Engineering Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenure-Track Faculty in Electrical, Computer and Energy Engineering Requisition Number: 74871 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 18-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Department of Electrical, Computer and Energy Engineering (ECEE) is hiring two tenure-track professors at the Assistant Professor rank. We will consider candidates in all areas of electrical and computer engineering. Potential research areas of interest include communications, power, sensing, controls, robotics, signals, computing hardware, and theory for quantum technologies. We are particularly interested in candidates who bridge disciplines, have synergistic interests with existing faculty, or bring unique capabilities. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs, earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally important, we are committed to fostering a diverse and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college's strategic vision has three core foci: (i) research and innovation, (ii) education, and (iii) inclusion. We believe our success depends on faculty who actively work to create an environment where all students can thrive and who are committed to the campus plan for creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science please visit our website . The ECEE Department includes 3 University Distinguished Professors, 8 endowed chairs, 40 tenure/tenure-track faculty, 8 teaching faculty, and 4 research faculty. Faculty honors include 16 IEEE Fellows, 4 members of the US National Academy of Engineering, a National Science Foundation (NSF) Presidential Faculty Fellow, and 22 NSF CAREER Award recipients. The ECEE program consists of about 650 undergraduate and 450 graduate students and has averaged over $16 million in annual research expenditures. The department maintains close ties with local industry and national institutes/labs such as NIST, NLR, and NOAA. More information about the Electrical, Computer & Energy Engineering Department is available on the department website . Information on the Electrical, Computer & Energy Engineering Department's strategic vision can be found here . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Successful candidates will be expected to engage in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; develop an externally funded research program in their technical area; contribute professional leadership and service to their unit, the college, and their professional community; and cultivate and contribute to the inclusive excellence of our academic community. The typical distribution of work for this role is 40% teaching, 40% scholarly and creative work (research), and 20% leadership and service. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for this position is at the start of the Fall 2027 semester; however, a later start date may be negotiated. What We Can Offer The anticipated academic-year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $115,000-$145,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits, visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to some of these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development . Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be challenged. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate in electrical engineering, computer engineering or a related field, or have their degree conferred before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual PDF files: Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals, as well as ways you may fit in the department. Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society (2-3 pages). A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement within engineering, or participation in advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision (1-2 pages). During the application process, you will be asked to provide the names and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, however for full consideration please apply by November 15, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. Applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Accounting-Technician-III/74853": {
      "title": "Accounting Technician III",
      "description": "Accounting Technician III Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Accounting Technician III Requisition Number: 74853 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 27-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 18-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Department of Student Life Finance is encouraging applications for the Accounting Technician III role! This role collects, verifies, and records cash receipts on behalf of division units in accordance with campus and division accounting standards, policies, and procedures. This position is part of a team that is the on-campus presence for Student Life Finance and Business Services. In particular, the role serves as the primary customer service contact for students, parents, campus constituents, and external contacts through in-person, phone, and email communications. The purpose of this role is to provide accounting support to units within the Division of Student Life (SL) by ensuring proper recording in the PeopleSoft (PS) Financial System and reconciling the general ledger with multiple financial subsystems of record and payment platforms used across the division. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The Division of Student Life consists of 36 departments that provide student support and services dedicated to helping students become successful, curious, competent individuals during their time at CU Boulder. Student Life Finance and Business Services Student Life Finance and Business Services serves as a centralized service center for the Division of Student Life, providing comprehensive support across financial management, procurement, travel, billing and invoicing, cash management, and reconciliation. The team partners closely with departments to understand their operational needs and to provide informed guidance on sound financial and accounting practices. Services include budget planning, financial management best practices, reporting and analysis, billing, and invoicing, accounting functions, and revenue reconciliation as well as procurement and travel support. Team members assist departments with acquiring and expanding goods and services, coordinating, and advising on group and individual travel, and ensuring timely and accurate substantiation of expenses. They also support departments in interpreting and implementing state and university financial policies and procedures, serving as liaisons to CU system and campus partners. Throughout this work, the team aligns processes with campus and system guidelines while advocating for the unique operational needs of the Division of Student Life. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Cash Management Process payments and refunds through in-person, phone, and online methods. Verify daily that payments received via online portals are posted accurately in financial subsystems Properly identify and record cash, checks, wire and ACH transfers, bank deposits, and online and in-office credit card transactions in financial subsystems promptly. Retrieve, verify and log cash receipts from various locations on central campus on a weekly basis or as needed. Ensure compliant deposit of all cash and checks, either by delivery to Office of Cash Management or via desktop deposit batch in office. Resolve account issues by troubleshooting, updating subsystems, and communicating with the payer or the account holder. Identify irregularities in units' shift reports and determine proper recording of overages/shortages, ensuring campus guidelines are followed. Collaborate with departments to meet key financial due dates. Maintain divisional change funds, including cash disbursements, regular reconciliation of change fund balances and distribution records, and replenishment check requests. Process and maintain documentation that records chain of custody for cash transactions, resolves reconciliation findings, and supports external and internal audits. Complete project work to support divisional goals and cash operations as needed. Accounting Support & Documentation Create journal entries to record payments, recognize revenue, and distribute fees to the accurate speedtypes and accounts with appropriate line descriptions. Use available resources to review transactions, maintain consistency, and meet monthly and annual financial close deadlines. Investigate discrepancies between subsystems and the General Ledger to support accurate financial reporting. Identify issues through accounting reconciliations, particularly balance sheet and revenue accounts. Exercise judgment in determining when to resolve issues. Stay current with operational changes that impact account reconciliations, procedures, or other accounting processes. Regularly review and make recommendations for additions, revisions, and updates to the Cash Management Processing Guides. Manage compliance with campus Records Retention & Disposition policy. Support fiscal year-end operations through preparing documentation, researching account balances, and creating accrual and adjusting journal entries as needed. Exercise critical analysis of operations and communicate to leadership when policies and procedures contradict an environment conducive to DEI (Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion) initiatives and student success Customer Service Primary respondent to shared SL Finance email inbox and phone line. Offer hospitality as a first point of contact to students, parents and other visitors while ensuring privacy of student data per FERPA (Family Education Rights and Privacy Act). Maintain productive and professional relationships with other departments/internal contacts. Other Duties Promote ethical and sustainable business practices by identifying development opportunities or process improvements. Engage in professional training opportunities, individually and with other SL Units Participate in New Student Welcome, Move-In, and other campus events to support Student Life and student success. What You Should Know This position is in a hybrid work opportunity, working 4 days on-campus and 1 day remote. This individual can expect to be on campus Monday-Friday during the initial training period, with a transition to work from home one day a week once established. The work schedule for this position is Monday - Friday, 8 am - 5 pm. What We Can Offer The salary range for this position is $50,845 - $52,000 annually. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be effective. Be flexible. Be Boulder. What We Require Three years of experience: Demonstrating customer service, or In a front-line hospitality role, or Working in a financial environment Appropriate education will substitute for the required experience on a year-for-year basis. What You Will Need Strong customer service skills. Proficiency in Excel. Highly detail-oriented and organized. Excellent verbal and written communication skills with experience communicating complex subjects to customers from a diverse set of backgrounds. Commitment to inclusion, and equity. What We Would Like You to Have Bachelor’s degree or equivalent work experience. Prior experience in cash handling. Prior experience in higher education, public administration or related field. Prior experience in a financial environment working with financial management software such as PeopleSoft and/or student management software i.e. StarRez. Prior experience of PCI Standards. Knowledge of FERPA and application in daily work. Excellent time management skills and ability to prioritize tasks. Demonstrated ability to learn multiple payment systems and adapt to new processes. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by September 27, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Grounds-Equipment-Mechanic-East-Campus/74773": {
      "title": "Grounds Equipment Mechanic",
      "description": "Grounds Equipment Mechanic – East Campus Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Grounds Equipment Mechanic – East Campus Requisition Number: 74773 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Classified Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 04-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 18-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary Facilities Management (FM) at CU Boulder encourages applications for a Grounds Equipment Mechanic ! Outdoor Services provides high quality, professional grounds and landscape services to enhance a safe, aesthetically pleasing and environmentally conscious campus. FM provides a physical environment that promotes the advancement and transfer of knowledge. This position performs, leads, and assigns emergency and routine repair and maintenance tasks on light to heavy-duty grounds machinery and equipment associated with the Outdoor Services Team. Additional responsibilities include parts inventory management, PM scheduling, and collecting information and reporting on equipment repair expenses. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are Facilities Management provides outdoor services to general fund areas and several auxiliary funded facilities. FM Outdoor Services is responsible for grounds maintenance and irrigation system operation and maintenance for the general fund areas on campus. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Specialized and Heavy-Duty Equipment Repair and Maintenance Diagnose problems, make repairs and perform preventative maintenance on specialized grounds equipment including multi-deck contour mowers, mid-sized and compact tractors and implements, skid steer loaders and attachments, backhoes, wood chippers and various utility vehicles. Inspect, diagnose, repair, rebuild and replace a wide variety of equipment systems such as engine, transmission, steering, driveline, braking, electrical, pneumatic, and hydraulic. Repairs may require fabrication of parts and/or welding parts and structural members. Keep accurate records of labor and parts expended on repairs. Report missing equipment and accidents resulting in physical damage to University vehicle(s) or property to appropriate departments. Battery/Electric/Alternate Fuel Powered Equipment Maintenance and Repair Troubleshoot, repair and maintain small and handheld grounds equipment including push mowers, string trimmers, blowers, edgers, chainsaws, golf carts etc.. This includes repairing small motors, batteries, electrical circuits, wiring, solenoids, pumps, switches, bearings AC and DC systems. Perform all preventative maintenance on this equipment, which is powered by a variety of diesel, gas, and rechargeable electric batteries. Crew Member Responsibilities Conduct training for staff (full-time, temporary, and student employees). Demonstrate an \"eye for detail\" when making and assessing actions as the work leader for the Assistant Mechanic by coordinating and assigning work assignments. Coordinate and schedule repairs with supervisors of crews and crew leads. Work and coordinate with outside vendors and equipment owners to schedule estimates and repairs. Make recommendations to supervisor for enhanced training and continuing education based on equipment inventory, skills, and knowledge. Provide timely updates on equipment repairs, associates costs, and delays. Track parts and labor with repairs and inform supervisor of activities or problems having an impact on operations. Coordinate with Supervisors and other employees to ensure smooth daily operations, partnership, and consistency of work efforts. Ensure timecards and charges are accurate and submitted. Snow and Ice Removal and Equipment Repair Support snow removal and ice abatement operations by making emergency repairs to plows, salt spreaders and other essential equipment. Conduct and participate in annual snow removal equipment readiness inspection, compile list of necessary repairs, prioritize and complete repairs identified. Repairs may include welding and fabricating parts to keep vehicle snow plowing systems operational, including plows, blades, mounting hardware, hydraulic and electrical systems. May assist with ice abatement and snow removal operations. Inventory Tracking, Record Keeping, Shop, Equipment and Tool Maintenance Input and track numerous, complex and changing data fields in computer maintenance system by each machine including data like serial number, location of equipment, and repair and replacement history, including capital and non-capital equipment. Track inventory of repair parts and shop supplies. Responsible for the proper use, maintenance and care of assigned equipment (vehicles, grounds utility vehicles, equipment lifts, power washers, specialty equipment, hand tools, etc.). Maintain neat and orderly shop with tools and supplies in their proper location. Maintain access to safety equipment. Safety Adhere to safety guidelines and policies. Responsible for the safe operation and repair of vehicles, equipment and tools; provide instruction on safe operating procedures to Staff, Work Leaders and Supervisor and uses personal protective equipment (PPE) as required. Ensures all staff under roles direction perform duties using appropriate safe work practices. Provide and train staff working under roles direction with appropriate safety equipment and PPE, and proper use of PPE. Be aware of surroundings, ensuring work area is clear and free of personnel, tools, trip hazards, flammables, etc. prior to commencing work. Understand aspects of tools and materials worked with, including welders, torches, gasses, compressors, tools, and other tools and equipment. There is a high risk of injuries in equipment repair and this position must do everything possible to minimize risk. Be familiar with the Safety Manual, providing recommendations to the Safety Committee. What You Should Know What You Should Know This role is eligible for our Facilities Management Employee Referral Program . This in-person position works 5 eight-hour days per week, Monday - Friday, between the hours of 6:00 am and 5:00 pm, based on staffing requirements of the Department. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The annual salary rate for this position is $74,000. If you have prior state-classified experience in the same class series as this position, your pay may be set at a higher amount per the state Step Pay Program implemented on July 1, 2024. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be collaborative. Be inclusive. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Must be a resident of Colorado to apply. Must have a valid Colorado driver's license. 4 years of relevant experience with current proficiency at the journey level OR a combination of related education, formalized apprenticeship program, and/or relevant experience equal to 4 years. What You Will Need What You Will Need Strong customer service and interpersonal skills. Attention to detail. Strong communication skills. Digital literacy (timecard entry, inventory tracking, emails). Reliability. Ability to work within a team environment and collaborate with other team members. Mechanically inclined. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have 2 years of institutional, golf course, or commercial landscape equipment maintenance and repair. Battery/Electric equipment repair experience highly desired. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume that includes date ranges for each position held and whether the position was full-time or part-time. Optional : A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. DD-214 for veteran applicants. Qualified veterans and surviving spouses are eligible for veteran’s preference points within the State classified system. If you are an honorably discharged veteran, the spouse of a veteran unable to work due to a service-connected disability, or the surviving spouse of a veteran, you may claim preference during the selection process for a position within the state personnel system. In order for us to determine your eligibility for veterans preference, you must attach a copy of a DD-214 form along with your application. If you have additional supporting documentation to provide, or do not wish to upload a DD214, please email a copy to recruiting@colorado.edu and reference the job to which you are applying. If using the educational substitution for experience: proof of degree or transcripts. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 4, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . Select here for additional information regarding Classified Staff positions: www.cu.edu/cu-careers/special-instructions-classified-staff-applicants In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Essential Services Essential Services This position is designated “essential services.” The incumbent is required to respond to requests for work during campus emergencies (snow, wind, rain, flood and any other natural or man-made event) and report to work for his / her regularly assigned shift during emergency campus closures. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Professional-Research-Assistant/74689": {
      "title": "Professional Research Assistant",
      "description": "Professional Research Assistant Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Professional Research Assistant Requisition Number: 74689 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 02-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 18-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Professional Research Assistant will conduct original research on the design, fabrication, and metrology of superconducting spintronic devices for advanced superconducting electronics.  The work includes thin-film materials development, cleanroom fabrication, high-speed (>10 GHz) electronic characterization, and modeling of magnetic flux behavior and mitigation strategies relevant to scalable superconducting and neuromorphic circuits. The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) invites applications for a Professional Research Assistant through the Professional Research Experience Program (PREP).  This position offers the opportunity to lead experimental research in superconducting spintronics, combining materials development, device fabrication, and high-speed electronic metrology within a national laboratory environment. This position is part of the National Institute of Standards and Technology's (NIST) Professional Research Experience Program (PREP).  NIST recognizes that its research staff may wish to collaborate with researchers at academic institutions on specific projects of mutual interest, thus requires that such institutions must be the recipient of a PREP award.  The PREP program requires staff from a wide range of backgrounds to work on scientific research in many areas.  Employees in this position will perform technical work that underpins the scientific research of the collaboration. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are PREP is a special partnership between the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) and the University of Colorado Boulder.  PREP provides research opportunities to CU undergraduate and graduate students, as well as researchers with a Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD in NIST labs to gain research experience alongside NIST scientists. The particular group that needs a researcher is the Spin Electronics Group at NIST. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Design, fabricate, and characterize superconducting spintronic devices Operate and optimize multi-gun sputter deposition systems Explore new ferromagnetic and spintronic material stacks. Perform high-speed electronic measurements of superconducting devices and circuits. Develop scalable measurement and data-analysis workflows. Publish in peer-reviewed journals and present at scientific conferences. Collaborate closely with NIST scientists while maintaining research ownership. What You Should Know This position requires the successful completion of an NAIS agreement and federal security clearance as mandated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).  Employment is contingent upon approval of these requirements as determined by NIST. Anticipated start date is November 20, 2026.  Anticipated end date is one year, but no later than March 31, 2028. Privacy Act Statement Authority: 15 U.S.C. § 278g-1(e)(1) and (e)(3) and 15 U.S.C. § 272(b) and (c) Purpose: The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) hosts the Professional Research Experience Program (PREP) which is designed to provide valuable laboratory experience and financial assistance to undergraduates, post-bachelor’s degree holders, graduate students, master’s degree holders, postdocs, and faculty. PREP is a 5-year cooperative agreement between NIST laboratories and participating PREP Universities to establish a collaborative research relationship between NIST and U.S. institutions of higher education in the following disciplines including (but may not be limited to) biochemistry, biological sciences, chemistry, computer science, engineering, electronics, materials science, mathematics, nanoscale science, neutron science, physical science, physics, and statistics.  This collection of information is needed to facilitate administrative functions of the PREP Program. Routine Uses: NIST will use the information collected to perform the requisite reviews of the applications to determine eligibility, and to meet programmatic requirements.  Disclosure of this information is also subject to all the published routine uses as identified in the Privacy Act System of Records Notices:  NIST-1: NIST Associates. Disclosure: Furnishing this information is voluntary.  When you submit the form, you are indicating your voluntary consent for NIST to use of the information you submit for the purpose stated. What We Can Offer The salary range for this full-time position is $65,000 - $108,000 annually. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Master’s degree (completed or near completion) in Physics, Electrical Engineering, Materials Science, or a related field. What We Would Like You to Have PhD degree (completed or near completion) in Physics, Electrical Engineering, Materials Science, or a related field. Experience with cryogenics. Background in ferromagnetic, spintronic, or superconducting measurements. Proficiency with Python or similar scripting languages. Strong written and oral communication skills. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. (optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor.  If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process, you will need to enter contact information for a reference.  We will request the letter of recommendation immediately following your application. For full consideration, please apply by October 2, 2026. Note:  Application materials will not be accepted via email.  For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Lisa Valencia Posting Contact Email: recruitprep@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Proposal-Analyst-Sponsored-Project-Specialist/74966": {
      "title": "Proposal Analyst/Sponsored Project Specialist",
      "description": "Proposal Analyst/Sponsored Project Specialist Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Proposal Analyst/Sponsored Project Specialist Requisition Number: 74966 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 30-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 17-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary JILA is welcoming applications for a Proposal Analyst/Sponsored Project Specialist. This role will support JILA's Principal Investigators and administration of awards both pre- and post-award. The Proposal Analyst (PA) works directly with JILA and potentially other University faculty, research administrators, and students to ensure proposals reﬂect sponsor and University requirements. PAs ensure proposals are submitted by the proposal deadline. This role is responsible for supporting JILA's Principal Investigators by assisting with the administration of sponsored research awards to ensure compliance with all applicable regulations. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are JILA is a joint institute of the University of Colorado Boulder and the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) located on the beautiful CU Boulder main campus at the base of the Rocky Mountains. Led by the roughly 30 research Fellows, JILA supports eclectic and innovative research programs that foster creative collaborations. The wide-ranging interests of our scientists have made JILA one of the nation's leading physical sciences research institutes. JILA has a total annual budget of $50 million, with 297 members (including faculty, staff, students, and postdoctoral researchers). JILA offers a uniquely collaborative and mission-driven environment. The institute has a strong culture of intellectual curiosity, community, and inclusiveness, which provides a meaningful and engaging workplace. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Proposal Analyst Collaborate with members of JILA, other campus departments, and sponsors to address issues and solve problems prior to proposal submission and during the sponsor's proposal review and evaluation. Responsible for researching and responding to inquiries from investigators, department administrators, University oﬃcers, colleagues, and sponsors. Collaborate with other campus departments, CU central offices and sponsors to address issues and solve problems throughout the life-cycle of sponsored projects. Educate Principal Investigators about sponsored project policies, procedures, and practices in Research Administration, within JILA, and within the University of Colorado Boulder. Upload and maintain timely, accurate proposal documentation in InfoEd. Serve as an Authorized Organizational Representative (AOR) by facilitating the development and submission of proposals to support sponsored projects on behalf of JILA and the University, while also protecting the legal and business interests of JILA and the University. Represent the University and work directly with a variety of sponsors: federal and non-federal, state agencies, businesses, foundations, and other universities. Sponsored Project Specialist Set up initial awards and subcontracts in JILA tracking system. Ensure that assigned sponsored research awards are administered in compliance with all applicable regulations. Work with the Campus Controllers Office on audits and project closeouts, ensuring appropriate documentation is collected and retained. Provide in-depth information or perform research in order to answer questions or address issues related to sponsored projects. Support proposal preparation, including budgets and supporting documentation. Prepare ﬁnancial analysis and reports on a monthly and as-needed basis. Advise JILA administration and Principal Investigators on sponsored project issues. Monitor spending and process invoices on subawards issued to collaborators outside the University in order to further speciﬁc research goals. Assist Principal Investigators in the preparation and submission of sponsored project funding proposals on behalf of JILA and in compliance with University of Colorado Boulder practices and policies. Ensure all expenditures on assigned awards are appropriate, allocable, reasonable and treated consistently within JILA, CU and sponsor standards. Submit and maintain all necessary documentation for accurate execution of processes such as accounts payable, reimbursements, allocations, journal entries, and adhering to additional requirements for sensitive expenditures. Review purchase transactions for allowability, including approval of expense reports and travel authorizations. Review accounting and budget records for accuracy and make corrections via journal entry if/as needed. Verify, research, and resolve outstanding obligations and encumbrances. Complete project closeouts in established timeline, including property reports. Complete requests for at-risk, no-cost extensions. Actively contribute to the development of JILA procedures, forms, and training compliance. Review and monitor documentation associated with awards in InfoEd, extracting detailed information relevant to award management. Provide consistent and high-quality customer service to researchers and develop strong working relationships with both internal and external customers. Administrative Support Provide administrative support as needed. What You Should Know This position offers a hybrid work modality with 1 day of remote work per week. We are unable to offer visa sponsorship for this position. What We Can Offer The hiring range for this position is $60,000-$70,000. Relocation is available for eligible candidates within department guidelines. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require A bachelor's degree and 1+ years of experience supporting grant proposals, including knowledge of pre- and post-award monitoring and compliance. An equivalent combination of education and experience may substitute for these requirements. What You Will Need Knowledge of federal regulations, state statutes, University policy, standard processes and project requirements. Adherence to Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200) requirements Navigation of Federal Acquisition Regulations (FAR). Understanding of the administrative processes and procedures for grants, contracts, and other agreements both internal and external to the University. Ability to assess the legal, administrative, and ﬁnancial risks associated with sponsor terms and conditions in order to guide investigators and other University personnel through business decisions related to potential awards. Skilled budgeting and adherence to University estimating practices and Cost Accounting Standards. Excellent organization, attention to detail, and time management, as well as the ability to manage contending priorities. Strong teamwork skills to support cross-training and collaboration with campus partners including the Oﬃce of Contracts and Grants and the Campus Controllers Office. Proficiency in Excel and accounting software, and a basic understanding of database systems. Proficiency with Microsoft business applications, including Word, Teams, and PowerPoint. Ability to communicate clearly and positively with a variety of persons. What We Would Like You to Have 2+ years experience in supporting grant proposals, including knowledge of pre- and post-award monitoring and compliance. Familiarity with federal agency electronic systems such as Research.gov (NSF), NSPIRES (NASA), Grants.gov/Workspace, NIH Assist, and eRA Commons. Knowledge of and experience using complex accounting systems such as Peoplesoft or SAP. Knowledge of and experience using Sponsored Research software systems and databases. Knowledge of and experience using Microsoft Power BI, and/or Excel pivot tables. Experience with purchasing and expense/travel software systems. Knowledge of pre-award and post-award administration with JILA’s main sponsors - NSF, DOE and DOD. Demonstrated experience of sponsored research administration practices. Demonstrated experience in preparing journal entries, reconciling complex financial accounts and preparing financial analysis. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by 9/30/26 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Director-of-Strategic-Initiatives/74963": {
      "title": "Director of Strategic Initiatives",
      "description": "Director of Strategic Initiatives Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Director of Strategic Initiatives Requisition Number: 74963 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 01-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 17-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Reporting to the Dean, the Director of Strategic Initiatives is a trusted thought-partner who provides a school-wide, integrative perspective across Leeds, identifying and advancing connections across units to achieve our strategic priorities. The Director maintains a broad understanding of strategic initiatives and activities across the School and works closely with the Dean, Dean’s Cabinet and other school leaders to find opportunities where greater coordination, alignment, and collaboration can advance Leeds’ strategic objectives. In close partnership with the Assistant Dean for Advancement and the Dean, the Director also helps shape the agenda for Leeds Advisory Board (LAB) meetings, ensuring the Board’s expertise and engagement are strategically leveraged to support and advance the School’s strategic priorities. The Director provides leadership and project management for AACSB accreditation in coordination with the Associate Dean for Research and Academics, as well as campus-level accreditation reviews. This includes close partnerships with faculty and various staff units to collect, compile, and report data on the success of the Leeds School, its programs and initiatives and coordination of accreditation review visits. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are At Leeds, we have an outstanding vantage point, and while we’re located at the foot of the Rocky Mountains, we’re not talking about the scenery. Set in the heart of Boulder, Colorado—one of the nation’s most vibrant business communities—Leeds offers unparalleled access to world-changing entrepreneurs, national research institutions and award-winning faculty! What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Strategic Plan Administration In partnership with the Dean, be responsible for administration and ongoing execution of the Leeds strategic plan. Manage regular check-ins with academic divisions and unit leaders to ensure alignment and execution of initiatives, providing ongoing support to move critical initiatives towards successful completion. Develop feedback mechanisms allowing faculty and staff to provide input on strategic plan implementation in their respective units. Use feedback to improve activation of the strategic plan across the school. Maintain records of unit-level strategic plans for each academic division and program, ensuring they are regularly reviewed and updated to track progress. Manage the external review process for academic divisions, in which visiting scholars travel to Leeds to provide their perspectives on our academic divisions to support innovation and improvement. Track school-wide progress on the strategic plan, using a dashboard of important measurements and other reporting tools to support ongoing monitoring and assessment. Maintain a broad, school-wide perspective on strategic priorities, initiatives, and emerging opportunities in order to identify connections, dependencies, gaps, and opportunities across organizational boundaries. Partner with the Dean to identify initiatives that would benefit from strategic integration or cross-functional coordination. Serve as a strategic thought partner to the Dean and other members of the Dean’s Cabinet on initiatives with significant cross-school implications. The Director coordinates cross-functional initiatives until clear ownership is established and transitions sustained implementation to the appropriate functional or portfolio leader. Participate in Cabinet and other leadership/strategy meetings to maintain current understanding of priorities, decisions, emerging initiatives, and organizational context. Maintain regular strategic touchpoints with Dean’s Cabinet members and other school leaders to identify developments, progress, and emerging priorities that should be reflected in updates to the School’s strategic plan. Accreditation & Institutional Review Lead the project management, coordination and overall process for Leeds' AACSB accreditation process, in close partnership with the Associate Dean for Research and Academics, who provides academic leadership and content expertise. Establish accreditation timelines, achievements, responsibilities, and documentation requirements. Manage the collection, organization, and maintenance of required accreditation data and supporting evidence across units. This includes coordination of a faculty and staff committee who determine assessment plans and data sources. Partner with academic and administrative leaders to develop required reports and manage the reporting process to ensure submission by established due dates. Coordinate internal and external review processes and evaluator visits. Maintain institutional knowledge and continuity between accreditation cycles. Leverage accreditation findings and documentation, where appropriate, as inputs to strategic planning and continuous improvement. Serve as the primary Leeds contact and coordinator for CU-campus level accreditation processes, including HLC and ARPAC. Communicate with Leeds Advisory Board (LAB) Along with the Assistant Dean of Advancement and the Dean, contribute to the development of the Leeds Advisory Board (LAB) agenda, positioning the Board as a resource for advancing the School’s strategic priorities and supporting the implementation of 2035 Leeds. Partner with the Assistant Dean of Advancement and the Dean to develop the Board’s meeting agendas and opportunities for involvement in alignment with the School’s strategic plan and emerging institutional needs. Maintain regular coordination with the advancement office to track the progress of Leeds Advisory Board working groups, ensuring their efforts remain aligned with the School’s strategic priorities. Coordination of the Portfolio of the Office of the Dean Coordinate with the Dean’s Cabinet, ensuring alignment among members around Leeds’ strategic priorities. Track key strategic priorities and follow-up items across the Dean’s Cabinet, helping maintain insight into progress, dependencies, and areas requiring the Dean’s attention or decision. In coordination with the Associate Dean for Administration and Finance, supervise the Dean’s Office Assistants, providing direction on administrative priorities and workflow to ensure the effective execution of the ongoing needs of the Dean’s Office and facilitate coordination across the portfolio of the Dean’s Office. What You Should Know This position offers a hybrid work modality with at least 3 days in the office and up to 5 depending on meeting schedule. Work hours are Monday-Friday, 8am-5pm plus evenings and weekends as needed for events, conferences and activities. What We Can Offer The hiring range for this position is $99,700-$115,000. Relocation is available for eligible candidates within department guidelines. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be engaged. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require Bachelor’s degree or equivalent combination of education and experience may substitute. 5+ years of professional experience including: working for an institution of higher education, supporting/advising executive or senior level staff, and supervisory experience. What You Will Need Requires a highly motivated individual with integrity, tact, discretion, a proven track record, and exceptional organizational skills. Ability to work across a variety of projects among diverse constituencies. Ability to problem-solve and effectively operate within a large, dynamic environment with experience in senior level administrative management in an academic environment. Experience with strategic planning processes and procedures. Management experience and skill, including the ability to be responsible for a complex and fast paced office in a manner that combines speed, precision, and thoroughness. Excellent interpersonal and communications skills with the ability to interact with a diverse group of internal and external constituents. Proven ability to engage effectively with high-level business leaders, scholars, alumni, school, and university administrators, faculty, and students. Ability to handle complex, multiple, concurrent tasks including excellent organizational skills and proficiency in prioritizing assignments to complete work accurately and in a timely manner. Anticipate, interpret, and respond to a variety of issues with minimum direction and the highest level of discretion and judgment. Process confidential matters and either resolve or refer questions to appropriate personnel. Must possess initiative and ability to anticipate executive needs for information and support. Demonstrated ability to plan, organize, coordinate, and direct multiple projects and activities with varied timelines, use sound judgment, and be a strategic problem solver. Ability to work with confidentiality and discretion when handling sensitive information. Promotes creating an inclusive environment to reflect our values and vision at Leeds. What We Would Like You to Have 5+ years of experience working for an institution of higher education. 5+ years of experience supporting/advising executive or senior level staff. 3+ years of supervisory experience. Experience working in a business school. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by October 1, 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Postdoctoral-Associate-or-Research-Associate/74947": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate Requisition Number: 74947 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 02-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 17-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary This work aims to develop hardware for quantum networks to implement advanced protocols such as quantum position verification. To this end, we are building a quantum network involving sources of high-quality photonic entanglement, fast quantum measurement stations, and stabilization technology that can be applied to quantum network links.  Careful modeling of network components is required, as well as building quantum sources and other related hardware required for distributing and characterizing the performance of entanglement distribution. The position requires working with both theory and experimental teams to ensure the hardware is capable of carrying out quantum position verification-related experiments. The project scope is limited to fundamental research, and results will be available in the public domain through journal articles and conference presentations. This position is part of the National Institute of Standards and Technology's (NIST) Professional Research Experience Program  (PREP).  NIST recognizes that its research staff may wish to collaborate with researchers at academic institutions on specific projects of mutual interest, thus requires that such institutions must be the recipient of a PREP award.  The PREP program requires staff from a wide range of backgrounds to work on scientific research in many areas.  Employees in this position will perform technical work that underpins the scientific research of the collaboration. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are PREP is a special partnership between the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) and the University of Colorado Boulder.  PREP provides research opportunities to CU undergraduate and graduate students, as well as researchers with a Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD in NIST labs to gain research experience alongside NIST scientists. The particular group that needs a researcher is the Quantum Nanophotonics Group at NIST. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Develop and test high-quality single and entangled photon sources. Characterize and measure entangled states distributed through a metropolitan fiber network. Carry out quantum position verification experimental protocols. Present results at internal meetings, and occasional meetings with external stakeholders. Ensuring that results, protocols, software, and documentation have been archived or otherwise transmitted to the larger organization. Have familiarity with fiber coupling, intensity and phase modulators, nonlinear optics (sum frequency generation or four-wave mixing), and aligning optical cavities. What You Should Know This position requires the successful completion of an NAIS agreement and federal security clearance as mandated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).  Employment is contingent upon approval of these requirements as determined by NIST. Anticipated start date is December 27, 2026. Anticipated end date is February 29, 2028. Privacy Act Statement Authority: 15 U.S.C. § 278g-1(e)(1) and (e)(3) and 15 U.S.C. § 272(b) and (c) Purpose: The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) hosts the Professional Research Experience Program (PREP) which is designed to provide valuable laboratory experience and financial assistance to undergraduates, post-bachelor’s degree holders, graduate students, master’s degree holders, postdocs, and faculty. PREP is a 5-year cooperative agreement between NIST laboratories and participating PREP Universities to establish a collaborative research relationship between NIST and U.S. institutions of higher education in the following disciplines including (but may not be limited to) biochemistry, biological sciences, chemistry, computer science, engineering, electronics, materials science, mathematics, nanoscale science, neutron science, physical science, physics, and statistics.  This collection of information is needed to facilitate administrative functions of the PREP Program. Routine Uses: NIST will use the information collected to perform the requisite reviews of the applications to determine eligibility, and to meet programmatic requirements.  Disclosure of this information is also subject to all the published routine uses as identified in the Privacy Act System of Records Notices: NIST-1: NIST Associates. Disclosure: Furnishing this information is voluntary. When you submit the form, you are indicating your voluntary consent for NIST to use of the information you submit for the purpose stated. What We Can Offer The salary range for the full-time Postdoctoral Associate position is $74,000 - $108,000 annually. The salary range for the full-time Research Associate position is $78,000 - $135,000 annually. Onboarding/relocation assistance may be available, however, if within a 12-month period you exercise your right to willfully terminate your employment with the University of Colorado, you hereby agree to reimburse the department for your relocation expense. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Postdoctoral Associate: A PhD in Electrical Engineering, Physics, or a related field with 0 – 4 years of post-doctoral degree experience. Research Associate: A PhD in Electrical Engineering, Physics, or a related field with 5+ years of post-doctoral degree experience. What We Would Like You to Have Two or more years of relevant experience in experimental nonlinear optics or quantum optics. Experience with building entangled photons using spontaneous parametric downconversion. Knowledge of bulk optics components as well as integrated photonic devices. Familiarity with Python, Matlab, or similar programming language. Strong oral and written communication skills, including a strong record of peer-reviewed publications. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor.  If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process, you will need to enter contact information for a reference.  We will request the letter of recommendation immediately following your application. For full consideration, please apply by October 2, 2026. Note:  Application materials will not be accepted via email.  For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Lisa Valencia Posting Contact Email: recruitprep@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Spacecraft-Electrical-Power-Subsystem-EPS-Lead-Engineer/74867": {
      "title": "Spacecraft Electrical Power Subsystem (EPS) Lead Engineer",
      "description": "Spacecraft Electrical Power Subsystem (EPS) Lead Engineer Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Spacecraft Electrical Power Subsystem (EPS) Lead Engineer Requisition Number: 74867 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 01-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 17-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary The Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics (LASP) is looking for a Spacecraft Electrical Power Subsystem (EPS) Lead Engineer, also commonly known as a Spacecraft Power Systems Engineer, to join the Spacecraft Team for the Emirates Mission to the Asteroid Belt (EMA), a deep space mission that will tour the inner solar system and explore the asteroid belt. Launch is expected in early 2028. The EPS design is mature and flight hardware is in integration and test. We are looking for an experienced engineer who can take ownership of an established power subsystem and carry it through spacecraft assembly, integration, and test (AIT/I&T), launch, and into mission operations. This mission builds on LASP’s growing experience with deep-space missions, including its successful first deep-space mission, the Emirates Mars Mission (EMM). CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are Founded a decade before NASA, the Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics (LASP) at the University of Colorado Boulder is revolutionizing human understanding of the cosmos. LASP is deeply committed to inspiring and educating the next generation of space explorers. From enabling the first exploratory rocket measurements of Earth’s upper atmosphere to trailblazing observations of every planet in the solar system, LASP is at the forefront of solar, planetary and space physics research, space-weather monitoring, and the search for evidence of habitable worlds. LASP advances scientific discovery and inspires the next generation through research, innovation, and education in space sciences. We employ over 700 scientists, engineers, support personnel, and students that work closely to cultivate creative ideas and explore novel solutions to make the impossible possible. LASP is dedicated to building and maintaining a unique synergism of expertise in space science, engineering, and spacecraft operations. Through research projects, LASP participates actively in the training of the future leaders of space research and helps the University of Colorado to educate students with valued technical and scientific skills. Development and use of innovative technologies and continuing participation in new research initiatives ensures a strong leadership role for LASP into the future. LASP is proud of our work environment that supports employee creativity and productivity and offers opportunities for a broad range of experiences. Further information about LASP can be found here: https://lasp.colorado.edu/home/ . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be The EPS Lead is the responsible engineer for the spacecraft power subsystem and the electrical aspects of the solar-electric propulsion (SEP) system. This includes the li-ion battery, power conditioning and distribution electronics (PCDU/PCDE), high-voltage electric propulsion (EP) power electronics, and the solar array and solar array drive electrical interfaces. The EPS Lead directs a small team of power subsystem engineers and students, owns EPS verification and test execution during the AIT campaign, leads subsystem troubleshooting and anomaly resolution, and is responsible for delivering the power analysis products, operations tools, and documentation needed to fly the mission. Required Competencies/Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: The applicant must be a hands-on engineer with strong electrical power system fundamentals and demonstrated experience taking spacecraft hardware through integration, test, and launch. The role requires sound engineering judgment, disciplined troubleshooting, clear communication with subsystem leads, systems engineering, mission operations, and international partners, and the ability to lead and mentor a small team. Job Duties: Lead the electrical power subsystem team through spacecraft integration and test, launch, and early mission operations Serve as responsible engineer for the battery, power conditioning and distribution electronics, high-voltage electric propulsion power electronics, and solar array electrical interfaces Plan and execute subsystem functional and performance tests, and support system-level test campaigns, ground support equipment (GSE/EGSE) usage, and program level reviews Lead EPS troubleshooting and anomaly investigation and resolution during integration, test, and operations Finalize EPS command and telemetry definitions and develop EPS telemetry displays for integration, test, and operations Perform and oversee final pre-launch power analyses and high-fidelity power system simulations Develop and maintain operations power analysis tools and power models used for mission planning Author and maintain EPS user’s manuals, operator’s guides, flight rules, and other operations products Support mission simulations, launch operations, and cruise-phase operations as the EPS subject matter expert Maintain EPS requirements verification status, technical budgets, and subsystem documentation Mentor junior engineers and students, including team members from an international partner Communicate subsystem status, risks, and performance to spacecraft leadership and at program reviews Other Duties Assigned What You Should Know What You Should Know This position supports the Emirates Mission to the Asteroid Belt (EMA), a deep-space mission with launch expected in early 2028. The spacecraft is currently in the assembly, integration, and test phase, so this role will join the project at a critical point in mission development. The position involves collaboration with LASP teams as well as external vendors and international mission partners. Some travel and non-standard work hours may be required during major testing activities, mission simulations, launch, and early mission operations. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer Annual Salary: $145,754.06 - $229,394.91 Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require BS in Electrical Engineering, Aerospace Engineering, or a related field and 9+ years of relevant spacecraft experience. Experience with spacecraft electrical power systems, including batteries, solar arrays, and power electronics Spacecraft assembly, integration, and test experience, including functional test execution and hardware troubleshooting Experience leading engineering tasks or small teams Strong written and verbal communication, including technical documentation and presentations at design and program reviews Team player, willing to contribute where needed to get the job done, yet also self-motivated with minimal supervision on tasks Willingness to travel and work with international partners Due to US Export Control restrictions, only US Citizens and Permanent Residents may apply What You Will Need What You Will Need Strong engineering judgment and the ability to make sound technical decisions in a fast-paced spacecraft development environment. A hands-on, solutions-oriented approach to troubleshooting and resolving complex technical issues. Strong communication and collaboration skills, including the ability to work effectively across engineering disciplines and with international partners. Ability to organize and prioritize multiple technical activities while maintaining attention to detail and documentation. Ability to lead and mentor others while also working independently with minimal supervision What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Mission operations experience, including console operations, flight rule development, mission simulations, or flight anomaly response Experience with lithium-ion battery systems and photovoltaic power generation Experience with high-voltage power electronics or solar-electric propulsion systems Experience with spacecraft command and telemetry systems, ground support equipment, and test-like-you-fly test programs Experience developing power system models and analysis tools in MATLAB and/or Python Experience working with hardware vendors and contracted development partners Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV. Cover Letter. (Optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree: If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor. However, if your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit an English-translated version as an Optional document. Please apply by 10/1/2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Professional-Research-Assistant/74681": {
      "title": "Professional Research Assistant",
      "description": "Professional Research Assistant Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Professional Research Assistant Requisition Number: 74681 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 15-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 17-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The position involves joint responsibility for the daily coordination of a National Cancer Institute-funded randomized trial of an online behavioral intervention to reduce depression symptoms and support medication adherence among adults with breast cancer who are taking endocrine therapy. The project is conducted in collaboration with community cancer care sites throughout Colorado as well as the University of Colorado Cancer Center. We are looking for a highly motivated, detail-oriented, and committed research assistant with outstanding organizational and interpersonal skills. Strong writing and basic statistical analytic or basic computer programming skills are preferred (REDCap, SPSS, and/or Qualtrics), as is experience working with medical populations. This position will strongly emphasize phone-based participant recruitment as a primary duty. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Dr. Arch’s research focuses on anxiety disorders and mindfulness-based behavioral interventions particularly in the context of cancer. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Recruit, screen, enroll and complete participant activities for one or more research studies Use Electronic Medical Records (EMR) software (both EPIC and McKesson) to pre-screen patients at collaborating community cancer care centers Communicate study information to collaborating medical staff regarding recruiting patients Provide technical support for any issues with study devices, surveys or online programs with study participants Collaborate in developing, modifying as needed, and implementing Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for research protocols. Work closely with other team members to ensure consistent compliance. Attend all necessary research and supervisory meetings; report updates on recruitment status, enrollment numbers and completion statistics. Assist in creating and maintaining project collection databases as well as enter all study-related data. Assist in the organization of research data for presentations, papers, and grant applications including assisting in manuscript preparation Submit amendments, continuing reviews and reportable events to the IRB and other oversight bodies as needed. Research management duties include ordering laboratory supplies, tracking of orders and expenses, scheduling team meetings and maintenance of laboratory equipment and computers Oversee compliance with university and departmental policies regarding student engagement in research studies (e.g., mentoring frequency, appropriate roles/responsibilities, confidentiality) Oversee student/volunteer research assistants, maintaining a cohesive team and positive atmosphere Perform other duties and special projects as assigned What You Should Know Candidates should have experience working with anxiety, depression, medication adherence, or psycho-oncology research and populations. What We Can Offer Salary range is $40,000 - $45,000 Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require Candidates should have a B.A. in Psychology or a related field and be willing to commit to a one-year appointment. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV. Cover Letter. (Optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree: If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor. However, if your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit an English-translated version as an Optional document. Please apply by October 15 , 2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Research-Instrumentation-Team-and-Safety-Manager/74574": {
      "title": "Research Instrumentation Team and Safety Manager",
      "description": "Research Instrumentation Team and Safety Manager Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Research Instrumentation Team and Safety Manager Requisition Number: 74574 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: University Staff Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 04-Oct-2026 Date Posted: 17-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary JILA seeks an experienced and collaborative Research Instrumentation Team and Safety Manager to oversee and support its research production shops, which include the Electronics Shop, Instrument Shop, and the Keck Metrology Lab and Clean Room. This position plays a critical role in supporting world-class experimental research by ensuring these shops operate efficiently, safely, and in alignment with JILA’s scientific mission. As JILA’s safety manager, this role guides program development, monitors safety practices, and supports implementation of safety initiatives. The position will provide leadership, coordination, and strategic oversight for the technical staff who support JILA’s instrumentation, machining, electronics fabrication, and research infrastructure needs. A key strategic role will be to connect the shops to the broader Boulder scientific communities, provide novel insight on scientific device design and application, and to enhance efficiency and make improvements that maintain our leading edge. The position further serves as JILA’s Safety Manager, providing leadership and resources for the institute’s safety program, and guiding the development and implementation of JILA’s Safety Plan. The role will include chairing the Safety Committee (a committee of mainly JILA fellows that oversee the JILA culture of safety) and aiding compliance with university and federal safety requirements through collaboration with CU EH&S and NIST safety partners. In shared resource spaces of the Scientific Shops, the JILA Safety Manager will oversee all aspects of safety. In the context of individual PI laboratories, the JILA Safety Manager will assist and coordinate to provide knowledge and support for PIs and student safety proctors. The position will partner with the JILA finance team and shop Fellow advisors to ensure accountable and sustainable shops operations, including budget monitoring, equipment repair and acquisition cost options, and staffing coverage and costs. This is not a hands-on design or fabrication role; rather, it focuses on management, communication, prioritization, and support for highly skilled technical personnel and the researchers they support. The Instrument Shop offers JILA researchers and staff a variety of services, including precision machining, welding, as well as the design and fabrication of custom laboratory devices and equipment. JILA’s secret to making custom equipment is one-on-one communication between scientists and instrument makers. This communication strategy makes it possible for Instrument Shop staff to create just about anything scientists need for their research. The JILA Electronics Shop provides a full suite of electronics engineering design and fabrication expertise in support of JILA’s scientific mission. Their services are available to all JILA personnel, including fellows, students, and faculty. We are available for quick on-site consulting, repairs, troubleshooting, as well as world class instrumentation and circuit design. The Keck Lab and Clean Room Shop is an optical metrology laboratory with advanced capabilities for (1) precision measurement, (2) micro and nano fabrication in a Class 1000 clean room, and (3) atomic force and optical microscopy. Keck Lab staff offers tutorials on using the lab measuring instruments and software, including instruction on photolithography, ellipsometry, E-beam lithography, and spectrophotometry. Supervision: This position supervises the Instrument Shop (5 employees), the Electronics Shop (3 employees), and the Keck lab (1 employee), responsible directly and indirectly for 9 FTE total. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are JILA is a joint institute of the University of Colorado Boulder and the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) located on the beautiful CU Boulder main campus at the base of the Rocky Mountains. Led by the roughly 30 research Fellows, JILA supports eclectic and innovative research programs that foster creative collaborations. The wide-ranging interests of our scientists have made JILA one of the nation's leading physical sciences research institutes. JILA has a total annual budget of $50 million, with 297 members (including faculty, staff, students, and postdoctoral researchers). JILA offers a uniquely collaborative and mission-driven environment. The institute has a strong culture of intellectual curiosity, community, and inclusiveness, which provides a meaningful and engaging workplace. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Operational Management and Outreach Provide leadership, guidance, and coordination across the Electronics Shop, Instrument Shop, and Keck Lab/Clean Room; undertake performance evaluations; and facilitate professional development for shop staff. Be responsible for project management, work distribution, and prioritization for shop activities to balance workload and meet Principal Investigator (PI) and research needs. Maintain effective communication and alignment with Principal Investigators (PIs), acting as a buffer to protect shop staff from conflicting demands and unbalanced workloads. Represent shop operations in meetings with JILA Fellows and leadership, providing technical, fiscal, and operational updates as needed. JILA Safety Manager Serve as the JILA Safety Manager, helping observe compliance with all safety policies in partnership with CU Environmental Health & Safety (EH&S), PIs and lab safety proctors. Chair the JILA Safety Committee, and guide the development, implementation, and upkeep of the JILA Safety Plan. Coordinate functions, roles, and responsibilities for lab safety in conjunction with PIs and lab safety proctors. Work with the NIST Safety Embed to observe Federal NIST safety requirements and best practices. Administrative, Strategic and Institutional Collaboration Collaborate with Facilities and Administrative staff and CU Facilities Management, with input around shared infrastructure needs (e.g., utilities, shop infrastructure, maintenance issues). Work collaboratively with internal and external partners, guide and advise JILA Fellows in areas of expertise, with openness and goals of the institution in mind. Identify and implement process improvements to enhance shop efficiency, scheduling, and responsiveness. Assess equipment, practices, and maintenance needs for improvement and cost savings. Explore business development opportunities, networking within CU and with external research and industry partners, following up on potential collaborations or service leads. Support long-term planning and budgeting for shop equipment, maintenance, and capital improvements. Budget coordination and oversight with Fellow guidance and approval. Personnel actions, performance management, leave management, and professional development. Compliance, finance, and budgeting in coordination with JILA and CU offices. What You Should Know What You Should Know This is a fully on-site position. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer The annual hiring range for this position is $115,000-$142,000. Relocation is available for eligible candidates. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Bachelor’s degree in engineering, applied physics, or a related technical field and 4+ years of experience in technical operations, engineering management, or research support environments. An equivalent combination of education and experience may substitute. Experience supervising professional staff. Experience in project coordination, budgeting, and compliance in a research or academic environment. What You Will Need What You Will Need Strong people management, communication, and interpersonal skills; approachable and effective working across administrative and technical boundaries. Familiarity with research instrumentation, electronics, and machining and fabrication workflows, with enough technical understanding to engage with staff and PIs. Proven ability to manage multidisciplinary technical teams and balance complex project priorities. Exemplary interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Ability to establish and maintain positive and cooperative working relationships. Ability to organize and successfully manage multiple projects occurring at the same time and to keep detailed and accurate records. Ability to work effectively in a working environment with multiple interruptions. Willingness to mentor and aid research students, postdoctoral scholars, staff, and faculty. Familiar with various fiscal business applications for productivity, reporting, and data record keeping and integrity. Dedication to fostering a collaborative, inclusive, and respectful workplace. Internal motivation with the ability to work independently as well as in partnership with an interdisciplinary team. Ability to anticipate the needs of the broader team and develop/recommend policies and procedures. Knowledge of safety practices and regulatory compliance in technical shops or lab environments. Skilled using Microsoft business suite applications, including Outlook, Excel, and Word. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Advanced degree in engineering, applied physics, or a related technical field. Experience working in a university research environment, research institute, or national laboratory setting. Familiarity with vacuum systems, cleanroom operations, CAD/CAM processes, or electronics fabrication. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A current resume. A cover letter that specifically tells us how your background and experience align with the requirements, qualifications, and responsibilities of the position. We may request references at a later time. Please apply by 10 /4/26 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs . In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-Aerospace-Engineering-Sciences/74888": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Aerospace Engineering Sciences",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Aerospace Engineering Sciences Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenure-Track Faculty in Aerospace Engineering Sciences Requisition Number: 74888 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 16-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Ann and H.J. Smead Department of Aerospace Engineering Sciences (AES) at the University of Colorado Boulder invites applications for four tenure-track faculty positions with a targeted focus in the following topical areas: 1. Bioastronautics and Human Space Exploration Research focus may include areas of interest in: space habitats and relevant systems engineering, environmental control and life support systems, in situ resource utilization (ISRU), space biology and associated payload engineering, mission integration and operations, human health and performance in aerospace environments, in-space manufacturing particularly in the areas of biology, pharmaceuticals, materials and infrastructure, and assembly for exploration systems. 2. Aeronautics and Aviation Research focus may include areas of interest in: autonomous uncrewed aircraft systems (UAS), aircraft control and flight dynamics, flight operations, aircraft systems for atmospheric sensing, advanced air mobility, airspace operations and air traffic management systems, aircraft design and optimization, sustainable aviation and energy systems. 3. Aerospace Sensing, Navigation, and Information Systems Research focus may include areas of interest in: positioning, navigation, and timing (PNT) systems, including but not limited to satellite navigation, inertial, celestial, and vision-based navigation; integration of novel remote sensing payloads with spaceborne, airborne, and ground-based platforms; sensor fusion and estimation theory for autonomous and uncrewed aerospace systems; space situational awareness and orbit determination; distributed and networked sensing architectures (e.g., small satellite constellations, swarms, or UAS); onboard and ground-based information and signal processing, including machine learning and data fusion for navigation and sensing applications; resilient and secure navigation and communication systems. 4. Other Critical and Emerging Areas in Aerospace Engineering Sciences In addition, we encourage applications from candidates pursuing research in other critical areas of Aerospace Engineering Sciences. We are particularly interested in candidates whose work bridges or complements existing areas of departmental strength, helps establish new areas of strategic strength, or advances promising and emerging directions in Aerospace Engineering Sciences. Candidates in these areas will be considered equally alongside those in the targeted areas described above. These positions are anticipated to be hired at the Assistant Professor rank. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs. Earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally importantly, we are committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college has three core foci: (i) Research & Innovation, (ii) Education, and (iii) Inclusion. We believe that our college’s success requires faculty who actively work to create an inclusive culture within the college and are committed to the campus plan of creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science please visit our website . CU Boulder's Ann and H.J. Smead Department of Aerospace Engineering Sciences is nationally known for teaching and research that address both challenges and opportunities facing the aerospace profession. Students work extensively on hands-on experiments in the Practical Innovation Through Learning, Observation, and Teaching (PILOT) Laboratory and design projects alongside expert faculty in the department's world-class research centers. CU aerospace alumni are working at top companies and research labs, including the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Johnson Space Center, The Boeing Company, Lockheed Martin, SpaceX, Anduril, Draper Laboratory, and many others. We are home to over 55 tenure-track, research, and instructional faculty, over 600 graduate students, and over 1,200 undergraduates. Our vibrant community of engineers and scientists tackle challenges in aerospace technology and science. The Smead Aerospace Sciences Department has active research programs spanning the breadth of aerospace engineering sciences through the Research and Engineering Center for Uncrewed Systems (RECUV), the Colorado Center for Astrodynamics Research (CCAR), the Aerospace Mechanics Research Center (AMReC), BioServe Space Technologies (BioServe), the Integrated Remote and In-Situ Sensing (IRISS) initiative, and the new CEAS Robotics Program. Applicants are encouraged to visit the Smead Aerospace Engineering Sciences website to understand the educational mission of the department, the specific courses that are taught in the undergraduate and graduate curricula, and how they would specifically contribute to offering these courses. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Successful candidates will be expected to engage in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; develop an externally funded research program in their technical area; contribute professional leadership and service to their unit, the college, and their professional community; and cultivate and contribute to the inclusive excellence of our academic community. The typical distribution of work for this role is 40% teaching, 40% scholarly and creative work (research), and 20% leadership and service. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2027; however, a later start date may be negotiated. The University of Colorado Boulder has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their current and previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination, before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $110,000-125,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to some of these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development . Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be genuine. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate in aerospace, aeronautical, or astronautical engineering, or a closely related field, or have their degree conferred before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. What You Will Need Applicants should possess excellent communication and interpersonal skills along with the ability to teach and work in a highly collaborative environment. An understanding and appreciation for aerospace engineering fundamentals, as well as excellent management, communication, and organizational skills, along with a willingness to learn quickly, is required. Candidates should be able to develop an externally funded research program that can include collaborations with colleagues throughout the Smead Aerospace Engineering Sciences Department and across the College and Engineering and Applied Science. Candidates should be capable of teaching a wide range of undergraduate aerospace engineering courses in both lecture and laboratory formats. Candidates should also be capable of teaching core graduate courses as well as developing new graduate courses. What We Would Like You to Have Prior experience teaching, conducting research, and/or industry experience in aerospace engineering, aeronautics, astronautics, or closely related fields. Research topics should focus on one of the three topical areas listed above (Bioastronautics and Human Space Exploration; Aerospace Sensing, Navigation, and Information Systems; Aeronautics and Aviation) or in other critical areas within Aerospace Engineering Sciences. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual PDF files: Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals. Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society (2-3 pages). A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement in or with engineering, or serving on advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision (1-2 pages). During the application process, you will be asked to provide the names and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, however for full consideration please apply by November 1, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Senior-QA-Engineer/74869": {
      "title": "Senior QA Engineer",
      "description": "Senior QA Engineer Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Senior QA Engineer Requisition Number: 74869 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 30-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 16-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary The QA Engineer (QAE) provides planning, leadership, and supervisory quality assurance expertise to the Safety & Mission Assurance (S&MA) team and flight hardware development projects. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are Founded a decade before NASA, the Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics (LASP) at the University of Colorado Boulder is revolutionizing human understanding of the cosmos. LASP is deeply committed to inspiring and educating the next generation of space explorers. From enabling the first exploratory rocket measurements of Earth’s upper atmosphere to trailblazing observations of every planet in the solar system, LASP is at the forefront of solar, planetary and space physics research, space-weather monitoring, and the search for evidence of habitable worlds. LASP advances scientific discovery and inspires the next generation through research, innovation, and education in space sciences. We employ over 700 scientists, engineers, support personnel, and students that work closely to cultivate creative ideas and explore novel solutions to make the impossible possible. LASP is dedicated to building and maintaining a unique synergism of expertise in space science, engineering, and spacecraft operations. Through research projects, LASP participates actively in the training of the future leaders of space research and helps the University of Colorado to educate students with valued technical and scientific skills. Development and use of innovative technologies and continuing participation in new research initiatives ensures a strong leadership role for LASP into the future. LASP is proud of our work environment that supports employee creativity and productivity and offers opportunities for a broad range of experiences. Further information about LASP can be found here: https://lasp.colorado.edu/home/ . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Maintains NASA and IPC certifications for workmanship inspections and performs inspections when needed. Generates high level quality planning documents for programs and the LASP Quality Management System (QMS). Contributes to writing of proposals for possible new LASP programs. Audits, monitors, evaluates, and reports on vendor and subcontractor performance and activities. Evaluates prospective suppliers’ quality systems for compliance with requirements. Guides, oversees, and makes recommendations to anomaly review boards throughout the project anomaly resolution process. Works with program managers in generation of budgets and schedules. Advises project teams and lab personnel on complex QA matters. Presents S&MA status and other data at project-level reviews. Resolves issues of non-compliance with quality standards and procedures. Plays an active role in maintaining and improving the QMS and its processes. Trains personnel on LASP Engineering processes, quality system updates and other topics as needed. May supervise, lead and direct multiple S&MA personnel on flight programs. Represents LASP as prime QA contact on programs and contracts, interacting with senior external personnel on significant technical matters. Performs other related duties as assigned. What You Should Know What You Should Know Work is 100% on-site. Due to requirements to access export-controlled data and information, only U.S. citizens, lawful permanent residents (green card holders), or other protected individuals (i.e., persons designated as an asylee, refugee, or temporary resident under amnesty provisions) may apply. Occasional travel to vendors, subcontractors and affiliate facilities may be required. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer Annual Salary: $120,416.44 - $156,628.10. Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require Bachelor’s degree in engineering or related field Ten years of QA experience in an aerospace environment What You Will Need What You Will Need Ability to develop technical solutions to complex problems requiring ingenuity and creativity. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Exceptional attention to detail. Experience with QA systems such as ISO 9001, AS9100, conducting audits, and corrective actions. A demonstrated ability to understand aerospace specifications and standards and the requirements contained therein that guide the design, build, and test of hardware. A demonstrated ability to understand quality engineering principles, tools, methods, and standards. Willingness to take initiative, good time management skills, and ability to complete tasks on schedule. Ability to maintain professional working relationships with fellow employees, vendors, and prime contractor personnel. Ability to demonstrate diplomacy when negotiating agreements involving differing opinions. Ability to work effectively with minimal supervision. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite or similar software for maintaining accurate records. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Quality Assurance experience supporting the design, fabrication, integration, testing, and delivery of: Spacecraft Scientific instruments Flight electronics Ground support equipment. Experience with NASA flight projects from concept through launch. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV. Cover Letter. (Optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree: If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor. However, if your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit an English-translated version as an Optional document. Please apply by 9/30/2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Postdoctoral-Associate-or-Research-Associate/74699": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate Requisition Number: 74699 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 29-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 15-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary NIST is actively engaged in applied research that will improve performance and scalability of quantum information devices, thereby bridging the gap between fundamental research and commercialization.  To achieve such transformative improvements, NIST is leveraging expertise in device fabrication, material characterization, and measurements to identify and mitigate sources of decoherence in qubits.  This position will focus on materials characterization, but it will require close collaboration with fabrication and measurement teams.  Multiple qubit architectures, including semiconducting quantum dots and superconducting transmons, are under study at NIST.  These qubit devices will be characterized by use of scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM). X-ray diffraction, scanning probe microscopy, and atom probe tomography may also be used. It is critical that conclusions be drawn from a statistically significant number of device measurements, so optimized, high-throughput workflows will be necessary.  The microscopist in this position will work closely with the device team to quantitatively explore the materials-design space and identify high-performance regimes. Results will be communicated to stakeholders, as well as presented and/or published as appropriate. This position is part of the National Institute of Standards and Technology's (NIST) Professional Research Experience Program (PREP).  NIST recognizes that its research staff may wish to collaborate with researchers at academic institutions on specific projects of mutual interest, thus requires that such institutions must be the recipient of a PREP award.  The PREP program requires staff from a wide range of backgrounds to work on scientific research in many areas.  Employees in this position will perform technical work that underpins the scientific research of the collaboration. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are PREP is a special partnership between the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) and the University of Colorado Boulder.  PREP provides research opportunities to CU undergraduate and graduate students, as well as researchers with a Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD in NIST labs to gain research experience alongside NIST scientists. The particular group that needs a researcher is the Quantitative Nanostructure Characterization Group at NIST. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Research and develop scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) and related microanalysis tools for metrology of semiconducting and superconducting quantum devices. Research and develop focused ion beam (FIB) preparation of semiconducting and superconducting quantum device lamella for STEM. Establish optimized, in-house workflows, including automation where appropriate, for FIB sample prep, microscopy data collection, and data analysis. Engage directly with device fabricators to optimize devices for improved performance through measurement-driven design feedback. Communicate and collaborate with team members. Present and publish results as appropriate. What You Should Know This position requires the successful completion of an NAIS agreement and federal security clearance, as mandated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).  Employment is contingent upon approval of these requirements, as determined by NIST. Anticipated start date is December 27, 2026. Anticipated end date is December 27, 2027. Privacy Act Statement Authority: 15 U.S.C. § 278g-1(e)(1) and (e)(3) and 15 U.S.C. § 272(b) and (c) Purpose: The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) hosts the Professional Research Experience Program (PREP) which is designed to provide valuable laboratory experience and financial assistance to undergraduates, post-bachelor’s degree holders, graduate students, master’s degree holders, postdocs, and faculty. PREP is a 5-year cooperative agreement between NIST laboratories and participating PREP Universities to establish a collaborative research relationship between NIST and U.S. institutions of higher education in the following disciplines including (but may not be limited to) biochemistry, biological sciences, chemistry, computer science, engineering, electronics, materials science, mathematics, nanoscale science, neutron science, physical science, physics, and statistics.  This collection of information is needed to facilitate administrative functions of the PREP Program. Routine Uses: NIST will use the information collected to perform the requisite reviews of the applications to determine eligibility, and to meet programmatic requirements.  Disclosure of this information is also subject to all the published routine uses as identified in the Privacy Act System of Records Notices:  NIST-1: NIST Associates. Disclosure: Furnishing this information is voluntary.  When you submit the form, you are indicating your voluntary consent for NIST to use of the information you submit for the purpose stated. What We Can Offer The salary range for the full-time Postdoctoral Associate position is $74,000 - $108,000 annually. The salary range for the full-time Research Associate position is $78,000 - $135,000. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Postdoctoral Associate: A PhD in Materials Science and Engineering, or a related field with 0 – 4 years of post-doctoral degree experience. Research Associate: A PhD in Materials Science and Engineering, or a related field with 5+ years of post-doctoral degree experience. What We Would Like You to Have Three (3) or more years of experience with advanced scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) techniques . Experience with STEM-based spectroscopies (EDS and EELS). Experience in STEM data analysis. Experience with Python, py4DSTEM, or similar programming language. Experience operating a focused ion beam (FIB) and scanning electron microscope (SEM). Experience with X-Ray Diffractometry (XRD) and/or Atom Probe Tomography (APT) and/or scanning probe microscopy preferred but not required. Strong oral and written communication skills. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. (optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor.  If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process, you will need to enter contact information for a reference.  We will request the letter of recommendation immediately following your application. For full consideration, please apply by September 29, 2026. Note:  Application materials will not be accepted via email.  For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Lisa Valencia Posting Contact Email: recruitprep@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-Chemical-and-Biological-Engineering/74834": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Chemical and Biological Engineering",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Chemical and Biological Engineering Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenure-Track Faculty in Chemical and Biological Engineering Requisition Number: 74834 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 14-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering at the University of Colorado Boulder is currently seeking applications for a tenure-track faculty position. The department and college are particularly interested in qualified candidates who can contribute through their research, teaching, and service to the diversity and excellence of our academic community. This position will be hired at the Assistant Professor rank. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs, earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally importantly, we are committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college has three core foci: (i) Research & Innovation, (ii) Education, and (iii) Inclusion. We believe that our college’s success requires faculty who actively work to create an inclusive culture within the college and are committed to the campus plan of creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science please visit our website . Ranked 11th among public universities for its graduate program and 11th for its undergraduate program by US News, the Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering is a world-class department with 38 faculty members, approximately 42 postdoctoral and other research associates, 137 graduate students, and 401 undergraduate students. The department has a proud tradition of academic excellence. We have two robust ABET-accredited undergraduate degree programs, where the emphasis is on interactive learning, computer applications, a wide variety of interdisciplinary options, and undergraduate research. We have created thousands of screencasts and interactive simulations – available on LearnChemE.com – that are accessed millions of times annually. To learn more about the Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering, please visit our website . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Successful candidates will be expected to engage in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; develop an externally funded research program in their technical area that synergizes with others in the University of Colorado system (the search is open with respect to area); contribute professional leadership and service to the department, the college, and their professional community; and cultivate and contribute to the inclusive excellence of our academic community. The typical distribution of work for this role is 40% teaching, 40% scholarly and creative work (research), and 20% leadership and service. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2027; however, a later start date may be negotiated. Candidates whose expertise cuts across engineering and related disciplines are especially encouraged to apply. The University of Colorado Boulder has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their current and previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination, before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $120,000-$130,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development . Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be authentic. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate or equivalent degree in chemical engineering, biological engineering, bioengineering, or a related field, and have their degree conferred before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual PDF files: Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals. Please indicate in the cover letter if you plan to attend the fall national meetings for the American Institute of Chemical Engineers (AIChE), Biomedical Engineering Society (BMES), or Materials Research Society (MRS) and provide the details of the sessions and presentations scheduled (if any). Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society. Applicants should describe their plans to develop a recognized research program based on scholarly work in their particular field, their ideas for interdisciplinary collaboration, and their plans for inspiring diverse students to conduct research. A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body. Applicants should describe their ideas for interdisciplinary collaboration, their experience and interests in teaching undergraduates and graduates, and their plans for inspiring diverse students (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement in or with engineering, or serving on advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision (1-2 pages). During the application process, you will be asked to provide the names and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, however for full consideration please apply by November 15, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-Computer-Architecture-and-Systems/74811": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Computer Architecture and Systems",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Computer Architecture and Systems Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenure-Track Faculty in Computer Architecture and Systems Requisition Number: 74811 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 14-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Department of Computer Science at the University of Colorado (CU) Boulder seeks applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the broad areas of Computer Architecture and Systems. The specific focus of this position is interdisciplinary, spanning the broad areas of computer architecture, networking, operating systems, distributed systems, database systems, and mobile systems. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs. Earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally importantly, we are committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college has three core foci: (i) Research & Innovation, (ii) Education, and (iii) Inclusion. We believe that our college’s success requires faculty who actively work to create an inclusive culture within the college and are committed to the campus plan of creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science please visit our website . The Department of Computer Science is one of the largest departments within the College of Engineering and Applied Science. The department is home to over 50 tenured and tenure-track faculty and 20 instructional faculty, and includes about 2000 undergraduate and 500 graduate students. The Computer Systems Lab consists of over ten faculty members whose expertise lies in building practical computer systems. As members of one of the premier research universities in the country, Computer Science faculty, staff, and students are engaged in cutting-edge research projects that address some of the most important challenges facing society today. The Department's research and education efforts interact broadly with many interdisciplinary programs and collaborators in Boulder and the Denver Metropolitan area, including the national labs at National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) , National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA), National Laboratory of the Rockies (NRL) , and National Center for Atmospheric Research ( NCAR ) , and CU Boulder research centers and institutes, including the Center for National Security Initiatives , the ATLAS Institute , the BioFrontiers Institute , and the Institute for Cognitive Science . More than 20 faculty members have joint affiliations with one of these labs. The Department also has a long-standing partnership with National Center for Women and IT and has faculty active in increasing the broadening of participation in computer science via their research, teaching, and service. In addition, the Department has extensive ties with the thriving local tech community and inhabits a picturesque location in the foothills of the Rocky Mountains. The Department fosters and supports entrepreneurial activities – CU Boulder is ranked No. 1 for launching startups based on university discoveries. Our department is committed to creating a community in which every individual matters and all can thrive. We embrace inclusive excellence as a guiding principle, actively fostering a culture of belonging, equity, and respect. Through intentional practices and shared responsibility, we celebrate the diversity of our students, faculty, and staff, and work collaboratively to ensure that all members of our community have the opportunity to succeed and contribute fully. For more about the Department of Computer Science, please visit our website . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Successful candidates will be expected to engage in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; develop an externally funded research program in their technical area; contribute professional leadership and service to their unit, the college, and their professional community; and cultivate and contribute to the inclusive excellence of our academic community. The typical distribution of work for this role is 40% teaching, 40% scholarly and creative work (research), and 20% leadership and service. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2027; however, a later start date may be negotiated. The University of Colorado Boulder has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their current and previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination, before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $130,000 - $145,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. A recruitment incentive is available within Department guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to some of these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development . Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be engaged. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate in Computer Science or a related discipline, or have their degree conferred before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. What You Will Need Preferred candidates are those that demonstrate the potential for excellence in research and teaching through a strong publication track record and a history of working with undergraduate and graduate students of varied backgrounds. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual PDF files: Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals. Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society (3-4 pages). A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement in or with engineering, or serving on advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision (1-2 pages). Names and contact information for three or more references: Contact information for at least three references who may be contacted as the search progresses. Please include names, job titles, phone numbers, and email addresses. Separately during the application process, you will be asked to provide the name and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. This is allowed to be the same individuals. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, however for full consideration please apply by November 15, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-Architectural-Engineering/74738": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Architectural Engineering",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Architectural Engineering Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenure-Track Faculty in Architectural Engineering Requisition Number: 74738 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 14-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Department of Civil, Environmental and Architectural Engineering at the University of Colorado (CU) Boulder seeks applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor within the field of Architectural Engineering. This search is particularly interested in scholars whose work advances the design, construction, and operation of terrestrial built environments and extra-planetary settlements. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, innovative materials and construction systems; mechanical, electrical, and lighting systems; sensing and monitoring technologies; digital twin modeling approaches; and automated optimized controls. Applications are welcomed from candidates whose work spans diverse domains, including high-performance and resilient buildings, livable spaces, data centers, artificial intelligence infrastructure, and other emerging built-environment applications. All candidates with interdisciplinary engineering fields related to designing, constructing, operating, and deconstructing built environments will be considered. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs. Earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally importantly, we are committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college has three core foci: (i) Research & Innovation, (ii) Education, and (iii) Inclusion. We believe that our college’s success requires faculty who actively work to create an inclusive culture within the college and are committed to the campus plan of creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science please visit our website . The Department of Civil, Environmental and Architectural Engineering (CEAE) at CU Boulder educates students to become leaders in the professional practice of engineering, contributing to technological advances that benefit humankind while enhancing the earth’s physical and biological resources. The fields of civil, environmental and architectural engineering offer careers in planning, design, construction and management of the built environment as well as in the interaction between the built and natural environment. The Architectural Engineering program at the University of Colorado Boulder is a premier education and research interdisciplinary program in building systems engineering. In particular, the program covers a wide range of building technologies including construction materials, structural, mechanical, electrical, and lighting systems as well as advanced controls with a focus on integrated and innovative solutions for designing, constructing, and operating sustainable buildings and urban communities. To learn more about the CEAE Department including its Architectural Engineering program, please visit the department’s website . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Successful candidates will be expected to engage in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; develop an externally funded research program in their technical area; contribute professional leadership and service to their unit, the college, and their professional community; and cultivate and contribute to the inclusive excellence of our academic community. The typical distribution of work for this role is 40% teaching, 40% scholarly and creative work (research), and 20% leadership and service. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2027; however, a later start date may be negotiated. The University of Colorado Boulder has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their current and previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination, before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $115,000 - $128,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to some of these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development . Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be authentic. Be visionary. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate in any relevant engineering field, or have their degree conferred before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual PDF files. Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals. Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society (2-3 pages). A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement in or with engineering, or serving on advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision (1-2 pages). During the application process, you will be asked to provide the names and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, however for full consideration please apply by November 1, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Professional-Research-Assistant/74700": {
      "title": "Professional Research Assistant",
      "description": "Professional Research Assistant Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Professional Research Assistant Requisition Number: 74700 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 30-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 14-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The work will entail the development of advanced radio-frequency (RF) detection systems using stabilized laser-based optical technologies and the digitization of RF signals.  The research will focus on improving efficiency of RF detection, sensitivity, measurement speed, and reliability through the integration of optical measurement techniques, high-speed signal acquisition, and digital signal processing. This position is part of the National Institute of Standards and Technology's (NIST) Professional Research Experience Program (PREP).  NIST recognizes that its research staff may want to collaborate with researchers at academic institutions on specific projects of mutual interest and, therefore, requires those institutions to be recipients of a PREP award.  The PREP program involves staff from a wide range of backgrounds conducting scientific research across various fields.  Individuals in this position will perform technical work supporting the collaboration's scientific research. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are PREP is a special partnership between the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) and the University of Colorado Boulder.  PREP provides research opportunities to CU undergraduate and graduate students, as well as researchers with a Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD in NIST labs to gain research experience alongside NIST scientists. The particular group that needs a researcher is the RF Technology Division - HQ at NIST. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Design analog RF measurement and digitization systems. Develop traceable, high-accuracy RF measurement methods. Integrate photonic and optical technologies with RF systems. Characterize RF propagation and interaction with media. Develop automated experimental tools for RF measurement and analysis. What You Should Know This position requires the successful completion of an NAIS agreement and federal security clearance as mandated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).  Employment is contingent upon approval of these requirements as determined by NIST. Anticipated start date is December 28, 2026. Anticipated end date is one year, but no later than March 31, 2028. Privacy Act Statement Authority: 15 U.S.C. § 278g-1(e)(1) and (e)(3) and 15 U.S.C. § 272(b) and (c) Purpose: The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) hosts the Professional Research Experience Program (PREP) which is designed to provide valuable laboratory experience and financial assistance to undergraduates, post-bachelor’s degree holders, graduate students, master’s degree holders, postdocs, and faculty. PREP is a 5-year cooperative agreement between NIST laboratories and participating PREP Universities to establish a collaborative research relationship between NIST and U.S. institutions of higher education in the following disciplines including (but may not be limited to) biochemistry, biological sciences, chemistry, computer science, engineering, electronics, materials science, mathematics, nanoscale science, neutron science, physical science, physics, and statistics.  This collection of information is needed to facilitate administrative functions of the PREP Program. Routine Uses: NIST will use the information collected to perform the requisite reviews of the applications to determine eligibility, and to meet programmatic requirements.  Disclosure of this information is also subject to all the published routine uses as identified in the Privacy Act System of Records Notices:  NIST-1: NIST Associates. Disclosure: Furnishing this information is voluntary.  When you submit the form, you are indicating your voluntary consent for NIST to use of the information you submit for the purpose stated. What We Can Offer The salary range for this full-time position is $54,000 - $78,000 annually. Onboarding/relocation assistance may be available, however, if within a 12-month period you exercise your right to willfully terminate your employment with the University of Colorado, you hereby agree to reimburse the department for your relocation expense. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Master’s degree in Electrical Engineering, Physics, Optical Engineering, Photonics, or a related field. What We Would Like You to Have Experience in radio-frequency measurement, instrumentation, or signal analysis. Experience developing experimental RF or optical measurement systems. Ability to develop prototypes and automated tools for measurement, digitization, and data analysis. Familiarity with photonics, laser-based measurement, and electro-optic techniques. Familiarity with RF calibration, measurement traceability, and uncertainty analysis. Strong oral and written communication skills. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. (optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor.  If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process, you will need to enter contact information for a reference.  We will request the letter of recommendation immediately following your application. For full consideration, please apply by September 30, 2026. Note:  Application materials will not be accepted via email.  For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Lisa Valencia Posting Contact Email: recruitprep@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Postdoctoral-Associate-or-Research-Associate/74698": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Postdoctoral Associate or Research Associate Requisition Number: 74698 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 28-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 14-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary This position involves quantum information science research.  The main task is to develop theory and methods in applied quantum information science. This may include theory and analysis of quantum characterization protocols, developing such protocols, investigating the behavior of differently randomized protocols and circuits, and specification of test problems for comparing performances of quantum and classical computing systems. This position is part of the National Institute of Standards and Technology's (NIST) Professional Research Experience Program  (PREP).  NIST recognizes that its research staff may wish to collaborate with researchers at academic institutions on specific projects of mutual interest, thus requires that such institutions must be the recipient of a PREP award.  The PREP program requires staff from a wide range of backgrounds to work on scientific research in many areas.  Employees in this position will perform technical work that underpins the scientific research of the collaboration. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are PREP is a special partnership between the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) and the University of Colorado Boulder.  PREP provides research opportunities to CU undergraduate and graduate students, as well as researchers with a Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD in NIST labs to gain research experience alongside NIST scientists. The particular group that needs a researcher is the Quantum Information Group at NIST. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Engage with researchers in quantum information theory and experiment. Presenting results at internal meetings, and occasional meetings with external stakeholders. Ensuring that results, protocols, software, and documentation have been archived or otherwise transmitted to the larger organization. What You Should Know This position requires the successful completion of an NAIS agreement and federal security clearance as mandated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).  Employment is contingent upon approval of these requirements as determined by NIST. Anticipated start date is December 26, 2026.  A nticipated end date is March 31, 2028. Privacy Act Statement Authority: 15 U.S.C. § 278g-1(e)(1) and (e)(3) and 15 U.S.C. § 272(b) and (c) Purpose: The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) hosts the Professional Research Experience Program (PREP) which is designed to provide valuable laboratory experience and financial assistance to undergraduates, post-bachelor’s degree holders, graduate students, master’s degree holders, postdocs, and faculty. PREP is a 5-year cooperative agreement between NIST laboratories and participating PREP Universities to establish a collaborative research relationship between NIST and U.S. institutions of higher education in the following disciplines including (but may not be limited to) biochemistry, biological sciences, chemistry, computer science, engineering, electronics, materials science, mathematics, nanoscale science, neutron science, physical science, physics, and statistics.  This collection of information is needed to facilitate administrative functions of the PREP Program. Routine Uses: NIST will use the information collected to perform the requisite reviews of the applications to determine eligibility, and to meet programmatic requirements.  Disclosure of this information is also subject to all the published routine uses as identified in the Privacy Act System of Records Notices:  NIST-1: NIST Associates. Disclosure: Furnishing this information is voluntary.  When you submit the form, you are indicating your voluntary consent for NIST to use of the information you submit for the purpose stated. What We Can Offer The salary range for the full-time Postdoctoral Associate position is $74,000 - $108,000 annually. The salary range for the full-time Research Associate position is $78,000 - $135,000 annually. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Postdoctoral Associate :  PhD in Physics or a related field with 0 – 4 years of post-doctoral degree experience. Research Associate: PhD in Physics or a related field with 5+ years of post-doctoral degree experience. What We Would Like You to Have Experience with theoretical quantum information science research. Familiarity quantum information theory and its applications. Familiarity with quantum physics simulation. Strong oral and written communication skills. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV Cover letter addressed to the Search Committee briefly describing your qualifications, professional goals, and specific interest in this position. (optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor.  If your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit a translated version as an optional attachment. During the application process, you will need to enter contact information for a reference.  We will request the letter of recommendation immediately following your application. For consideration, please apply by September 28, 2026. Note:  Application materials will not be accepted via email.  For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Lisa Valencia Posting Contact Email: recruitprep@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
