    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Senior-Principal-Electrical-Engineer-Spacecraft-and-Instruments/74227": {
      "title": "Senior/Principal Electrical Engineer",
      "description": "Senior/Principal Electrical Engineer - Spacecraft and Instruments Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Menu Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Senior/Principal Electrical Engineer - Spacecraft and Instruments Requisition Number: 74227 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Research Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Posting Close Date: 30-Sep-2026 Date Posted: 14-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary Job Summary Design, analyze, build, and test the electronics behind space science missions. At the Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics (LASP), electrical engineers develop custom flight and ground electronics for scientific instruments and spacecraft exploring Earth, the Sun, the Moon, planets, and the wider universe. We are seeking a senior electrical hardware engineer who enjoys hands-on circuit design and can carry high-reliability electronics from requirements and schematic capture through PCB implementation, board bring-up, environmental qualification, and system integration. This position requires you to be a motivated, largely self-directed individual contributor with capabilities to also lead a small electrical design team. You will work within a multidisciplinary mission team, make and document consequential hardware decisions, and provide technical direction to keep the electrical design effort moving forward. You will often be the responsible engineer for a circuit-card assembly or instrument electrical subsystem, but people management is not the focus of the position. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are Who We Are LASP combines the agility of a university laboratory with the technical capability of a premier space mission organization. Engineers work directly alongside scientists, students, and mission operators, allowing technical decisions to move quickly while maintaining scientific excellence. As part of the University of Colorado Boulder (CU), LASP began in 1948 and is a world-recognized space science research institute that implements the full life cycle of science missions from the definition of the science questions through the development of space flight hardware and the subsequent mission operations. We are the only research institute in the world to have sent science instruments to all eight planets, Pluto, and beyond the solar system. LASP combines all aspects of space exploration through our expertise in science, engineering, mission operations, and scientific data analysis. As part of CU, LASP also works to educate and train the next generation of space scientists, engineers and mission operators by integrating undergraduate and graduate students into working teams. Our students take their unique experiences with them into government or industry or remain in academia to continue the cycle of exploration. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Focused design in at least one area of discipline including analog, mixed-signal, power, motor-control, embedded, and high-speed digital electronics for spaceflight instruments and small spacecraft. You should have deep experience in at least one of these areas. Develop electrical requirements, interfaces, architecture, grounding, power and performance budgets, fault-management concepts, and verification plans with systems, FPGA, software, mechanical, thermal, science, manufacturing, quality, parts, and radiation engineers. Capture schematics in Altium Designer, guide and review PCB layout, and release circuit-card assembly (PCBA) design data for fabrication and assembly. Select EEE parts; complete derating, worst-case, reliability, and circuit-performance analyses; and model designs using LTspice, PSpice, Ansys, or comparable simulation tools. Bring up and debug new hardware in the laboratory, support EMI and EMC, thermal-vacuum, vibration, and other environmental tests, investigate anomalies, and integrate electronics with instruments or spacecraft. Prepare and present design decisions, technical risks, verification results, and readiness evidence at internal design reviews and mission milestone reviews. Provide technical leadership and mentoring within small project teams. Other Duties Assigned The engineer who will thrive here You can move between schematic design, PCB review, circuit analysis, laboratory troubleshooting, supplier conversations, and project decisions without losing the technical thread. You communicate assumptions and residual risk plainly, work well across disciplines, and build the documentation and configuration discipline required for reproducible, reviewable flight hardware. Your experience allows you to work day-to-day with minimal direction and you are excited to mentor early career engineers and student engineering assistants. What You Should Know What You Should Know Because this position requires access to export-controlled information, applicants must be U.S. citizens, lawful permanent residents, or other protected individuals as defined by applicable U.S. export-control laws. The work requires regular onsite presence in Boulder for flight hardware, laboratory work, and integration. Casual remote work 1 or 2 days per week is possible with project approval. What We Can Offer What We Can Offer $120,416.44 - $189,585.27/per year. CU Boulder offers a leading 401(a) retirement savings package matched at 200% with your mandatory 5% salary contribution. That's 15% of your pay invested for retirement every month, with 100% immediate vesting through TIAA. CU employees are eligible for medical, dental, and life insurances, plus 3 weeks paid vacation annually and paid sick leave. Additional benefits information can be found at https://www.colorado.edu/jobs/benefits . Benefits Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be Statements Be ambitious. Be groundbreaking. Be Boulder. What We Require What We Require A bachelor's degree in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, Physics, or a closely related field with at least 8 years of relevant experience; a master's with at least 6 years; or a doctorate with at least 4 years. Demonstrated performance with technical ownership of a complex electronic assembly or subsystem carried from requirements and architecture through production, qualification, and integration. Deep capability in at least one relevant design domain, such as analog or sensor electronics, power conversion, motor control, embedded systems, FPGA-enabled hardware, or high-speed digital design, with working breadth across adjacent disciplines. Schematic capture and PCB design experience using Altium Designer, or transferable experience with Cadence OrCAD or KiCad. Experience modeling and simulating circuit performance using LTspice, Ansys, MATLAB, Python, or similar tools Hands-on experience in the lab for board bring-up, electrical test, and troubleshooting with standard laboratory equipment such as oscilloscopes, power supplies, spectrum analyzers. Experience working in multidisciplinary teams and providing technical leadership for a major engineering work package, design review, or hardware-development effort. Strong candidates build expertise in different ways. If this work excites you and you meet many of these core qualifications, please apply even if your background does not include every core or preferred qualification. What You Will Need What You Will Need You are comfortable making progress when requirements are incomplete and several risks interact. You can move between architecture, schematic and PCB review, circuit analysis, laboratory troubleshooting, supplier conversations, and program decisions without losing the technical thread. You communicate assumptions and residual risk plainly, mentor other engineers, and build the documentation and configuration discipline needed for reproducible, reviewable flight hardware. You are motivated by LASP’s mission of exploration and furthering humanity’s understanding of the cosmos. What We Would Like You to Have What We Would Like You to Have Experience with spaceflight, avionics, or other high-reliability electronics, including radiation effects and mitigation, EEE parts, and design for thermal, vacuum, vibration, or shock environments. Working knowledge of electronics manufacturing standards, including IPC-6012 and IPC/WHMA-A-620 and experience selecting EEE parts and applying derating guidance such as NASA EEE-INST-002. Experience using advanced design simulation and analysis software such as Siemens HyperLynx for signal integrity, power integrity, or high-speed PCB analysis. Knowledge of high-speed digital interfaces and hardware design using AMD Xilinx and Microchip FPGAs and DDR3 or DDR4 memory. Experience presenting to and interfacing with project customers through requirements definition, project milestone reviews, design reviews, and anomaly investigations. Experience with EMI and EMC design, test, troubleshooting, and corrective-action closure. Experience with design for manufacture and test, rigid-flex PCB or harness design, supplier coordination, production support, or automated electrical test. Experience performing worst-case circuit analysis, electrical parts stress analysis, failure modes and effects analysis, or reliability analysis for high-consequence hardware. Special Instructions Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume or CV. Cover Letter. (Optional) Transcripts/Proof of Degree: If you are selected as the finalist, your degree will be verified by the CU Boulder Campus Human Resources Department using an approved online vendor. However, if your degree was obtained outside of the United States, please submit an English-translated version as an Optional document. Please apply by 9/30/2026 for consideration. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Open-Rank-Assistant-Associate-Professor-English-Education-Secondary-Literacy/74789": {
      "title": "Open Rank Assistant, Associate, Professor: English Education/ Secondary Literacy",
      "description": "Open Rank Assistant, Associate, Professor: English Education/ Secondary Literacy Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Open Rank Assistant, Associate, Professor: English Education/ Secondary Literacy Requisition Number: 74789 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 11-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The School of Education invites applications for an open-rank faculty position in English Education and Secondary Literacy. This position responds to a critical need in the field as schools navigate evolving standards, emerging digital literacies, and persistent disparities in literacy learning and achievement. Hiring at an open rank will allow the School to recruit either an emerging or established scholar whose expertise will strengthen our teacher preparation programs, graduate offerings, and historically strong Literacy Studies program. The successful candidate will contribute to scholarship that advances knowledge of English and literacy toward equitable, inclusive, and culturally responsive literacy practices. They will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in English education, secondary literacy, and related areas of teacher learning and practice and will play a key role in preparing future educators to support diverse learners, including multilingual students. This position will also support continued program growth, partnerships with schools and communities, and sustainable teaching and mentoring capacity across undergraduate and graduate programs. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder’s School of Education is a collaborative, research-driven community dedicated to transforming education through scholarship, innovation, and partnership. We prepare educators, researchers, and leaders to address complex challenges in learning and educational systems through rigorous research, engaged teaching, and meaningful collaboration with schools, communities, and organizations. Our faculty generate impactful scholarship that informs practice and policy, and our work is grounded in a deep commitment to equity, inclusive excellence, and expanding opportunity for learners across diverse contexts. We are driven by our shared commitments to diversity, democracy, equity, and justice, and evidence-based policy and practice. For more information about the School of Education, please visit: https://www.colorado.edu/education/. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Maintaining an active research agenda and corresponding publication record that demonstrates a compelling progression of scholarship. Engaging in strong teaching and mentoring and contributing to the School’s teacher education, master’s, and doctoral programs; advising undergraduate students; mentoring graduate students; and providing outreach and leadership to schools, districts, campus partners, professional organizations, and broader communities. Teaching undergraduate courses in English education and secondary literacy, as well as undergraduate and graduate courses in literacy studies, teacher learning and practice, and related areas of teacher education. Supporting the development of equitable, inclusive, culturally responsive, and evidence-based English education and literacy curricula that prepare educators to support diverse learners. Building and sustaining meaningful partnerships with local schools, districts, educators, and communities. Contributing service to the program, School, university, profession, and broader community, consistent with expectations for rank. What You Should Know This role is located in Boulder, CO. The workload for this role is 40% research, scholarly, and creative work, 40% teaching, advising & mentoring, and 20% service. Tenure track faculty are expected to teach four courses per academic year, and all courses are typically taught in person on campus. What We Can Offer The salary range for this nine-month faculty role is $87,500 - $97,500 for Assistant Professor, $106,000 - $128,500 for Associate Professor, $131,500 - $165,000 for Professor, plus an $8,000 relocation allowance as applicable. Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be creative. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require Doctorate (Ph.D. or Ed.D.) in English education, literacy education, curriculum and instruction, teacher education, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. A record of scholarly productivity—or demonstrated promise of such—in English education, secondary literacy, or a closely related field. Expertise in one or more areas of secondary literacy or English Education, for instance: adolescent literacies; digital and multimodal literacies; English and secondary literacy teacher learning; classroom literacy teaching and learning; culturally responsive and sustaining literacy practices; young adult literature; youth identities and literacies; and/or social and cultural contexts of literacy education. PK–12 teaching experience in U.S. schools, preferably in secondary English language arts or literacy. Evidence of effective teaching at the university level, at either the undergraduate or graduate level. A record of working successfully with people from a wide range of backgrounds and fostering a strong sense of community and belonging. Candidates seeking appointment at the associate or full professor level must demonstrate a record of teaching, scholarship, and service appropriate for appointment with tenure at the proposed rank. What We Would Like You to Have A research agenda focused on educational equity, justice, and access to high-quality literacy learning opportunities. Three or more years of PK–12 teaching experience in U.S. schools, preferably at the secondary level. Experience working with culturally, linguistically, socioeconomically diverse, and/or LGBTQ+ student populations in K-12 public schools in the U.S. Experience advancing inclusive, culturally responsive literacy practices. Experience developing partnerships with schools, districts, educators, or community-based organizations. Experience supporting teacher licensure programs. Experience mentoring graduate students as emerging researchers and teacher educators. Evidence of, or potential for, contributing to the growth and intellectual community of a graduate program in Literacy Studies. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: A 3-page cover letter addressing how you meet the required and preferred qualifications with an emphasis on your scholarly/research agenda CV 1–2-page teaching statement Additional materials may be requested of candidates who advance to the second phase of the search (sample syllabi and letters of reference) During the application process you will need to enter contact information for three references who will be contacted to provide letters of recommendation as the search progresses. Review of applications will begin October 19, 2026 (full consideration will be given to applicants who submit by this deadline) and will continue until the position is filled. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, please apply through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-Quantum-Computation/74741": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Quantum Computation",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Quantum Computation Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenure-Track Faculty in Quantum Computation Requisition Number: 74741 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 11-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Department of Computer Science at the University of Colorado (CU) Boulder seeks applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Quantum Computation. We invite candidates from all areas of quantum computing to apply, but the priority consideration will be given to the following areas: Quantum computation theory: quantum algorithms, quantum complexity theory, quantum information theory, and quantum error correcting codes Applications of quantum computation to important or emerging areas such as quantum optimization and quantum machine learning CU Boulder sits at the center of one of the world’s most vibrant quantum information science and engineering ecosystems. The Boulder area is home to Elevate Quantum, a U.S. Department of Commerce–designated Tech Hub that unites more than 70 partners across academia, national laboratories, startups, and established companies. The area also hosts a thriving cluster of quantum companies - including Infleqtion, Quantinuum, Atom Computing, and IonQ - making it one of the nation’s leading quantum industry centers. CU Boulder has launched a quantum incubator that has secured over $20 million in investment capital to accelerate quantum innovation. CU Boulder also leads major federally funded quantum research centers and initiatives, including NSF Quantum Leap Challenge Institute for Quantum Systems through Entangled Science and Engineering (NSF Q-SEnSE). For more information, please visit the CUbit Quantum Initiative’s website . CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs. Earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally importantly, we are committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college has three core foci: (i) Research & Innovation, (ii) Education, and (iii) Inclusion. We believe that our college’s success requires faculty who actively work to create an inclusive culture within the college and are committed to the campus plan of creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science please visit our website . The Department of Computer Science is one of the largest department within the College of Engineering and Applied Science at the University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder). The department is home to over 50 tenured and tenure-track faculty plus 25 instructional faculty and now includes about 2000 undergraduate and 500 graduate students. To learn more about the Department of Computer Science, please visit the department’s website . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Successful candidates will be expected to engage in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; develop an externally funded research program in their technical area; contribute professional leadership and service to their unit, the college, and their professional community; and cultivate and contribute to the inclusive excellence of our academic community. The typical distribution of work for this role is 40% teaching, 40% scholarly and creative work (research), and 20% leadership and service. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2027; however, a later start date may be negotiated. The University of Colorado Boulder has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their current and previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination, before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $130,000 - $145,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. A recruitment incentive is available within Department guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to some of these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development . Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be engaged. Be impactful. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate in Computer Science or a related discipline before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. What We Would Like You to Have Preferred candidates are those that demonstrate the potential for excellence in research and teaching through a strong publication track record and a history of working with undergraduate and graduate students of varied backgrounds. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual PDF files: Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals. Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society (3-4 pages). A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement in or with engineering, or serving on advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision (1-2 pages). Names and contact information for three or more references: Contact information for at least three references who may be contacted as the search progresses. Please include names, job titles, phone numbers, and email addresses. Separately during the application process, you will be asked to provide the name and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. This is allowed to be the same individuals. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, however for full consideration please apply by November 15, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-Artificial-Intelligence/74736": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Artificial Intelligence Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenure-Track Faculty in Artificial Intelligence Requisition Number: 74736 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 10-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Department of Computer Science at the University of Colorado (CU) Boulder seeks applications for two tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in artificial intelligence, broadly construed. We invite applications from all areas of AI. Areas of current interest include the following: Foundations of machine learning Multimodal models Alignment and AI safety Physical and embodied AI Reasoning Systems that make large models trainable and deployable This list is illustrative rather than exhaustive. Applicants whose work falls outside these areas are encouraged to apply, including applicants whose work spans artificial intelligence and another discipline. Artificial intelligence is a growth area for the campus and for the department. The Department of Computer Science offers a Bachelor of Science in Artificial Intelligence, and these two positions are part of a broader cluster of AI hiring. The department has recently made strategic investments in two AI Centers. Computing has been expanded alongside that: a Research Computing reinvestment has added NVIDIA H200 capacity, supported in part by the college and the department, with a share of it dedicated to teaching. AI research at CU Boulder extends beyond computer science into partners across the College of Engineering and Applied Science, cognitive science, information science, and collaborations with the federal laboratories along the Colorado Front Range. The Boulder area also supports a growing concentration of companies working in artificial intelligence and robotics. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs. Earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally importantly, we are committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college has three core foci: (i) Research & Innovation, (ii) Education, and (iii) Inclusion. We believe that our college’s success requires faculty who actively work to create an inclusive culture within the college and are committed to the campus plan of creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science please visit our website . The Department of Computer Science is one of the largest departments within the College of Engineering and Applied Science at the University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder). The department is home to over 50 tenured and tenure-track faculty plus 25 instructional faculty and now includes about 2000 undergraduate and 500 graduate students. To learn more about the Department of Computer Science, please visit the department’s website . What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Successful candidates will be expected to engage in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; develop an externally funded research program in their technical area; contribute professional leadership and service to their unit, the college, and their professional community; and cultivate and contribute to the inclusive excellence of our academic community. The typical distribution of work for this role is 40% teaching, 40% scholarly and creative work (research), and 20% leadership and service. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for these positions is Fall 2027; however, a later start date may be negotiated. Only one application is necessary to be considered for both positions. The University of Colorado Boulder has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their current and previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination, before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $130,000 - $145,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. A recruitment incentive is available within Department guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to some of these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development . Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be inspired. Be game-changing. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate in Computer Science or a related discipline before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. What You Will Need A research agenda that can be pursued with external funding at CU Boulder. Ability to teach at both the undergraduate and graduate level within the artificial intelligence curriculum. Research interests that connect with existing work in the department or elsewhere on campus. What We Would Like You to Have A record of peer-reviewed publication in artificial intelligence or a related field, calibrated to career stage. Experience teaching or mentoring students at the undergraduate or graduate level. Experience collaborating across research groups, departments, or disciplines. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual PDF files: Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals. Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society (3-4 pages). A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement in or with engineering, or serving on advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision (1-2 pages). Names and contact information for three or more references: Contact information for at least three references who may be contacted as the search progresses. Please include names, job titles, phone numbers, and email addresses. Separately during the application process, you will be asked to provide the name and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. This is allowed to be the same individuals. Applications will be accepted until the positions are filled, however for full consideration please apply by November 1, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Tenured-Tenure-Track-Faculty-in-Engineering-Education/74730": {
      "title": "Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty in Engineering Education",
      "description": "Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty in Engineering Education Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty in Engineering Education Requisition Number: 74730 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 10-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Engineering Education Program within the College of Engineering and Applied Science at the University of Colorado Boulder invites applications for a tenure-track or tenured faculty position in Engineering Education. This is an open-rank search, and candidates will be considered for appointment at the Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor rank, commensurate with qualifications and experience. We seek a scholar whose research focuses on Engineering Education, Computer Science Education, or a closely related area. The successful candidate will contribute to the Engineering Education PhD program through doctoral student teaching, mentoring, research advising, and program development. The faculty member will be rostered in one of the departments in the College of Engineering and Applied Science, where their tenure home will be located. The faculty member is expected to contribute to the teaching, research, leadership, and service missions of both the Engineering Education Program and their tenure-home department. We are especially interested in candidates who will advance engineering education research, support the preparation of future engineering educators, contribute to inclusive and evidence-based approaches to engineering teaching and learning, and help strengthen the College’s interdisciplinary capacity in Engineering Education. We welcome applications across all areas of engineering research and practice. CU Boulder has a campus commitment to sustainability, the ethical and responsible use of AI and related technology, and the societal dimensions of engineering and computing. Applicants with engineering education scholarship that advances these areas may be invited to join an initiative to grow a network of faculty members that includes professional development and support. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado Boulder (CU Boulder) is one of Colorado’s flagship public institutions and among the most research-active public universities in the country. The College of Engineering and Applied Science is the second largest school or college on the CU Boulder campus and boasts top-tier rankings for both its graduate and undergraduate engineering programs. Earning the 11th spot among public graduate programs and 17th nationwide for undergraduate engineering. Multiple specialty areas are also ranked within the top 20. Equally importantly, we are committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive, and academically excellent community. Our approximately 250 tenure-track, 120 instructional-track, and 30 research-track faculty serve more than 6,000 undergraduate students, 1,300 master’s students, and 850 PhD students. Our college has three core foci: (i) Research & Innovation, (ii) Education, and (iii) Inclusion. We believe that our college’s success requires faculty who actively work to create an inclusive culture within the college and are committed to the campus plan of creating and sustaining supportive campus environments . To learn more about the College of Engineering and Applied Science please visit our website . The Engineering Education Program is an interdisciplinary program in the College of Engineering and Applied Science that offers a PhD degree in Engineering Education. The program brings together faculty from across the university to create a community of scholars and educators focused on student learning, engineering formation, inclusive and evidence-based teaching, program assessment, and the broader systems that shape engineering education. The PhD program prepares graduates to conduct research on significant problems in engineering education, apply research-based instructional strategies in engineering settings, and understand policy and systemic implications for student success. To learn more about the Engineering Education PhD program, please visit the Engineering Education PhD website . The Engineering Education Program is not a tenure-granting unit. Therefore, the successful candidate will have a tenure home in one of the departments in the College of Engineering and Applied Science, which may include Aerospace Engineering Sciences; Chemical and Biological Engineering; Civil, Environmental and Architectural Engineering; Electrical, Computer and Energy Engineering; or Mechanical Engineering. The specific tenure-home department will be determined based on the candidate’s background, research area, teaching interests, and alignment with departmental needs. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Develop and sustain a nationally recognized, externally funded research program in Engineering Education, Computer Science Education, or a closely related field. Teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, including advising Engineering Education PhD students and serving on doctoral committees. Contribute to graduate curriculum development, program-building, and interdisciplinary collaboration in Engineering Education. Teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses in the faculty member’s tenure-home department, the Engineering Education Program, and/or interdisciplinary programs in CEAS, depending on expertise and program needs. Contribute professional leadership and service to the Engineering Education Program, the tenure-home department, the college, the university, and relevant professional communities. Cultivate and contribute to the inclusive excellence of our academic community. The typical distribution of work for this role is 40% teaching, 40% scholarly and creative work, and 20% leadership and service. What You Should Know The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2027; however, a later start date may be negotiated. The successful candidate will be rostered in a CEAS department, which will serve as the candidate’s tenure home. The candidate will also be expected to contribute substantively to the Engineering Education Program. Candidates considered for appointment with tenure must meet University standards and the criteria for tenure and promotion in the proposed tenure-home department. We welcome applications from candidates whose scholarship uses a wide range of research traditions and methodologies, including qualitative, quantitative, mixed-methods, design-based, critical, theoretical, computational, and interdisciplinary approaches. The University of Colorado Boulder has implemented a reference check program with respect to final candidates for tenured faculty appointments. The reference check program is intended to allow CU Boulder to collect and review information about a candidate's conduct at their current and previous institutions, specifically conduct related to sexual misconduct, harassment, and/or discrimination, before making hiring decisions. All final candidates for tenured faculty appointments are required to complete an Authorization to Release Information. What We Can Offer The salary and rank will be determined based on applicable experience and tenure home. The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Assistant Professor rank is $110,000 - $145,000. The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Associate Professor rank is $122,000 - $170,000. The anticipated academic year salary range for this position at the Professor rank is $155,000 - $210,000. A relocation stipend may be available within CEAS guidelines. CU offers a competitive 401(a) retirement plan, with employees contributing 5% of their annual eligible compensation and CU contributing 10% of their eligible compensation. This plan is standard for most faculty hires. For more information on CU's retirement benefits visit our website here . In addition to a competitive salary, the University of Colorado Boulder offers a wide variety of other benefits and perks. Links to some of these are below. To forecast the value of your total compensation, check out our total compensation calculator . More information on some of our benefits and perks is available here: gender-neutral paid parental leave ; housing down-payment assistance programs ; dual-career & relocation resources ; and teaching , research , and professional development . Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be authentic. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Applicants must have earned a research doctorate in Engineering Education, Computer Science Education, Engineering, Computer Science, STEM Education, Education, Learning Sciences, Information Science, or a closely related field, or have their degree conferred before starting a tenure-track faculty appointment. Applicants must have a research agenda focused on Engineering Education, Computer Science Education, or a closely related area relevant to engineering or computing education. Candidates being considered for appointment at the Associate Professor or Professor rank must meet University standards and unit criteria for tenure and promotion to the designated rank. These criteria generally include having an established and ongoing program of original research, a strong publication record, a demonstrated ability to obtain external funding, and a record of high-quality teaching and mentoring. What You Will Need Ability to contribute to an interdisciplinary Engineering Education PhD program. Ability to teach and mentor students in Engineering Education, Computer Science Education, engineering, computing, or a closely related area. Ability to work collaboratively across disciplinary, departmental, and college boundaries. Ability to communicate the relevance and rigor of Engineering Education research to engineering, computing, education, and interdisciplinary audiences. Interest in contributing to the continued development of the Engineering Education PhD program and collaborating across CEAS and other campus units. Ability to pursue externally funded research. What We Would Like You to Have Evidence of scholarly contributions to Engineering Education, Computer Science Education, STEM Education, or a closely related field. Record of externally funded research. For candidates at the Associate Professor or Professor rank: Evidence of doctoral advising, externally funded research, and leadership in Engineering Education or a related field. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials as individual PDF files: Cover Letter: A one-page cover letter describing your interest in the position and summarizing your personal academic goals, as well as potential tenure-home departments. Resume/CV: A current curriculum vitae. A Statement of Research Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for research and/or creative work that contributes to the body of knowledge in your field and provides broader benefits to education and society (2-3 pages). A Statement of Teaching Philosophy: A statement of your experience and plans for teaching and mentoring a diverse student body (2-3 pages). Leadership, Service and Community Contributions Statement: In this statement, describe your leadership and service experience, and demonstrated ability to contribute to a supportive campus community. Please provide details on your track record, calibrated to your career stage, that describe the specific role you played in these efforts. These could include, but are not limited to service on committees, outreach activities to broaden engagement in or with engineering, or serving on advisory boards or task forces contributing to policies or regulations. In addition, describe your plans to contribute to our campus plan to Create Supportive Environments , and the College of Engineering and Applied Science Strategic Vision (1-2 pages). During the application process, you will be asked to provide the name and contact information for at least three references to submit a letter of recommendation to be considered along with your application materials. Reference letters may be requested for candidates who advance in the search process. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, however for full consideration please apply by October 7, 2026 . Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs . Background checks will be conducted for all final applicants. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
    },
    "https://jobs.colorado.edu/jobs/JobDetail/Open-Rank-Tenure-Track-Position-in-Accounting-Division/74696": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenure Track Position in Accounting Division",
      "description": "Open Rank Tenure Track Position in Accounting Division Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Jobs UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO BOULDER Skip to content Search jobs Living Here Benefits Perks Jobs Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Join the CU Boulder Talent Community Create Job Alert FAQ Login Home Job Detail Open Rank Tenure Track Position in Accounting Division Requisition Number: 74696 Location: Boulder, Colorado City Boulder State Colorado Employment Type: Faculty Schedule: Full-Time Date Posted: 10-Sep-2026 Close All Job Summary The Leeds School of Business at the University of Colorado Boulder invites applications for an Open Rank appointment (i.e., Assistant, Associate or Full Professor) in Accounting to start in Fall 2027. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Who We Are The University of Colorado at Boulder, and the city of Boulder, offers a rich intellectual, cultural, recreational, and family-friendly environment. Boulder is widely recognized as an entrepreneurial hub, with active venture capital, technology, natural foods, and renewable energy sectors; it is consistently recognized as one of the best places to live in the United States. The economy of the Front Range of Colorado is strong, and private support for the University and the Leeds School is increasing steadily, as evidenced by a broad range of new programs. The University of Colorado and the Leeds School of Business are nationally recognized for the excellence of our faculty, programs, and students. By joining the Accounting Division, you will be part of a collegial and productive group of scholars with the highest standards of research and teaching excellence. What Your Key Responsibilities Will Be Job duties include research, teaching, and service to the university and profession. The research interests of our faculty are mainly in archival empirical research in accounting. Compensation and support for research activities are competitive. What We Can Offer Based on credentials and experience, the salary ranges are as follows: Full Professor : $330K–$365K Associate Professor: $280K–$300K Assistant Professor: $258K–$270K Benefits At the University of Colorado Boulder , we are committed to supporting the holistic health and well-being of our employees. Our comprehensive benefits package includes medical, dental, and retirement plans; generous paid time off; tuition assistance for you and your dependents; and an ECO Pass for local transit. As one of Boulder County’s largest employers, CU Boulder offers an inspiring academic community and access to world-class outdoor recreation. In fact, CU Boulder has been named one of Forbes' America's Best Large Employers , ranking #4 among large employers in Colorado and #28 among universities nationwide. Explore additional perks and programs through the CU Advantage program. Be Statements Be ambitious. Be pioneering. Be Boulder. What We Require Assistant Professor candidates must hold a PhD or equivalent in accounting or a closely related field or be close to completion of a PhD. Successful candidates will demonstrate strong potential in conducting scholarly research, in teaching ability, and in effective interaction with faculty colleagues, students, staff, and external constituents. Seasoned faculty are also encouraged to apply. Note: Candidates who have not yet been conferred their degree will be appointed as an Instructor until the semester following the PhD degree conferral. Candidates who would like to be considered for an Associate or Full Professor position must hold a PhD or equivalent in accounting or related field. A successful candidate will demonstrate a strong record of scholarly research with potential for continued success, high quality teaching, and effective interaction with faculty colleagues, students, staff and external constituents. Special Instructions To apply, please submit the following materials: Resume/CV Curriculum Vitae/Resume Representative research paper(s) and/or publication(s): Please combine all materials into a single PDF before submitting. During the application process you will need to enter contact information for three academic references who will be contacted to provide letters of recommendation later in the recruitment process. Review of applications is ongoing; applications received by October 10, 2026 will receive full consideration. The position will remain posted until filled. Note: Application materials will not be accepted via email. For consideration, applications must be submitted through CU Boulder Jobs. In compliance with the Colorado Job Application Fairness Act, in any materials you submit, you may redact or remove age-identifying information such as age, date of birth, or dates of school attendance or graduation. You will not be penalized for redacting or removing this information. Posting Contact Information Posting Contact Name: Boulder Campus Human Resources Posting Contact Email: Recruiting@colorado.edu Apply Now Share Facebook X Email Help Jobs Submit a Request for Help Frequently Asked Questions For Technical Support: 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) The University of Colorado does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, age, pregnancy, disability, creed, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran status, marital status, political affiliation, or political philosophy. All qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Click here for a list of ADA and Title IX coordinators . To view the Regent policy , please click here. This organization participates in E-Verify . We will provide the Social Security Administration and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security, with information from each employee's I-9 form to confirm work authorization. Reasonable Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities CU Boulder is committed to making information and resources that are available via the web accessible for all users. If you are a job seeker and need accessibility assistance or an accommodation in order to apply for one of our open positions, please submit an accessibility request or call 303-735-4357 (5-HELP) to submit a request for assistance. Download the Clery Act Annual Security & Fire safety report , or request a paper copy from the CU Boulder Police Department. Administrative and Research Center, East Campus • 3100 Marine St.• Boulder, CO 80309-0565 University of Colorado Boulder © Regents of the University of Colorado Privacy • Legal & Trademarks • Campus Map"
